{"type":"tool_call_update","toolCallId":"call-77432cca-91cf-4093-86cf-a75a4a76254f-3","status":"completed","content":[{"type":"content","content":{"type":"text","text":"1890→    consecutive `#` lines is seen only when the comment text is joined. So the sweep also reads\n    each `.py` file's comment tokens (`tokenize`, with the `#` stripped), joined and\n    whitespace-collapsed.\n  - **A file that `ast.parse` rejects.** It is never skipped silently. The sweep reports it by name\n    with a count (`unparsed=N`), its line-grep and comment readings still apply, and a nonzero\n    count means the file is read by hand before the sweep counts as clean.\n  - **Bytes literals** (`b\"…\"`) are not `str` constants. The line grep covers them on one line.\n    A bytes literal split across lines is the residual of this axis.\n\n  **The sweep is therefore the union** of the line grep over every tracked file and the joined\n1900→  reading (the `.py` `str` constants, the joined `.py` comment text, and the whitespace-collapsed\n  text of every other file). One measurement at `5f72c2f3` (h-mad last changed at `083a8233`):\n  joined reading 7 files, line grep 6 files, line grep minus joined reading 0 files, union\n  7 files; comment-token hits 0 files, joined-comment hits 0 files, `unparsed=0`. The comment\n  zero is incidental: no comment in `h-mad/` restates the rule today. It is not a property of the\n  sweep, and it does not license dropping the comment reading.\n\n  Residual: a later surface that restates the rule is not covered until this sweep is re-run. A\n  phrase built at run time (an f-string field, `%`/`.format` interpolation, or a `+` join of names\n  rather than literals) is not a `str` constant, so no reading in the sweep sees it.\n1910→  Such a surface is found only by reading the code that builds the dispatch text. A bytes literal\n  split across lines is the other residual, as stated above.\n\n- **The relaxation is shown to be exactly the intended case (differential corpus).** The carve-out\n  makes the 5d RED check accept a RED it used to halt, so `h-mad/invariants.base.md` §\"Guard\n  narrowing\" applies. The rule has no code implementation. At `e7730f78`,\n  `git ls-files h-mad | grep -v '\\.md$' | xargs grep -ln 'red_wrong_reason\\|missing.symbol'`\n  hits only two test files (`test_h_mad_wire_pin_gate.py` and `test_h_mad_wiring_task_shape_docs.py`),\n  and no script. So the corpus runs over **RED evidence**, not over a function. Each case is\n  a fixture repository with a RED base, a WIRE-PIN test and the real pytest RED it produces. The\n1920→  four kinds of missing symbol are:\n\n  | case | missing symbol in the RED | callee at the RED base | old verdict | new verdict |\n  |---|---|---|---|---|\n  | `new-caller` | the caller the task creates | resolves | halt `red_wrong_reason` | **valid** |\n  | `missing-callee` | the callee | absent | halt | halt |\n  | `both-missing` | the caller the task creates (the callee is also absent) | absent | halt | halt |\n  | `unrelated-import` | a symbol the task neither creates nor calls (for example an unrelated module failing to import) | resolves | halt | halt |\n\n  - For each case the probe records the RED's exception line, whether the callee resolves at\n1930→    the RED base (shown by importing it at that commit, not by reading the source), and the\n    verdict under the old rule text and under the new one. The design names how a verdict is\n    derived from rule text: either a classifier function that both texts' prose describes, or a\n    stated derivation per case.\n  - **Pass:** the set of cases whose verdict changed is exactly `{new-caller}`. Any other member\n    is a widening, and it is fixed in the rule text, never in the table.\n  - The four rows are one per kind of missing symbol on the RED. The residual axis is the\n    exception type: the corpus produces each case's RED as the exception the fixture actually\n    raises, and a case whose RED is `NameError` is a fifth fixture only when the new rule's text\n    treats `NameError` differently from `ImportError`/`AttributeError`.\n1940→- **Incident replay against the real artifacts.** The carve-out was motivated by a real incident,\n  so `h-mad/invariants.base.md` §\"Incident replay\" applies, and the synthetic corpus above does not\n  replace the replay. The artifacts are in the HemaSuite checkout (`/Users/kimhawk/orca/HemaSuite`):\n  - `docs/archive/2026-10/notebooklm-integration-interface/notebooklm-integration-interface.analysis.md`,\n    item **F-3 — WIRE-PIN RED on a new caller (T4/T5/T6 WIRE 2)**. It names the cases and their\n    wire-cut evidence: M4.1, M5.1 and the WIRE 2 inline revert;\n  - the same directory's `notebooklm-integration-interface.impl-plan.md`, whose WIRE-PIN table\n    maps each case to its pin and mutation row;\n  - the three pins, each in `hematology-paper-writer/tests/` at its task commit:\n\n1950→    | case | pin (test file `::` test) | task commit (subject locator) |\n    |---|---|---|\n    | T4 WIRE-PIN 1 | `test_notebooklm_resolver.py::test_resolver_calls_registry_arm_notebook_id_once` | `531a6296b` (`git log --all --oneline --grep='Task 4 FR-1 resolver'`) |\n    | T5 WIRE-PIN 1 | `test_notebooklm_initialize.py::test_initialize_entry_point_resolves_through_registry_arm` | `7aa0d9dec` (`--grep='Task 5 FR-5 initialize'`) |\n    | T6 WIRE-PIN 2 | `test_notebooklm_status_report.py::test_status_entry_point_resolves_through_registry_arm` | `c2d671f6b` (`--grep='Task 6 CLI surface'`) |\n\n    Each task commit's parent is its RED base. The pin names come from the impl-plan's\n    `**WIRE-PIN N**:` lines, and each file was checked with `git show --stat <commit>`.\n\n  **Which form of the pin the replay evaluates.** The committed pins do not share one form:\n1960→  - T4 looks the new caller up with two-argument `getattr(integration, \"resolve_registry_notebook\")`.\n    At the RED base this raises `AttributeError`, so the committed pin's RED is a missing symbol.\n  - T5 and T6 use the impl-plan's \"WIRE-PIN RED scaffold\". It is a three-argument\n    `getattr(..., None)` (T5: `getattr(integration, \"initialize_project_notebook\", None)`; T6:\n    `getattr(integration.NotebookLMIntegration(), \"generate_setup_report\", None)`) inside\n    `except (Exception, SystemExit)`, followed by an assertion on the spy. Their committed RED is\n    an `AssertionError`, which the old rule already accepts.\n\n  So for T5 and T6 the replay evaluates the **direct-call form** of the pin: the scaffold is\n  replaced by a two-argument `getattr` on the same name (or a direct call), the `try`/`except` is\n1970→  removed, and the spy assertion is unchanged. That is the form whose RED is the missing caller,\n  and it is the form the carve-out is about. The replay also records each committed pin's own RED,\n  for the record. For T4 the committed pin is already in the direct-call form.\n\n  For each of the three cases, the replay:\n  1. reproduces the direct-call form's RED at the RED base, with the pin from the task commit\n     (rewritten as above for T5 and T6). The RED must be `AttributeError`/`ImportError` on the\n     caller, and the callee must resolve at the RED base (shown by import);\n  2. applies the new rule to that RED and records its verdict, which must be **valid**;\n  3. records the old rule's verdict on that RED, which must be halt. For T5 and T6 it also records\n1980→     the committed scaffolded pin's RED (`AssertionError`) and the old verdict on it, which is\n     **valid**: the scaffold is how those tasks avoided the halt;\n  4. re-executes the wire-cut at the task commit with the committed pin: delete the call only, keep\n     the caller and the callee, and confirm the pin fails. It also locates the original evidence\n     (M4.1, M5.1, the WIRE 2 revert) and cites it beside the re-run.\n\n  - The replay is a committed probe under `docs/03-analysis/probes/agy-coder-fallback/`, stamped\n    with this repository's sha and the HemaSuite sha it read.\n  - A case whose RED does not reproduce, or whose original evidence cannot be located, is recorded\n    with the command that failed. It is never dropped from the count.\n1990→  - **Residual.** The locators are commit subjects and archive paths. If HemaSuite rewrites\n    history or moves the archive, re-locate with `git log --all --grep='notebooklm-integration-interface'`.\n\n### FR-10 — Documentation surfaces\n\n- **SKILL.md** §\"Codex authors Phase 5 — enforced, not just instructed\" becomes implementer-neutral\n  and states FR-2/FR-3. §\"Exit-code dispatch for 5d/5e\" lists `exec agy` as an implementer\n  dispatch beside its review role.\n- **`h-mad/references/agy-runtime.md`** §\"The TDD gate\" names the new gate and FR-5's check, and keeps\n  the halt.\n2000→- **`h-mad/references/state-schema.md`** gains the FR-1 rows. Its existing `fallback_agent` and\n  `codex_status` rows are rewritten, and so are the schema JSON's `fallback_agent` and\n  `codex_status` `description`s, all four per FR-1.\n- **`h-mad/references/failure-recovery.md`** gets the 5d `red_wrong_reason` row carve-out per\n  FR-9.\n- **SKILL.md, the Phase-5 orchestrator steps:** the `implementation_authors` append on accepting\n  a 5e GREEN or fix dispatch, and the attribution read from it before each review leg, per FR-1\n  and FR-7 (D-15).\n- **SKILL.md, Phase 7 closure step:** the report carries every `WARNING` line that\n  `h_mad_phase7_preconditions.py` prints, the FR-7 warnings included.\n2010→- **SKILL.md §\"Teammate audit leg — when codex is unavailable\"**, the bullet that begins\n  `- **It does not license Claude to write Phase 5 production code`. Today it says \"but the\n  declaration you just made does\". Under FR-2 that is false when agy is available: the\n  declaration routes the audit leg, but it moves Phase-5 authorship to agy, and FR-3 refuses\n  Claude (`agy-authorship`). The rewrite keeps the anchor prefix, which\n  `test_h_mad_tdd_gate_docs.py` `_skill_gate_bullet` requires exactly once, and the tokens\n  `ACTIVE`, `every`, `unavailable` and `exhausted`, which\n  `test_skill_gate_bullet_states_the_every_record_rule` requires. It states two things separately:\n  - **Audit-leg selection.** `codex_status` alone routes the 5b audit to the teammate leg.\n  - **Resolved authorship.** Claude may write Phase-5 production code only when FR-2's `resolved`\n2020→    is `claude` for every ACTIVE `step5` record on the chain. That means both peers are\n    unavailable and `fallback_agent` is absent, `null` or `claude`.\n  The replaced condition is \"the `codex_status` declaration licenses Claude\". The new condition is\n  \"`resolved` = `claude`\", and the bullet names `h_mad_tdd_judge.py implementer` as the way to read\n  it.\n- **Audit legs in Phases 3/4/5b are unchanged.** `codex_status` still routes the second audit\n  surface to the teammate leg (SKILL.md §\"Teammate audit leg — when codex is unavailable\").\n  `agy_status` has no audit-routing consumer.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n2030→\n- **AC-1.1:** The schema accepts each FR-1 field at each listed value and rejects a value outside\n  it. A record with none of the fields validates unchanged. The `fallback_agent` description in\n  the schema JSON and its `state-schema.md` row state FR-2's both-peers condition. The\n  `codex_status` description and row no longer say that an unavailable Codex permits Claude to\n  author, and state authorship as FR-2's resolution. `test_h_mad_state_fallback_agent.py` stays\n  green. For `implementer_switch.ts`, \"the listed value\" is FR-1's narrowed profile, leap second\n  excluded (D-14); its fixtures are AC-1.2's.\n- **AC-1.2:** A record whose `implementer_switch.ts` is `not-a-date`, `2026-10-16`,\n  `2026-02-30T00:00:00Z` or the leap second `2016-12-31T23:59:60Z` (D-14) is refused on the library path and, under the test seam that forces it,\n2040→  on the bundled path. Each value is its own fixture, run once per path. A `.ts` in the writer's\n  form `%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ` is accepted on both paths. If the mechanism uses the library's format\n  checker, one fixture runs with `rfc3339_validator` made unimportable and still refuses\n  `not-a-date`.\n- **AC-1.3 (D-15):** `implementation_authors` is pinned on the library path and, under the same\n  test seam as AC-1.2, on the bundled path; each fixture runs once per path:\n  - accepted: `[]`; one entry per agent `codex`, `agy`, `grok`, `claude`, each its own fixture;\n    and `[{\"module\":\"m\",\"agent\":\"agy\"},{\"module\":\"m\",\"agent\":\"codex\"},{\"module\":\"m\",\"agent\":\"agy\"}]`\n    (duplicates and order kept);\n  - refused, each its own fixture: an unknown agent (`\"agent\":\"gemini\"`), a missing `agent`, a\n2050→    missing `module`, `\"module\":\"\"`, an extra key, a non-object entry, and a non-array value;\n  - a record with no `implementation_authors` validates unchanged.\n  An append of a refused entry through the Record mechanism leaves the store byte-identical.\n- **AC-2.1:** The resolution function: for every combination of {`implementer` absent / `codex` /\n  `agy` / invalid} × {`codex_avail`, `agy_avail` ∈ true/false}, `resolved` and `switch` match FR-2.\n  Each switch reason is produced by its own fixture, with only that cause present.\n- **AC-2.2:** The `implementer` verb prints exactly one line for each of the six token rows, and\n  for `record=failed`. `--record` writes `implementer_switch` once and does not rewrite it on an\n  identical re-run.\n- **AC-3.1:** Each of the five gate-specific refusal kinds is produced by its own fixture. In\n2060→  addition, `implementer-invalid` outranks `codex-authorship`, a peer refusal outranks\n  `fallback-invalid`, and the chain fold blocks on the FIRST eligible record.\n- **AC-3.2:** The `codex-authorship`, `fallback-grok` and `fallback-invalid` message bytes equal\n  those at `a8d9ba99`. Every existing `test_h_mad_tdd_gate_*.py` stays green, and the only change\n  allowed is FR-3's stub-line exception. In the two files FR-3 names, a string literal that stubs\n  a `TDD-STATE: active` line, and the constants it is composed from, are re-shaped to the new\n  line. Every assertion, expected kind, reason substring, test name and parametrisation id stays\n  byte-identical (checked by diffing the file against `a8d9ba99`). Every other\n  `test_h_mad_tdd_gate_*.py` is unmodified. A new fixture shows that a line in today's shape now\n  refuses `judge-error`. In `test_h_mad_tdd_judge.py`, `ACTIVE_RE` is the only changed literal, and\n2070→  the real-line assertions FR-3 lists stay byte-identical and green (checked by the same diff).\n- **AC-4.1:** With the scan reading `inactive`, the agy gate allows every tool, except that a\n  class-(b) production `.py` target whose chain reads `unreadable` still denies (the `chmod 000`\n  `docs/` chain fixture). With it `active`, it covers:\n  - each class with at least one member;\n  - an unknown tool name → class (d) deny;\n  - a class-(b) payload with no path key → deny, and the same with the key's value `\"\"` → deny;\n  - a production `.py` without a failing test → deny, with a judge kind;\n  - with a failing test → allow;\n  - `/tmp` and `artifactDirectoryPath` non-`.py` → allow, and `.py` there → deny;\n2080→  - a class-(b) target naming an existing directory (`.` and `src/`) → deny `kind=judge-error`,\n    and the same target with the scan reading `inactive` → allow (D-16).\n  With the scan reading `unknown` (a `docs/.bkit-memory.json` that is not JSON), each deny above\n  that does not need the judge still fires (class (d), no path key, `\"\"`, out-of-root `.py`, a\n  directory target, a `run_command` FR-6 refuses, and the `workspacePaths` cross-check), each from its own fixture.\n  The **target differential** runs one corpus of target shapes through both gates and shows they\n  agree on every shape except those FR-4's §\"Deliberate differences\" names, where the agy gate\n  refuses.\n- **AC-4.2:** Every exit path prints exactly one JSON object: malformed stdin, an internal\n  exception and an unreadable state each still yield parseable stdout. Every deny path the design\n2090→  enumerates is driven by its own fixture, and each fixture asserts that `reason` ends with\n  exactly one `kind=<kind>` whose value is in the design's closed list (FR-4 §\"Every deny carries\n  a kind\"); a deny path with no fixture, or a kind outside the list, fails the AC.\n- **AC-5.1:** The wiring check emits each `UNWIRED` reason from its own fixture. `--agent agy`\n  writes no prompt and halts on each of them.\n- **AC-5.2:** Each row of FR-5's exit-code table is pinned by its own fixture, and each fixture\n  asserts the stdout token and the exit code separately:\n  - an `UNWIRED` fixture (one per reason, reusing AC-5.1's) shows check exit 0, and assembler\n    `ASSEMBLE-TDD: HALT agy_tdd_hook_unverified` with exit 0;\n  - a `hooks.json` holding invalid JSON shows check `UNREADABLE` with exit 2, and the assembler\n2100→    HALT with exit 2;\n  - a check stubbed to print no token shows the assembler `HALT agy_tdd_hook_unverified check=no-token`\n    with exit 2;\n  - `WIRED` shows exit 0 on both;\n  - the agy gate's `--self-check` with a verdict `FAIL` forced prints that `FAIL` token on stdout\n    with exit 0, and the wiring check reads it as `UNWIRED reason=self-check-failed`.\n\n  A mutation that returns 2 on `UNWIRED` is killed, and so is one that returns 2 on a self-check\n  verdict `FAIL`.\n- **AC-6.1:** The shell policy is imported, not copied: one definition in the tree. The Codex gate's\n2110→  existing shell tests pass unmodified. Every mutation row re-pointed under FR-6 runs\n  `MUTATION: ALL_CAUGHT` against the shared module, `h_mad_target_identity.py` (D-12). The agy gate refuses each `unsafe` command in the Codex\n  gate's `--self-check` list.\n- **AC-7.1:** `h_mad_phase7_preconditions.py` emits each of the three FR-7 warnings from its own\n  fixture, and none of them moves `ready`. Adding a `review_independence` entry to a record that\n  already holds two leaves both earlier entries byte-identical. An entry that fails FR-1 is\n  refused, and the store is unchanged.\n- **AC-7.2:** `review_independence_unrecorded` fires, with no `leg=6a-prime` entry, for each\n  implementer `codex` (field absent included), `agy`, `grok` and `claude`, each from its own\n  fixture (`grok` and `claude` through `implementer_switch.to`, the only FR-1 routing field that takes them; `implementation_authors` is an authorship record and does not trigger the warning), and for each `archreview` value `READY_TO_MERGE`, `WITH_FIXES` and `NO`. It does not fire\n2120→  when a `leg=6a-prime` entry exists, nor when `archreview` is absent, `SKIPPED_NO_PANE` or\n  `SKIPPED_OPERATOR_OVERRIDE`.\n- **AC-7.3 (v1.8):** FR-7 §\"Attribution\" is pinned by fixtures, each RED against the mutant it\n  names and GREEN on the rule:\n  - **routing is not authorship:** `implementer_switch.to=agy` stored, the module's code from a\n    Codex dispatch, a Codex reviewer → `implementer=codex`, `independent=false`, and\n    `review_non_independent` fires. Mutant: attribution read from `implementer_switch.to`.\n  - **multi-author, recovered (E2):** authors agy then Codex; a grok 5e-review →\n    `implementer=codex`, `independent=true`; a Codex 5e-verify → `implementer=codex`,\n    `independent=false`; then, with the dispatch context discarded, an agy 6a-prime →\n2130→    `implementer=agy`, `independent=false`. Mutant: authors recovered from earlier entries'\n    `implementer` values only. The recovered set's source is `implementation_authors` (FR-1,\n    D-15), holding `{m, agy}` then `{m, codex}`.\n  - **pre-field feature (D-15):** the same reviews on a record with no `implementation_authors`\n    → the 6a-prime leg writes no entry for the module. Mutant: an absent field read as an empty\n    author set, so the agy reviewer reads as independent.\n  - **RED is not authorship (E4):** an agy 5d RED dispatch then a Codex 5e GREEN; an agy reviewer →\n    `independent=true`, `implementer=codex`. Mutant: a 5d RED dispatch counted as an author.\n  - **unaccepted dispatches author nothing:** a rejected agy GREEN then an accepted Codex GREEN;\n    an agy reviewer → `independent=true`.\n2140→  If the design leaves attribution as orchestrator prose with no code to drive, this AC has no\n  surface to run on; that is owed to the impl-plan as an OPEN-DECISION, not waived.\n- **AC-8.1:** The probe directory holds the payload capture, the real refusal, and the RED/GREEN\n  readings with re-run, revert and mutation results, each stamped with the sha and the\n  `agy --version`. The default timeout in `h_mad_assemble_tdd.py` equals FR-8's formula over\n  those readings. The readings come from FR-8's post-merge stage (D-13), and the feature is not\n  recorded COMPLETE before AC-8.1 and AC-8.2 pass.\n- **AC-8.2:** The probe directory holds the forced-handler-timeout reading from FR-8 step 2a,\n  stamped with the sha, `agy --version` and the T it used. The reading has three parts: whether\n  the file exists, the wall time and agy's message. It names exactly one outcome from step 2a's\n2150→  table, and that outcome is consistent with the three readings. It also shows that the delay\n  hook was removed and that `.agents/hooks.json` matches its pre-probe bytes. A `session-error`\n  outcome does not satisfy this AC. If the outcome is `timeout-allows`, `agy-runtime.md` §\"The TDD\n  gate\" carries the fail-open residual, and the operator question is raised before the gate is\n  called verified.\n- **AC-9.1:** The prompt contains no implementer-specific opening. `test_prompt_file_is_agent_independent`\n  covers `codex`, `grok` and `agy`. The new-caller rule appears in RED item 1, in the WIRE-PIN\n  paragraph, in the GREEN variant, in the SKILL.md 5d halt text, in the `failure-recovery.md`\n  `step5d:red_wrong_reason:<module>` row and in the wiring-task header that `h_mad_assemble_tdd.py`\n  emits. `test_h_mad_wiring_task_shape_docs.py`, `test_h_mad_agent_definitions.py` and\n2160→  `test_h_mad_assemble_tdd.py` stay green. FR-9's sweep, re-run on the shipped tree, hits no file\n  that states the rule without the carve-out. The sweep is the union FR-9 defines: the line grep\n  over every tracked file and the joined reading, which takes in `.py` comment text. The re-run\n  reports `unparsed=N`. A nonzero `N` does not satisfy this AC until each unparsed file has been\n  read by hand.\n- **AC-9.2:** The FR-9 differential corpus runs all four cases (`new-caller`, `missing-callee`,\n  `both-missing`, `unrelated-import`) as fixture repositories with real pytest REDs. For each case\n  it records the RED's exception line, whether the callee resolves at the RED base (by import) and\n  both verdicts. The set of cases whose verdict changed is exactly `{new-caller}`.\n- **AC-9.3:** The FR-9 incident replay covers HemaSuite `notebooklm-integration-interface` T4\n2170→  WIRE-PIN 1 (`test_notebooklm_resolver.py` at `531a6296b`), T5 WIRE-PIN 1\n  (`test_notebooklm_initialize.py` at `7aa0d9dec`) and T6 WIRE-PIN 2\n  (`test_notebooklm_status_report.py` at `c2d671f6b`). For each case the committed probe holds:\n  - the direct-call form's RED reproduced at the task commit's parent: `AttributeError`/\n    `ImportError` on the caller, with the callee resolving by import. For T4 this is the committed\n    pin; for T5 and T6 it is the pin with the scaffold replaced, per FR-9;\n  - on that RED, the new rule's verdict **valid** and the old rule's verdict halt;\n  - for T5 and T6 only, the committed scaffolded pin's RED (`AssertionError`) and the old rule's\n    verdict on it, which is valid;\n  - a wire-cut re-run at the task commit in which the committed pin fails;\n2180→  - a citation of the original evidence (M4.1, M5.1, WIRE 2 revert).\n\n  It is stamped with both repositories' shas. A case that cannot be reproduced is recorded with\n  its failing command and counted. It does not satisfy the AC.\n- **AC-9.4:** The six copies of the new-caller rule (FR-9 surfaces 1–5 and the GREEN variant) are\n  bound to one authority. Either every surface is generated from or checked against one shared\n  source, or one test asserts that the carve-out text is equal across all six copies. The plan\n  names which mechanism. Whichever it is:\n  - **The carve-out text is the whole quoted block in FR-9**, all three sentences: the validity\n    condition, \"The RED report must name both.\", and the wire-cut obligation. A check that binds\n2190→    only the first sentence does not meet this AC.\n  - **Each copy is read from its own source location.** The span read for one copy contains no\n    other copy. Three readings do not meet this AC, because a healthy copy in the same span\n    masks a sick one: a contains-check over the whole assembled prompt (the template's copies\n    1, 2 and 6 are filled into it beside the header copy); a file-level contains-check over\n    `codex-implementer-prompt.md`, which holds three copies; and a file-level check over any\n    file that holds more than one copy. The design names each copy's locator. A locator states\n    what it does not pin, and the test fails, rather than passing, when a locator matches\n    nothing or more than one span.\n  - **The control is a mutation of exactly one copy, and it makes the check fail.** It is run\n2200→    once per copy, so six times, and once per sentence of the block in at least one copy. Each\n    mutation row's `find` is unique in its file, because `h_mad_mutation_harness.py`\n    `anchor_status` refuses a `find` that does not occur exactly once. A `find` therefore carries\n    copy-specific context, and the `.py` copy's `find` is written against the source bytes,\n    including the adjacent-literal split. A row the harness refuses is not a control.\n- **AC-X:** Every new guard carries a mutation row that bites (`MUTATION: ALL_CAUGHT` with a `test`\n  per row). The full suite passes per task.\n\n## Decisions on the brainstorm's open questions\n\n2210→- **D-1 `run_command`:** command-shape rule shared with Codex (FR-6). Rationale: the tree shows\n  that is what Codex parity is.\n- **D-2 Hook scope:** per project (`.agents/hooks.json`). Rationale:\n  - The Codex gate is wired per project (HemaSuite `.codex/hooks.json`; this repository has no\n    `.codex/`).\n  - A tracked file reaches every worktree.\n  - The handler cwd gives the root without trusting a payload field.\n  - No global hook fires in every agy session on the machine.\n\n  Residual: a project without the file has no agy gate. FR-5 turns that into a halt, not a silent\n2220→  pass.\n- **D-3 Payload cwd:** the root comes from the handler cwd (FR-4), with `workspacePaths` as a\n  cross-check.\n- **D-4 Timeout:** provisional 1500 s, then FR-8's formula. The hook-handler timeout is a separate\n  figure, derived from `JUDGE_BUDGET_S`.\n- **D-5 Availability:** CLI on PATH + `agy_status` + `HMAD_AGY_UNAVAILABLE`, symmetric with Codex.\n  No liveness probe: the gate runs on every write and must be offline and fast, and a probe spends\n  quota. An exhausted agy shows up when the dispatch fails, and the orchestrator records it with\n  `--set agy_status=exhausted`.\n- **D-6 Project default:** feature record only. A second source doubles the surface the gate\n2230→  resolves, and per-feature is the operator decision. A later default can seed the same field at\n  `--create`.\n- **D-7 Flag location:** a state field (FR-7). It is not on the extracted verdict, because that\n  line is `<KEY>: <VALUE>` and its consumers parse it.\n- **D-8 Audit legs:** unchanged (FR-10).\n- **D-9 Codex reviewing Codex (was OQ-1; operator decision, 2026-10-16):** yes. FR-7's flag fires\n  for any `reviewer == implementer`, Codex included, for example the 5e anti-gaming verifier\n  dispatched to Codex on a Codex-implemented feature. It is a warning only, never a block.\n  Consequence: Codex-implemented features can now carry a `review_non_independent` Phase-7\n  warning that has never appeared before.\n2240→- **D-10 `.agents/` exclusion (was OQ-2; operator decision, 2026-10-16):** narrow it. Applied in this\n  clone by the orchestrator on the operator's answer: the `.git/info/exclude` entry `/.agents/` is now `/.agents/skills/`.\n  Evidence at `d65374e9`: `git check-ignore -v .agents/hooks.json` prints nothing (exit 1, not\n  ignored), and `git check-ignore -v .agents/skills` still reports the `/.agents/skills/` entry.\n  D-2's tracked `.agents/hooks.json` therefore needs no `git add -f`.\n  - **Residual / prerequisite.** `.git/info/exclude` is per-clone local configuration and is not\n    versioned. Every other clone of this repository, and every other project that adopts the agy\n    gate, needs the same narrowing (or a `git add -f`) before its `.agents/hooks.json` can be\n    tracked. Linked worktrees share the main clone's `info/exclude` (git's common dir), so they\n    inherit the narrowing from the clone they belong to. Where the file is not tracked, FR-5's\n2250→    check reads `UNWIRED reason=no-hooks-file` in any worktree that lacks it, so the gap is a halt,\n    not a silent pass.\n- **D-11 `review_independence_unrecorded` for every implementer (operator decision, 2026-10-16):**\n  widened from agy-only to `codex|agy|grok|claude`, matching D-9 (FR-7). Warning only, never a\n  block. Rationale: it fires only when the recording step was skipped, so a feature that follows\n  the new flow stays quiet; without it, a missing record would hide a Codex-on-Codex self-review,\n  which D-9 now flags when it IS recorded.\n  - **Pre-existing features — settled, no grandfather rule.** The check applies to every feature\n    whose Phase-7 check runs after this ships. Archived features are untouched because nothing\n    re-runs the check over them (FR-7). A grandfather rule would need a signal separating \"began\n2260→    before this shipped\" from \"skipped the recording step\", and the only signal the record offers\n    — absence of `review_independence` — is the skip itself, so any such rule would also silence\n    the case the warning exists for. A warning on an in-flight feature costs one backfilled entry\n    or one report line.\n- **D-12 Shared shell-policy module (was the design's OQ-A; operator decision, 2026-10-16):** the\n  FR-6 policy moves into the existing `h-mad/scripts/h_mad_target_identity.py`, not a new module.\n  The design's reason: `test_h_mad_codex_tdd_gate_judge.py` `test_codex_gate_judge_timeout_reason`\n  builds a tree by copying only the Codex gate, a judge stub and `h_mad_target_identity.py`\n  (`grep -n -A12 'def test_codex_gate_judge_timeout_reason' h-mad/tests/test_h_mad_codex_tdd_gate_judge.py`),\n  so a new module would be missing there, and FR-6 forbids editing that test.\n2270→- **D-13 FR-8 timing (was the design's OQ-B; operator decision, 2026-10-16):** FR-8 runs as a\n  post-merge stage after Phase 7's integrate, and the feature is recorded COMPLETE only after it.\n  Reason: the tracer must run the gate at its installed path, which is main.\n- **D-14 Leap-second `.ts` refused (orchestrator decision, design audit cycle 1, 2026-10-16):**\n  FR-1 narrows `implementer_switch.ts` to seconds `00`–`59`. Reason: state timestamps are written\n  by h-mad's own writer through Python `datetime`, which cannot produce a seconds field of `60`.\n- **D-15 Durable author record (OD-E2, operator decision, 2026-10-16: option (a)):** FR-1 gains\n  `implementation_authors`, an optional array of `{module, agent}` entries appended (never\n  removed or altered) through FR-7's Record mechanism, one per accepted 5e GREEN or accepted fix\n  dispatch and never for a 5d RED. FR-7 §\"Attribution\" reads a module's author set from it.\n2280→  Reason: design v1.2 (OD-E2) showed AC-7.3's \"multi-author, recovered\" fixture could not pass\n  without a durable author record, because the assembler's prompt, out and log files are under\n  `/tmp` and its slug names no agent. The fixture stays as written and now has its source. The\n  rejected option (b) was to amend the fixture to \"no entry\".\n- **D-16 Directory target refused under active step5 (OD-2, operator decision, 2026-10-16):** a\n  class-(b) target that names an existing directory is refused with `kind=judge-error` when the\n  scan reads `active` or `unknown`, and allowed when it reads `inactive` (FR-4 deliberate\n  difference 6). Reason: impl-plan v1.1 Task 6 showed that `canonicalise` returns no name and\n  no `unresolvable` flag for `.`, `src/`, `src` and the root, so the class-(b) sequence would\n  otherwise allow a directory target under `active`. Residual: a trailing-`/` path that names no\n2290→  existing directory is not covered and is judged as a file name.\n\n## Open questions for the operator\n\nNone — OQ-1/OQ-2 settled 2026-10-16; the design's OQ-A/OQ-B settled 2026-10-16 (D-12, D-13);\nthe design's OD-E2 settled 2026-10-16 (D-15); the impl-plan's OD-2 settled 2026-10-16 (D-16).\n\n## Non-goals\n\n- Per-task implementer selection. It can come later on the same field.\n2300→- Refusing a peer's production write in that peer's own gate when the other peer is the resolved\n  implementer, for example a Codex verifier dispatch writing production code under\n  `implementer=agy`. Residual: the test-first judge still applies, and the per-dispatch\n  `git status --short` diff (agy-runtime.md §\"Author and reviewer roles\") is what shows it.\n- A write-time gate for grok, and authorship rules for agy as the orchestrating host.\n- Routing audit legs by `agy_status`.\n\n## Owed by other documents\n\nNone of these are edited by this spec.\n2310→- `h-mad/references/agy-runtime.md` says there is no `SessionStart` on two surfaces:\n  - §\"Hooks\" (\"There is **no `SessionStart`**\");\n  - the §\"Construct mapping\" table's `hook-event` row (\"there is no `SessionStart`\").\n\n  agy's current hooks.md lists `SessionStart` as a supported event. Both surfaces need a re-check\n  against the installed agy (`agy --version` prints 1.4.0 at `200ffd52`), they are corrected\n  together, and the correction stamps the version it was checked against.\n  (`grep -n 'SessionStart' h-mad/references/agy-runtime.md` at `200ffd52`: 2 matching lines, one\n  under each heading.) Residual: this is a content predicate, so a third restatement added later\n  is found only by re-running the grep.\n2320→- **Design (v1.8 items).** FR-4: enumerate the closed kind list and give every deny path a member\n  (E6); its claim that `unknown` \"denies `kind=judge-error`, as the Codex gate does for a shell\n  command\" is false at `cff71b49` — the Codex gate's `unknown` shell deny carries no `kind=`\n  token. FR-7: name where the complete author set is kept (E2) and correct \"the GREEN dispatch the\n  orchestrator accepted at 5d\" to 5e (E4).\n- **Design (v1.9, D-15).** FR-1: add `implementation_authors` with FR-1's entry schema to the\n  schema JSON and a `state-schema.md` row. FR-7: retire \"the complete set is kept in the\n  procedure\" and OD-E2 in favour of reading the set from `implementation_authors`; the\n  `--append` mechanism (DO-3) must accept this key with the same guarantees, and the \"When the\n  dispatch list is lost\" rule becomes FR-7's three closed unrecoverable cases.\n2330→- **Design (spec v1.10).** FR-4: record D-16 as a directory check before the target is judged,\n  and add it to §\"Deliberate differences\" as item 6; FR-4 `--self-check`: a verdict `FAIL` exits\n  0 (replacing \"a nonzero exit\"), and the Codex gate's `FAIL shell-policy` is corrected in impl-plan\n  Task 4; FR-2: adopt the halts `step5:implementer_invalid` and `step5:implementer_unresolved`.\n- **Impl-plan.** AC-7.3's surface (FR-7 §\"Attribution\" must have code to drive, or the AC is an\n  OPEN-DECISION), now including the pre-field fixture; AC-1.3's fixtures; AC-4.1's `unknown`\n  fixtures and AC-4.2's per-deny-path kind fixtures.\n- **Plan.** If the plan carries FR-1's field list or AC-7.3's fixtures, it gains\n  `implementation_authors`, AC-1.3 and the pre-field fixture.\n\n2340→## Version History\n- v1.0: Initial spec (2026-10-16) from brainstorm v1.1. Settles 8 open questions (D-1..D-8): run_command takes the Codex command-shape rule, not a stated residual; per-project .agents/hooks.json; root from handler cwd; provisional 1500 s timeout then tracer formula; PATH+status+env availability; feature-only implementer; flag in state; audit legs unchanged. Folds brief fix 3 (#8) WIRE-PIN new-caller RED case as FR-9. OQ-1 (Codex-reviews-Codex flag), OQ-2 (.agents exclude) for the operator.\n- v1.1: Operator decisions 2026-10-16 (2026-10-16). OQ-1 -> D-9: FR-7 flag fires for any reviewer == implementer, Codex reviewing Codex included; warning only. OQ-2 -> D-10: operator narrowed .git/info/exclude /.agents/ to /.agents/skills/ (check-ignore evidence at d65374e9), no git add -f; per-clone residual stated. Open questions section now empty.\n- v1.2: Operator decision 2026-10-16 -> D-11: FR-7 review_independence_unrecorded widened from agy-only to every implementer (codex|agy|grok|claude), matching D-9; warning only. Trigger pinned to a 6a-prime leg that ran (archreview READY_TO_MERGE|WITH_FIXES|NO) with no leg=6a-prime entry; other archreview values already surface. Pre-existing features settled: no grandfather rule, archived features untouched (no batch caller). AC-7.2 added.\n- v1.3: Plan-author findings against v1.2 (2026-10-16). FR-3: a missing state-line field refuses judge-error (tree precedent: missing-field fixture), so the two gate stub-line fixtures are re-shaped with assertions byte-identical — the AC-3.2 exception; test_h_mad_tdd_judge.py ACTIVE_RE and line-quoting mutation rows follow the line. FR-7: writer has no append (--set replaces); add-without-loss is a requirement, mechanism a design obligation; AC-7.1 gains the two-entry fixture. FR-6: rows re-pointed to the shared module, then ALL_CAUGHT; moved names stay bound on the Codex gate module. FR-9/AC-9.1/FR-10: failure-recovery.md red_wrong_reason row; rule surfaces closed with sweep. FR-1/FR-10/AC-1.1: fallback_agent description on schema JSON and state-schema.md, pinned literals named. Owed: second SessionStart surface (Construct-mapping hook-event row). All ACs reformatted to the h_mad_ac_census.py grammar (- **AC-n.m:**).\n- v1.4: Plan audit cycle 1 findings owned by the spec (2026-10-16). F1 -> FR-5 exit-code table: AGY-GATE WIRED/UNWIRED (all seven reasons, no-hooks-file included) exit 0 and the assembler's agy_tdd_hook_unverified verdict HALT exits 0; UNREADABLE and a no-token check exit 2; existing assembler HALTs keep exit 2 (residual); AC-5.2. F2 -> FR-1: .ts is RFC 3339 date-time; neither validator path enforces format today (probed), so refusal on both paths is the requirement and the mechanism a design obligation; rfc3339-validator hazard; residual for started_ts/owner_heartbeat_ts/halt_ts; AC-1.2. F3 -> FR-9 differential corpus over four missing-symbol cases, changed set == {new-caller}; AC-9.2. F4 -> FR-9 incident replay of HemaSuite notebooklm F-3 (T4/T5 WIRE-PIN 1, T6 WIRE-PIN 2) with wire-cut re-run; AC-9.3. F5 -> FR-8 step 2a forced handler timeout with four classified outcomes; step 2 now settles two unknowns; FR-5 re-run trigger includes it; AC-8.2. F6 -> no contradiction; FR-3 now lists the real-line assertions (two in test_h_mad_tdd_gate_fallback_agent.py, four tests in test_h_mad_tdd_judge.py) as placement constraints; AC-3.2 extended.\n- v1.5: Plan audit cycle 2 findings owned by the spec, final corrective revision after the round cap (2026-10-16). B -> FR-10 adds the SKILL.md teammate-audit bullet: audit-leg selection vs resolved authorship (Claude only when resolved=claude), anchor prefix and the four pinned tokens kept. C -> FR-9 surface census gains surface 5, the h_mad_assemble_tdd.py assemble() wiring header (split string literals), and its pin test_h_mad_assemble_tdd.py TestWiringShape.test_the_pin_is_told_what_its_red_must_be; the census method now joins Python adjacent literals via ast and collapses markdown whitespace, measured at 9c3b799d; residual: run-time-built strings. AC-9.1 lists the header. D -> FR-9 replay and AC-9.3 name all three pins with files and commits (531a6296b, 7aa0d9dec, c2d671f6b); T5/T6 replay evaluates the direct-call / two-argument getattr form; the committed scaffolded RED (AssertionError) is recorded as old-verdict valid, correcting the false old-verdict=halt claim. A -> new AC-9.4: the six rule copies bound to one authority, with a per-copy control.\n- v1.6: Advisory delta review of plan v1.3 (608e430), spec-owned parts (2026-10-16). Should-fix 3 -> FR-9: the joined reading (ast str constants) is blind to non-str text in a .py file; axis named with three members (comments, files ast.parse rejects, bytes literals). The sweep is now the union of the line grep and the joined reading, which adds joined .py comment tokens; parse failures are reported as unparsed=N and read by hand; residual: run-time-built strings and a bytes literal split across lines. Measured at 5f72c2f3: union 7 files, line-grep-minus-joined 0, comment hits 0 (incidental), unparsed=0. AC-9.1 re-run is the union and reports unparsed=N. Must-fix 1 (spec side) and Should-fix 4 -> AC-9.4: the carve-out text is the whole three-sentence FR-9 block; each copy is read from its own source location, never from the assembled prompt or a file-level contains-check over a file holding more than one copy; a locator matching zero or several spans fails the test; the control mutates exactly one copy, runs once per copy and once per sentence in at least one copy, with a find unique in its file (mutation harness anchor_status), the .py find written against source bytes including the literal split.\n- v1.7: Design audit cycle 1, spec-owned items (2026-10-16). S1 (codex C1) -> FR-1/AC-1.1/AC-1.2 + D-14: .ts narrowed to seconds 00-59, a leap second (2016-12-31T23:59:60Z) refused on both paths; reason: every .ts comes from Python datetime via the writer, which cannot hold second 60 (orchestrator decision). S2 (codex C6) -> FR-7 Attribution: an entry's implementer is the author of the reviewed code, from its successful implementation dispatch, never implementer_switch.to (historical) or the implementer field; multi-author code is non-independent if the reviewer is any author. S3 -> FR-1/FR-10/AC-1.1: codex_status schema description and state-schema.md row rewritten to FR-2 authorship (no test pins the text; the fallback_agent-after-codex_status order pin stated); FR-8 Version: 1.4.0 at 200ffd52, each reading stamps its version; Owed SessionStart re-check re-stamped 1.4.0 at 200ffd52; FR-6/AC-6.1 shared module = h_mad_target_identity.py (D-12, OQ-A); FR-8/AC-8.1 post-merge stage, COMPLETE only after it (D-13, OQ-B). Open questions: OQ-A/OQ-B settled.\n- v1.8: Design audit cycle 2, final corrective revision after the round cap, not re-audited (2026-10-16). S1/E6 -> FR-4 'Every deny carries a kind': rule over every deny path, closed list owned by the design, spec names only judge-error, tool-not-admitted and the judge's Verdict kind; Codex gate has 6 of 13 kindless _deny call sites at cff71b49. E1 -> FR-4 'The activity reading': three-valued scan (active/unknown/inactive), every step5-gated deny also fires on unknown, class-(b) production .py still read through read_chain under inactive; workspacePaths and Tool classes sentences re-worded. E5 -> FR-4 Target keys: a non-empty-string value counts as no path key. S4 -> FR-4 'Deliberate differences from the Codex gate': design's four adopted plus the kind rule as a fifth. S2/E2/E4 -> FR-7 Attribution sub-bullets (5e GREEN + accepted fixes, never 5d RED; complete author set, never a projection) and new AC-7.3. S5 -> adopted: reviewer none of the authors names the latest accepted dispatch's author. S3 -> leap-second ValueError quote drops the version-dependent tail. AC-4.1/AC-4.2 extended; FR-6 states the unknown-scan shell deny; Owed gains design and impl-plan items.\n2350→- v1.9: Operator decision OD-E2 (a) (2026-10-16) -> D-15: FR-1 gains implementation_authors, an optional array of {module, agent} (agent codex|agy|grok|claude; module non-empty string; no extra keys), appended never altered through FR-7's Record mechanism on accepting a 5e GREEN or fix dispatch, never 5d RED or a halted/timed-out/rejected dispatch; duplicates kept, array order = acceptance order. New AC-1.3 (valid entries accepted, unknown agent and other malformed shapes refused, both validator paths). FR-7 Attribution reads the author set from the field on every leg; unrecoverable cases closed to three (field absent: pre-field features; no entry for the module: a record-step defect; malformed) -> no entry. AC-7.3 multi-author fixture unchanged with its source named; pre-field fixture added. AC-7.2 'only FR-1 field' narrowed to routing field. FR-10 SKILL.md Phase-5 step bullet. Owed: design FR-1/FR-7 (retire procedure-kept set and OD-E2), impl-plan AC-1.3 + pre-field fixture, plan mirror.\n- v1.10: Owed lines from impl-plan v1.1 (Phase 5b), final corrective revision, not re-audited (2026-10-16). P1 -> D-16 (OD-2, operator): a class-(b) target naming an existing directory denies kind=judge-error under active/unknown, allowed under inactive; FR-4 deliberate difference 6, AC-4.1 fixture; residual: trailing-/ path naming no existing directory. P2 -> FR-2 halts named: step5:implementer_invalid (invalid), step5:implementer_unresolved (no-record, unreadable), step5:implementer_switch_unrecorded (record=failed); adopted from impl-plan Task 16; failure-recovery.md rows not added (residual). P3 -> FR-5: the gate's --self-check verdict FAIL prints its token on stdout and exits 0, operational error nonzero; Codex gate FAIL shell-policy corrected in impl-plan Task 4; Codex FAIL parser residual; AC-5.2 gains the self-check fixture and mutant. P4 -> FR-9 Hook line keeps Claude's h-mad-tdd-gate.sh (test_hook_line_names_both_gates). Owed: design FR-4/FR-2 lines.\n\n\nAudit rubric (TWO axes, both mandatory):\n\nAxis A — Generic adversarial:\n- Contradictions inside the doc\n- Gaps (missing error paths, untestable AC, unstated assumptions)\n- Weak claims (load-bearing decisions without justification)\n2360→- Scope creep (work outside the stated feature boundary)\n\nAxis B — Invariant compliance (any breach = must-fix, you cannot downgrade).\nTwo layers, both binding:\n\n### Base invariants (workflow-universal; non-overridable by any project file — but the operator `## Acknowledged-not-fixed` sidecar still applies to base findings):\n\n# H-MAD Base Invariants — Axis B (workflow-universal)\n\n> Shipped with the `/h-mad` skill. The orchestrator inlines this file verbatim into the\n2370→> `INLINE_BASE_INVARIANTS` slot (name written bare — a bracketed mention inside an inlined\n> file survives substitution and reaches the reviewer as an unfilled-looking placeholder)\n> of every plan / design / impl-plan audit, **before** the\n> per-project `<PROJECT_ROOT>/.h-mad/invariants.md` domain layer. These rules apply to every\n> project that uses `/h-mad` and are **non-overridable by any project file**: a project's\n> invariants file may add domain rules but may not downgrade or delete a base rule. The\n> operator `## Acknowledged-not-fixed` sidecar escape hatch still applies to base findings\n> (a base item listed under that section in a sidecar `.audit.v<N+1>.md` is a conscious,\n> audited deferral — it is excluded from the gate count exactly like any other layer's item).\n>\n2380→> Axis A (generic adversarial review) is covered by the audit-prompt template — not repeated here.\n\n## Audit-gate signal discipline\n- Any gate/check whose verdict the orchestrator consumes MUST communicate PASS/FAIL via an\n  explicit **stdout token** and **exit 0** on a normal verdict (PASS or FAIL). Using a non-zero\n  process exit to mean \"FAIL\" is a violation — a non-zero exit registers as a Claude Code tool\n  failure (`PostToolUseFailure`) and leaks into coexisting plugins' error handling. A non-zero\n  exit is permitted ONLY for genuine operational errors (missing/unreadable input).\n\n## Single-source contract\n2390→- A rule applied by more than one surface (e.g. an audit blocking-item count read by both the\n  orchestrator gate step and a precondition checker) MUST have exactly one authoritative\n  implementation that all surfaces call, OR a test asserting byte-equivalence across surfaces.\n  Independent re-implementations that can silently diverge are a violation.\n- A contract stated in a template (e.g. an empty-section sentinel) MUST match what the consuming\n  gate logic treats as that case. Template guidance and gate behavior disagreeing is a violation.\n\n## Standalone / no plugin dependency\n- The skill MUST NOT acquire a **runtime dependency** on any other plugin (OMC, bkit,\n  context-mode, etc.) or external skill (spec-kit, b-mad, pdca). Coexistence accommodations\n2400→  (sharing a state filename, conforming doc structure to an external validator's required\n  sections) are allowed; *requiring another plugin installed at runtime* is a violation.\n\n## No new external dependency\n- Scripts MUST use only Python stdlib plus tooling already depended upon by the skill (the\n  agent dispatch substrate (cmux or orca, via `hmad-dispatch`); `jq` where the existing hook\n  already uses it; `pytest`).\n  Introducing a new third-party package or new CLI is a violation.\n- **Dispatched agent CLIs are not script dependencies.** `codex`, `agy` and `grok` are optional\n  runtime *agents*: reached only through a `hmad-dispatch` verb (`exec`, `send`, …), never imported,\n2410→  required, or invoked by a script or test for its own work — tests reach them through stubs, and an\n  absent agent CLI is a dispatch-time condition the verb reports, never a failure of the skill.\n  The list is closed: adding an agent to it is an operator decision recorded here, not something a\n  document or a dispatch may assume. (`grok` added 2026-09-28, operator decision on\n  `grok-codex-fallback` design audit v2.)\n\n## Portable time bounds\n- A time-bounded command MUST NOT be written as `timeout <s> <cmd>` or `gtimeout <s> <cmd>`.\n  **Neither is a macOS system component**, so the form is unportable in both directions.\n  A stock box ships neither and the call fails at 127; a box carrying `brew install coreutils`\n2420→  runs it fine and the bound silently rests on a CLI h-mad cannot assume is there.\n  **What your box has is not an input to this rule.** h-mad already owns a time-bounder that\n  reachable wherever `hmad-dispatch` is, so the form is forbidden unconditionally — not because\n  of what happens downstream. (Reachable, not universal: where the wrapper itself cannot be\n  called the rule below still applies — halt, do not drop the bound.) Do not reason from a local probe to an exemption. For anything committed\n  or dispatched the form is additionally a new external CLI dependency (§\"No new external\n  dependency\"); a downstream 127 is how that eventually surfaces, never the reason.\n  Do not settle the question by probing: a local `command -v timeout` that succeeds is not\n  licence, it proves only that this box has coreutils, and it means the 127 that would have\n  exposed the improvisation never fires. Use `hmad-dispatch run --timeout <s> -- <cmd...>`,\n2430→  which exits 124 at the deadline.\n- Where a 127 does fire, its aftermath is worse than the 127 itself. Measured: the reflex after `timeout: command not found`\n  is to re-run the same command **unbounded** and call it \"checking directly\". That does not fail\n  at the deadline, it hangs the phase, and in every log h-mad reads a hang and slow work are the\n  same bytes. A plan, a design, or a prompt that leaves an agent no reachable time-bounder is a\n  violation; the correct behaviour when none exists is to **halt**, not to drop the bound.\n\n## Doc-template superset compliance\n- Generated phase documents whose type is validated by an external doc-structure validator\n  (e.g. bkit PDCA: `plan` → `docs/01-plan/...`, `design` → `docs/02-design/...`,\n2440→  `report` → `docs/04-report/...`) MUST be supersets that satisfy that validator's required\n  sections AND retain the existing h-mad sections. Dropping an h-mad section or failing the\n  validator is a violation.\n- The contract binds the **saved document**, not only the template it came from. Where such a\n  validator reclassifies a document by a substring of its *content* — bkit's `isPlanPlus` promotes\n  a plan to a larger required-section list on seeing `Plan-Plus`, `Plan Plus`, `plan-plus`,\n  `Brainstorming-Enhanced`, or `Intent Discovery` anywhere in the file — a generated document\n  carrying that substring is a violation even though the template is clean. Guard it mechanically\n  (`scripts/h_mad_doc_shape_check.py`); the literals are unremarkable prose, so an author cannot\n  be expected to avoid them from memory.\n2450→\n## Operator-override preservation\n- The `## Acknowledged-not-fixed` sidecar override mechanism MUST remain functional for all\n  layers (base + project). Any gate change that causes overridden items to still count as\n  blocking, or that ignores the sidecar, is a violation.\n\n## Backward compatibility\n- A change to the audit gate MUST preserve the PASS verdict for audit docs that passed before\n  the change. Flipping a historically-passing audit to FAIL (absent a real new blocking item)\n  is a violation.\n2460→\n## Marker discipline\n- Orchestrator phase transitions and halts MUST emit `[H-MAD]` log markers so a run is\n  diagnosable from logs alone. Silent state transitions are a violation.\n\n## Mutation verification\n- A step that mutates state (a git operation, a file write, a remote/CLI call) MUST verify the\n  mutation by **re-reading the resulting state**; an **exit code is not evidence that a mutation\n  occurred**. A plan, design, or implementation that treats a zero exit — or an echoed success\n  string such as `Sent N bytes` or `{\"ok\":true}` — as proof of the intended effect is a violation.\n2470→- The failure this blocks is silent and looks exactly like success: two `zsh` no-ops (backtick\n  execution inside `-m`, a leading-dash path) both exited 0 while doing nothing, and a dispatch\n  reported `Sent 7293 bytes` into a dead pane. Where a command reports on its own behaviour, the\n  check must read the *thing it was supposed to change*, from a separate call.\n- **When several mutations target the SAME line, vary one field and keep the anchor shared.** A\n  guard whose failure has separable parts — exit code, stream routing, message content — needs one\n  mutation per part, each replacing the same anchor. That shape is what proves a content assertion\n  load-bearing: the mutants that keep exit and stream and strip only the text are exactly the ones a\n  returncode-only test survives. Two discriminators worth writing into the spec: a\n  first-vs-last-occurrence mutant survives whenever the sought item is last in BOTH regions, so the\n2480→  discriminating fixture must put the decoys between the markers and leave the tail empty; and\n  `survived` has four distinct causes — missing guard, equivalent mutant, weak test, and a mutant\n  that never ran — which the verdict token collapses into one word. Diagnose which before acting.\n\n## Test discrimination\n- A test or guard MUST be **observed failing against the unfixed code** before it is trusted.\n  For a regression test, revert the fix and re-run; for a guard, stub it to its permissive value\n  and re-run the suite. **Zero failures is a finding, not a reassurance** — it means the check is\n  unenforced, not that the code is safe. Keeping a check that has never been seen to fail is a\n  violation.\n2490→- A check that cannot fail is decoration, and it is worse than no check because it reports\n  coverage that does not exist. Two guards shipped green this way and were caught only by\n  stubbing: one passed solely because an unrelated helper stripped the env var its subject read,\n  and a documentation test passed with the documented guidance deleted, because both of its\n  component words already appeared in nearby prose. Neither was visible to review or to a green run.\n- The mutation must itself be verified (§\"Mutation verification\"): a `.replace()` that matches\n  nothing exits 0 and reports the guard as enforced.\n- **A criterion that cannot discriminate in EITHER direction is vacuous, and it survives both a\n  green run and a mutation battery.** The stronger case of the bullet above: not \"this check never\n  fails\" but \"this check returns the same observation whether the behaviour is present or absent\".\n2500→  Measured on AC-2.5b, which spied on `is_cli_available` *returning* `False` — the forced-`True`\n  mutation emits an identical record and returns the same value, so neither the passing run nor the\n  mutated run could tell the two states apart. AC-7.5c was the same shape. **Neither audit leg saw\n  either one**; the revising author found AC-2.5b while fixing an unrelated must. A document audit\n  reads prose and cannot ask \"does this pin discriminate?\", so the question has to be asked where the\n  criterion is written: **name the observation that differs between the implemented and unimplemented\n  states, and if you cannot name one, the criterion is decoration** — delete it or replace it with a\n  positive assertion only the implemented behaviour produces. Spying on a return value that both\n  states produce is the recurring form.\n- **Mutating a path-resolution function can disable the suite's own isolation.** Tests usually\n2510→  isolate by pointing an env override at a temp path, and that override is honoured by a branch —\n  the same kind of branch a mutation deletes. Stubbing the override branch in `_pin_file` redirected\n  every pin write in the suite onto the developer's live session file and replaced two real agent\n  handles, while the run reported 642 passed. Before mutating anything that decides *where* state is\n  written, snapshot the real target and restore it, or run in a sandboxed working directory.\n- **A stub must model the step the real system CONSUMES.** The rules above are about asserting the\n  right thing; this one is about the fixture telling the truth. A stub that replays state the real\n  system consumes once makes a test pass before the fix exists: an orca stub replayed an acked\n  delivery forever, so a sibling-cache test re-matched from the queue, pinned nothing, and passed\n  against unmodified code. Cardinality is part of the model — a fake that writes a path once where\n2520→  production writes it twice let a verb never dispatch with 57 tests green. Before trusting a stub,\n  ask which of its effects are destructive in production and make the stub destroy them too.\n\n## Verifying a review finding\n- **A review finding has three separable parts — facts, concern, prescription — and they fail\n  INDEPENDENTLY.** Before applying a prescription, diff it against the RED tests and the spec's\n  acceptance criteria. A finding that matches the design doc but breaks tests means the DESIGN\n  drifted, not the implementation. Measured: one pass's prescription broke a guard (1 failed, 53\n  passed) while its concern was entirely real, and another review's prescription was backwards.\n- Test a prescription by applying it as a mutation and reverting it. When two passes dissent, ask\n2530→  what the codebase ALREADY does — twice the house pattern beat both readings.\n\n## Guard narrowing\n- When a change **deliberately makes a guard accept something it used to reject**, the relaxation\n  MUST be shown to be *exactly* the intended case: run a corpus of inputs through the old and new\n  logic and diff the verdicts, then account for **every** input whose verdict softened. A green\n  suite is not evidence here — it encodes the cases someone already thought of, which is the wrong\n  population when the question is \"what else did this let through?\". This is the inverse of\n  §\"Mutation verification\": that proves a guard still bites, this proves a loosening did not widen.\n- The relaxation MUST rest on a **guarantee of the thing being parsed**, not on a heuristic that\n2540→  re-implements it. Narrowing a scanner by \"ignoring quoted regions\" means re-deriving the target\n  language's quoting rules in a regex, which is how a bypass gets introduced; narrowing it by a rule\n  the language itself guarantees is safe. Verify that guarantee **against the real interpreter**,\n  not its documentation (§\"Assumption verification\").\n- Both halves were load-bearing when this was written. Narrowing a heredoc scanner so it stopped\n  denying inert prose, a 23-input differential corpus flagged one case as a regression; running the\n  shape through real `bash` showed the body genuinely never expands, so the *test expectation* was\n  wrong and the code was right. Without the corpus that case ships as either an unnoticed hole or a\n  \"fix\" applied to correct behaviour — and nothing else would have distinguished them. The same run\n  confirmed the intended relaxations numbered exactly two, and that the remaining 21 verdicts were\n2550→  untouched.\n- A narrowing is scoped by **which checks consult the reduced input**, not by the reduction itself.\n  Exempting the quoted region from *one* class of check while every other class kept scanning the\n  full input is what kept the change from widening; a blanket exemption would have disabled\n  detections that were still correct for reasons unrelated to the false positive.\n\n## Connection enforcement\n- A task whose deliverable is a **connection** — a call site, an import, a registration, a route, a\n  flag or value propagated across a boundary — MUST ship a test that **fails when the connection\n  alone is removed and the callee is left intact**. A test that exercises the callee directly is not\n2560→  evidence that the callee is reached. Shipping a connection with no such test is a violation.\n- **A whole-module revert cannot establish this**, because it removes both sides: the RED split\n  returns identically for a wired and an unwired build. The same blindness runs through every other\n  layer — a RED phase goes red because the callee is *absent*, an anti-gaming audit finds a\n  callee-scoped unit test perfectly discriminating, and a review of the diff sees a call site that is\n  *present* and therefore reads as correct. **Presence is not enforcement.** Measured across two\n  consecutive wiring tasks: each shipped its single load-bearing decision untested through every\n  audit cycle and through the RED phase, and only a mutation scoped to the connection caught it.\n- Mutate the connection in **both directions**: remove it → the wire test must fail; force it to fire\n  unconditionally → the fall-through/negative test must fail. One direction certifies a connection\n2570→  that exists but is unconditional. Verify the mutation landed before trusting the run\n  (§\"Mutation verification\") — a revert that never happened reports as a pass.\n\n## Incident replay\n- A fix motivated by a specific observed incident MUST be **replayed against the real artifacts\n  already on disk** that motivated it, not only against cases authored alongside the fix.\n  **Synthetic cases alone are a violation** whenever such artifacts exist and are reachable.\n- Cases written next to a fix inherit the author's model of the bug, so they agree with it by\n  construction. A detector validated on 14 handcrafted samples rejected the real historical label\n  it was written to accept; the same replay then measured the true rate (7 of 13), which reclassified\n2580→  the defect from a one-off into the majority case. Replay is how a fix is told from a belief.\n\n## Assumption verification\n- Every **load-bearing assumption** in a plan or design — an API's accepted inputs, a command's\n  output shape, a boundary, a default — MUST be **executed as a throwaway command before it is\n  written into the design**, and the design **cites the observed output**. An assumption asserted\n  without evidence, where evidence was one command away, is a violation.\n- Design review cannot catch a wrong assumption: it reads as reasonable, and the implementation\n  and its tests are then both built from the same wrong model, so they agree with each other and\n  pass. Tracer-bulleting the assumptions of one feature confirmed a `--porcelain` boundary,\n2590→  confirmed a base-ref chain, and found a truncation hole — all before any code existed. Separately,\n  a selector grammar assumed from a wrapper's own code was wrong in a way that had already let a\n  destructive verb run unguarded.\n- The evidence belongs in the document, not only in the author's terminal. A cited output is\n  checkable by a reviewer; \"I verified this\" is not.\n- **Attributing a failure to a cause requires the controlled PAIR, not just the repro.** Run the\n  case with the blamed step and the case without it, and require the observable to differ. A repro\n  that reproduces confirms the symptom, never the cause. Measured four times: pane readiness was\n  blamed for an `injected:false` that a missing `--inject` flag fully explained, and that causality\n  shipped in a doc and a PR body; a title-only reading concluded \"no agents running\" and the\n2600→  operator corrected it; and two carried repros were each falsified by a control that removed the\n  step they blamed. Re-run the MEASUREMENT as well as the claim — a brief's conclusion can be right\n  while its method is wrong.\n\n- **A contract to RECOGNISE something needs a real instance of it, captured from the running\n  system, before any gate scores it clean** — a banner, a log line, a response shape.\n  `pin-agents-tail-banner` passed 2663 tests, 49 mutations and 53 audit cycles while its matcher\n  hit 0 of 5 real agent banners: all 12 corpus positives were written by hand. The fixture corpus must include at\n  least one captured sample with its capture command cited; an idealised fixture is not a sample.\n\n2610→## Behavioural premises carry their command\n- A premise about **behaviour** — \"X currently does Y\", \"this guard fires when Z\", \"the suite\n  collects N\", \"the parser rejects W\" — MUST carry the exact command that produced it, inline and\n  runnable, beside the observed output. An output recorded without its command is not\n  re-derivable, and a premise that cannot be cheaply re-run gets re-READ instead, cycle after\n  cycle, by every reviewer in turn. Measured: one false behavioural premise survived **10** audit\n  cycles that way.\n- Narrower than §\"Assumption verification\" and it fails at a different moment: there the evidence\n  was never gathered; here it was gathered once, written down as a conclusion, and thereby made\n  uncheckable. Both readers are then reasoning about prose.\n2620→- **The command belongs in the document even when the answer is one number**, because the number\n  alone does not say what was counted. Measured: two readers measuring \"the same\" census got **3**\n  and **23**, having run different commands, and neither could tell which was wrong until the\n  command was written down beside it. `git grep -l '```' -- '*.py'` is the census; \"21 files\" is\n  a claim about one.\n- A premise whose command has become expensive to re-run is not exempt. Say so, and say what the\n  cheap proxy is — an unrunnable premise is a premise no later cycle can falsify.\n\n## Counts a dispatch reports\n- **Re-derive every COUNT a dispatched agent reports; never carry it into a document, a commit\n2630→  message or a verdict on the agent's word.** A report that is right about its actions can still be\n  wrong about its numbers, and the two failures look identical from outside — the prose is accurate,\n  the figures are confident, and nothing in the report signals which is which.\n- Measured 2026-08-26: an `exec codex` report stated an anchor sweep as `mutations=238` when the\n  tree held **244**, with every spec byte-identical to HEAD and every other figure in the same\n  report checking out. Nothing had changed; the number was invented. The cheap control existed the\n  whole time — `--check-anchors` prints the count in about a second.\n- This is narrower than §\"Assumption verification\" and it applies at a different moment: there the\n  risk is an assumption written without evidence, here it is evidence that was produced, looks\n  right, and is false. Prefer a count you can re-derive with one command over any count a\n2640→  dispatch hands you, and when a count is load-bearing for a verdict, re-derive it before recording\n  the verdict rather than after.\n\n## Wrapper–runtime reconciliation\n- A wrapper verb over an **external runtime's CLI** MUST be exercised **live against that runtime**\n  — a full create → list → remove cycle, or the verb's own equivalent round trip — before it ships,\n  and its **output-key extraction fixed against the observed envelope**. Shipping a verb whose\n  response shape is known only from stub fixtures is a violation, however green the suite is.\n- Stub fixtures encode the author's model of the envelope, so the wrapper and its tests agree with\n  each other and both are wrong together — the Incident-replay gap, applied to response parsing.\n2650→  Four occurrences: a create-response `.id` read from the envelope (a per-request correlation uuid\n  that always exists, so it yielded a plausible but useless handle) in two different verbs; then\n  probing a live response for a delivery-id field surfaced `run_required` — the whole structured\n  path had been **dead at its first mutation**, and no stub test could see it because the stub had\n  no concept of a Run binding. Later the same day the real ack key proved to be `deliveryId` while\n  the extraction chain led with `delivery_id`, and the only test covering that loop pinned the\n  spelling the runtime never sends.\n- The live probe is what finds these, not the review: each was invisible to design review, to the\n  suite, and to a careful reading of the vendor guide. Where a live run is impossible (destructive\n  verb, no credentials), say so in the doc and name what stayed unverified — an unmeasured shape is\n2660→  an open risk, not a pass.\n\n## Regression provenance\n- When a change makes an existing test fail, the plan or design MUST establish whether that test\n  **asserts current behaviour that the change is fixing** before proposing to edit it. **Changing an\n  existing test to pass is a violation unless** the doc states that the test pinned a defect (or\n  stale contract) as correct, and cites what it asserted.\n- The reflex \"make the failing test pass\" preserves the bug when the test *was* the bug. Three tests\n  in one session asserted the defect as an acceptance criterion — a forwarded selector a live\n  runtime rejects, a create-response handle the pane never has, a cwd-relative path — and each would\n2670→  have survived a naive \"adjust the test\" edit. A red pre-existing test is evidence about the test as\n  often as about the change.\n\n## Both halves of a doc change\n- Removing a documented instruction, flag, or capability MUST be paired with **the executable\n  replacement landed** (or an explicit statement that the capability is intentionally dropped, with\n  the reason). A test asserting only that the old text is *gone* passes for a deletion that silently\n  lost the capability.\n- An unexecutable instruction was deleted in one session; a test checking only its absence would\n  have gone green while the run lost the ability it named. The gate is: assert the new path works in\n2680→  the same change that removes the old one.\n\n## Reimplementation parity\n- Replacing a third-party dependency (validator, parser, formatter) with an in-tree implementation\n  MUST ship a **differential test asserting identical results against the original** across a corpus\n  that covers every construct in use, AND against the **real artifacts on disk**. Shipping the\n  reimplementation with tests that exercise only itself is a violation.\n- A hand-rolled reimplementation is worth exactly what its differential test catches. A bundled\n  JSON-Schema validator agreed with the library on a construct-complete corpus and on the live state\n  records; the traps it had to match (annotation-only `format`, `bool` is not `integer`) were\n2690→  invisible without the library beside it. Parity on a synthetic corpus alone repeats the\n  Incident-replay gap: the corpus shares the author's model of the reimplementation.\n\n---\n\n## How agy uses this file\nagy reads this verbatim as the **base** portion of the Axis B rubric, before the project layer.\nAny finding that violates a base rule is auto-classified `## Must-fix` and cannot be downgraded.\n\n\n2700→### Project invariants (domain rules for this repository):\n\n# H-MAD Project Invariants — Axis B (BrightGold70/skills repo, domain layer)\n\n> Inlined by `/h-mad` as the **project (domain)** portion of Axis B, AFTER the\n> workflow-universal `invariants.base.md`. The base layer already enforces the\n> universal rules (audit-gate signal discipline, single-source contract,\n> no-plugin-dependency, doc-template superset compliance, operator-override\n> preservation, backward-compatibility, marker discipline) — they are NOT repeated\n> here. This file holds only rules specific to this repository (a collection of\n2710→> Claude Code skills: `SKILL.md` + `references/*.md` + `scripts/*.py` + `hooks/*.sh`).\n\n## Skill self-containment\n- A skill MUST remain runnable from a bare clone: no import of another skill's\n  internals, no hardcoded path outside the skill's own directory (except documented\n  host install locations: `~/.claude/...` for Claude Code, `~/.agents/skills/...` for\n  Codex, `~/.gemini/config/skills/...` for the Antigravity CLI (agy), each overridable\n  by its documented environment variable). Cross-skill coupling is a violation.\n  (Operator decision 2026-09-28, multi-host-runtime.)\n\n2720→## Skill manifest integrity\n- Every skill's `SKILL.md` MUST carry valid frontmatter with `name` and `description`.\n  A skill whose entry behavior is changed without updating its `SKILL.md` contract is\n  a violation.\n\n---\n\n## How agy uses this file\nagy reads this as the domain portion of Axis B, after the base layer. Domain-rule\nviolations are `## Must-fix`. If this repo has no domain-specific concern for a given\n2730→feature, this layer may legitimately add nothing beyond the base rules.\n\n\nAxis C — Spec reconciliation.\n\nThe spec is the source of truth for what this feature must do. A plan or design\nis derived from it and may legitimately argue for a narrower reading — but it\nmay not do so silently, because every downstream phase then measures the\nimplementation against a spec the design already walked away from.\n\n2740→Read the spec above and reconcile it against the target document **by\nidentifier**, not by impression:\n\n**Design audits — every acceptance criterion.** For each `AC-N.M` in the spec,\nclassify the design as exactly one of:\n\n| Classification | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `implemented-as-written` | the design covers the AC in its spec form |\n| `restated` | the design covers it in a different, usually narrower form |\n2750→| `absent` | the design does not address it at all |\n\n**Plan audits — every functional requirement.** Same three classifications at\n`FR-N` granularity. A plan is a strategy document and is not expected to restate\neach AC; it *is* expected to address every FR or explicitly defer it.\n\nReporting rules:\n\n- For every `restated` item, **quote both forms** — the spec's wording and the\n  design's — so the divergence is auditable rather than asserted. Then say which\n2760→  is narrower and in what respect.\n- `restated` and `absent` are **must-fix**. This is not a judgement that the\n  design is wrong: a narrowing may be well-argued and may be the right call. It\n  is a judgement that the divergence must be explicit and must land in the spec\n  before the gate clears, so the operator decides deliberately rather than\n  discovering it at verification.\n- An AC whose narrowing the spec **already reflects** is\n  `implemented-as-written`, not `restated`. That is the loop closing correctly.\n- Do not infer coverage from a section heading or a passing reference in a risk\n  or rationale table. An AC is covered when the document says what will satisfy\n2770→  it.\n\nReport Axis C as a table in your `## Summary`, then raise each `restated` or\n`absent` item as its own `## Must-fix` bullet.\n\nEffort contract (binding on you either way; MEASURED only on some dispatches):\n\n- **Sweep the whole document. Do not stop at the first blocking finding.** Measured\n  over 83 design / 74 plan / 34 impl-plan cycles on one feature: the median pass\n  returned **one** must-fix against a prompt asking for all of them, which turns every\n2780→  defect into its own round trip and is most of why those numbers are that large.\n- **Read the tree before asserting what is in it.** The documents are inlined above,\n  which is exactly why a pass can look thorough having opened nothing. Their claims are\n  *about* the tree — a `path:symbol`, a signature, a test name, a count against its own\n  list — and those are the claims that turn out to be wrong.\n- **When you re-derive a published figure, four things must match or your reading answers a\n  different question. Each has been got wrong here by an auditor, not only by the orchestrator:**\n  - *Commit.* Presence at a sha is not provenance. \"X was introduced in revision N\" needs the\n    needle PRESENT at N's landing commit **and ABSENT at N-1's** — two readings return N and\n    N+1 alike. `git rev-list <base>..<sha> -- <path>` first: if that commit does not touch the\n2790→    file, it is not the figure's provenance.\n  - *Corpus.* **Run the command the document DEFINES, not one that reproduces the document's\n    number.** A head-scoped series verified whole-blob agreed for four shas and then did not;\n    the gating auditor re-ran it, reported it reproduced, and the scope was wrong the whole\n    time. Agreement with a published value is evidence about arithmetic, never about scope.\n  - *Grammar.* `grep -c` answers \"how many lines contain this string\", never \"how many\n    instances of this concept exist\". Print the matching lines and classify them before you\n    publish a count — a Python kwarg and a diagnostic field spell identically. Over markdown,\n    collapse newlines and admit the inline-code delimiters (`` ` ``): a needle that cannot\n    match across a backtick reported 2 sites where there are 4.\n2800→  - *Shell.* Take each control in its own invocation; a `cd` in a chained command is not\n    scoped to that command.\n- **If a document publishes a screen with `# expect 0`, RUN IT** rather than reporting that it\n  is present. Three sheet entries certified \"no scoped census moved\" over a commit at which the\n  document's own trip-wire read 8.\n- **An ABSENCE claim is a figure.** \"No corpus instance …\", \"the corpus has none\", \"nothing in\n  this repository\" — hold each to the same command and sha as a count; both that shipped here\n  were false. A zero bounded only by some *unrelated* constraint is a defect: the document says\n  why it is zero and whether that reason is load-bearing.\n- **A stated PROPERTY of a screen is a claim about code — execute it.** \"No working-file edit can\n2810→  move this\" was false of the shipped line. Do the thing it says cannot move it, and re-run. A\n  control over an ALTERNATION tests the alternation, never its branches: a healthy sibling covers\n  a sick one (one boundary repair was half-applied three rounds running and passed every time).\n- **Your report is held to this too.** Every count carries its UNIT (`grep -n` lines are not\n  occurrences); a population you prescribe is derived once and cited twice; and your PRESCRIPTION\n  is a claim about the tree — one prescribed naming an enclosing function for a line that has\n  none. Check it or mark it unchecked, and give every alternative repair rather than the best-\n  reading one.\n- **A pass at or below two successful tool calls cannot certify a clean.** Writing the\n  report and its marker costs those two by itself, so at that floor nothing was read.\n2820→  When your transcript is captured — an `audit-cycle` dispatch — the combiner enforces\n  this and scores the cycle `UNVERIFIED` rather than clean. When it is not captured,\n  **nothing measures you and the rule binds you anyway**: say what you read. Findings\n  count at any effort; this bounds what a *clean* is worth, never what a finding is.\n- **State your evidence** on its own line in `## Summary`: `Evidence: <N> files opened,\n  <M> greps run.`\n\nOutput framing (mandatory — the orchestrator extracts on these markers):\n\nEmit your report bracketed by these two lines, each alone on its line, with\n2830→nothing before the first or after the second:\n\n    AUDIT-agy-coder-fallback-plan-v1-BEGIN\n    ...your report...\n    AUDIT-agy-coder-fallback-plan-v1-END\n\nYour report is read by scraping this terminal, and the previous cycle's report\nis usually still visible above the prompt. The markers are how the orchestrator\ntells your new report from that old one — omit them and the audit cannot be\nscored. Emit them even if you have nothing to report.\n2840→\nOutput schema (exact, no other top-level sections):\n\n## Summary\n<2-3 sentences>\n\n## Must-fix\n- <issue> — <why it breaks invariant or creates a hard gap>\n  *(empty section: write the single word `None` on its own line — NOT a `- None` bullet; a leading `- ` would be counted as a blocking item)*\n\n2850→## Should-fix\n- <issue> — <why it matters but isn't a hard gate>\n  *(empty section: write the single word `None` on its own line — NOT a `- None` bullet; a leading `- ` would be counted as a blocking item)*\n\n## Nit\n- <style/clarity issue>\n  *(empty section: write the single word `None` on its own line — NOT a `- None` bullet; a leading `- ` would be counted as a blocking item)*\n\nClass rule (Must-fix and Should-fix): **every bullet carries a `class:` continuation line**, `build` or\n`measurement`, under the bullet and never as a `- ` bullet of its own (a leading `- ` is a second\n2860→finding). The test is one question: **would the code or tests a 5d/5e implementer writes differ if this finding were fixed?** Yes → `class: build` (an interface, a contract, a\npredicate, a fixture, a test's assertion or RED/GREEN count, a false premise about the platform). No →\n`class: measurement` (a published number, stamp, ledger row, sha series, pointer or self-count that is\nstale or mis-derived; the tree is unchanged by fixing it). An untagged bullet is scored as `build`, so\nomitting the line never softens a finding — it only costs the orchestrator the triage. Put the `class:` line directly under its bullet (before or after `quote:`); do not indent a sub-bullet between a finding and its `class:` line, which leaves the finding untagged.\n\n    - <issue> — <why>\n      class: build\n      quote: <path/to/file.md> › `<span copied verbatim from that file>`\n\n2870→Quoting rule (Must-fix and Should-fix): **if a finding asserts what a document says, prove it.**\nAdd an indented `quote:` line under that bullet naming the file and carrying the span copied\nverbatim out of it:\n\n    - <issue> — <why it breaks the invariant>\n      quote: <path/to/file.md> › `<span copied verbatim from that file>`\n\nThe `quote:` line is a continuation of its bullet, never a new `- ` bullet — a leading `- ` would\nbe scored as a second finding. Use one `quote:` line per document the finding names.\n\n2880→This exists because backticks alone are ambiguous. Reviewers correctly backtick three other kinds\nof span — code they are **proposing** as the fix, test inputs they **constructed**, and commands\nthey **ran** — and those legitimately do not occur in the document. Measured over 37 must-fixes:\n43% of *real* findings carry a backticked span absent from the document, so absence alone proves\nnothing and no automatic check can be built on it. `quote:` is the marker that separates a claim\nabout the document from everything else you are entitled to write in backticks.\n\nA finding that asserts document content with no `quote:` line is not rejected automatically, but it\nis the first thing the orchestrator re-derives from source — and a `quote:` span that does not occur\nin the file it names is a finding withdrawn, not a finding to argue."}}],"rawOutput":{"type":"ReadFile","FileContent":{"content":"1890→    consecutive `#` lines is seen only when the comment text is joined. So the sweep also reads\n    each `.py` file's comment tokens (`tokenize`, with the `#` stripped), joined and\n    whitespace-collapsed.\n  - **A file that `ast.parse` rejects.** It is never skipped silently. The sweep reports it by name\n    with a count (`unparsed=N`), its line-grep and comment readings still apply, and a nonzero\n    count means the file is read by hand before the sweep counts as clean.\n  - **Bytes literals** (`b\"…\"`) are not `str` constants. The line grep covers them on one line.\n    A bytes literal split across lines is the residual of this axis.\n\n  **The sweep is therefore the union** of the line grep over every tracked file and the joined\n1900→  reading (the `.py` `str` constants, the joined `.py` comment text, and the whitespace-collapsed\n  text of every other file). One measurement at `5f72c2f3` (h-mad last changed at `083a8233`):\n  joined reading 7 files, line grep 6 files, line grep minus joined reading 0 files, union\n  7 files; comment-token hits 0 files, joined-comment hits 0 files, `unparsed=0`. The comment\n  zero is incidental: no comment in `h-mad/` restates the rule today. It is not a property of the\n  sweep, and it does not license dropping the comment reading.\n\n  Residual: a later surface that restates the rule is not covered until this sweep is re-run. A\n  phrase built at run time (an f-string field, `%`/`.format` interpolation, or a `+` join of names\n  rather than literals) is not a `str` constant, so no reading in the sweep sees it.\n1910→  Such a surface is found only by reading the code that builds the dispatch text. A bytes literal\n  split across lines is the other residual, as stated above.\n\n- **The relaxation is shown to be exactly the intended case (differential corpus).** The carve-out\n  makes the 5d RED check accept a RED it used to halt, so `h-mad/invariants.base.md` §\"Guard\n  narrowing\" applies. The rule has no code implementation. At `e7730f78`,\n  `git ls-files h-mad | grep -v '\\.md$' | xargs grep -ln 'red_wrong_reason\\|missing.symbol'`\n  hits only two test files (`test_h_mad_wire_pin_gate.py` and `test_h_mad_wiring_task_shape_docs.py`),\n  and no script. So the corpus runs over **RED evidence**, not over a function. Each case is\n  a fixture repository with a RED base, a WIRE-PIN test and the real pytest RED it produces. The\n1920→  four kinds of missing symbol are:\n\n  | case | missing symbol in the RED | callee at the RED base | old verdict | new verdict |\n  |---|---|---|---|---|\n  | `new-caller` | the caller the task creates | resolves | halt `red_wrong_reason` | **valid** |\n  | `missing-callee` | the callee | absent | halt | halt |\n  | `both-missing` | the caller the task creates (the callee is also absent) | absent | halt | halt |\n  | `unrelated-import` | a symbol the task neither creates nor calls (for example an unrelated module failing to import) | resolves | halt | halt |\n\n  - For each case the probe records the RED's exception line, whether the callee resolves at\n1930→    the RED base (shown by importing it at that commit, not by reading the source), and the\n    verdict under the old rule text and under the new one. The design names how a verdict is\n    derived from rule text: either a classifier function that both texts' prose describes, or a\n    stated derivation per case.\n  - **Pass:** the set of cases whose verdict changed is exactly `{new-caller}`. Any other member\n    is a widening, and it is fixed in the rule text, never in the table.\n  - The four rows are one per kind of missing symbol on the RED. The residual axis is the\n    exception type: the corpus produces each case's RED as the exception the fixture actually\n    raises, and a case whose RED is `NameError` is a fifth fixture only when the new rule's text\n    treats `NameError` differently from `ImportError`/`AttributeError`.\n1940→- **Incident replay against the real artifacts.** The carve-out was motivated by a real incident,\n  so `h-mad/invariants.base.md` §\"Incident replay\" applies, and the synthetic corpus above does not\n  replace the replay. The artifacts are in the HemaSuite checkout (`/Users/kimhawk/orca/HemaSuite`):\n  - `docs/archive/2026-10/notebooklm-integration-interface/notebooklm-integration-interface.analysis.md`,\n    item **F-3 — WIRE-PIN RED on a new caller (T4/T5/T6 WIRE 2)**. It names the cases and their\n    wire-cut evidence: M4.1, M5.1 and the WIRE 2 inline revert;\n  - the same directory's `notebooklm-integration-interface.impl-plan.md`, whose WIRE-PIN table\n    maps each case to its pin and mutation row;\n  - the three pins, each in `hematology-paper-writer/tests/` at its task commit:\n\n1950→    | case | pin (test file `::` test) | task commit (subject locator) |\n    |---|---|---|\n    | T4 WIRE-PIN 1 | `test_notebooklm_resolver.py::test_resolver_calls_registry_arm_notebook_id_once` | `531a6296b` (`git log --all --oneline --grep='Task 4 FR-1 resolver'`) |\n    | T5 WIRE-PIN 1 | `test_notebooklm_initialize.py::test_initialize_entry_point_resolves_through_registry_arm` | `7aa0d9dec` (`--grep='Task 5 FR-5 initialize'`) |\n    | T6 WIRE-PIN 2 | `test_notebooklm_status_report.py::test_status_entry_point_resolves_through_registry_arm` | `c2d671f6b` (`--grep='Task 6 CLI surface'`) |\n\n    Each task commit's parent is its RED base. The pin names come from the impl-plan's\n    `**WIRE-PIN N**:` lines, and each file was checked with `git show --stat <commit>`.\n\n  **Which form of the pin the replay evaluates.** The committed pins do not share one form:\n1960→  - T4 looks the new caller up with two-argument `getattr(integration, \"resolve_registry_notebook\")`.\n    At the RED base this raises `AttributeError`, so the committed pin's RED is a missing symbol.\n  - T5 and T6 use the impl-plan's \"WIRE-PIN RED scaffold\". It is a three-argument\n    `getattr(..., None)` (T5: `getattr(integration, \"initialize_project_notebook\", None)`; T6:\n    `getattr(integration.NotebookLMIntegration(), \"generate_setup_report\", None)`) inside\n    `except (Exception, SystemExit)`, followed by an assertion on the spy. Their committed RED is\n    an `AssertionError`, which the old rule already accepts.\n\n  So for T5 and T6 the replay evaluates the **direct-call form** of the pin: the scaffold is\n  replaced by a two-argument `getattr` on the same name (or a direct call), the `try`/`except` is\n1970→  removed, and the spy assertion is unchanged. That is the form whose RED is the missing caller,\n  and it is the form the carve-out is about. The replay also records each committed pin's own RED,\n  for the record. For T4 the committed pin is already in the direct-call form.\n\n  For each of the three cases, the replay:\n  1. reproduces the direct-call form's RED at the RED base, with the pin from the task commit\n     (rewritten as above for T5 and T6). The RED must be `AttributeError`/`ImportError` on the\n     caller, and the callee must resolve at the RED base (shown by import);\n  2. applies the new rule to that RED and records its verdict, which must be **valid**;\n  3. records the old rule's verdict on that RED, which must be halt. For T5 and T6 it also records\n1980→     the committed scaffolded pin's RED (`AssertionError`) and the old verdict on it, which is\n     **valid**: the scaffold is how those tasks avoided the halt;\n  4. re-executes the wire-cut at the task commit with the committed pin: delete the call only, keep\n     the caller and the callee, and confirm the pin fails. It also locates the original evidence\n     (M4.1, M5.1, the WIRE 2 revert) and cites it beside the re-run.\n\n  - The replay is a committed probe under `docs/03-analysis/probes/agy-coder-fallback/`, stamped\n    with this repository's sha and the HemaSuite sha it read.\n  - A case whose RED does not reproduce, or whose original evidence cannot be located, is recorded\n    with the command that failed. It is never dropped from the count.\n1990→  - **Residual.** The locators are commit subjects and archive paths. If HemaSuite rewrites\n    history or moves the archive, re-locate with `git log --all --grep='notebooklm-integration-interface'`.\n\n### FR-10 — Documentation surfaces\n\n- **SKILL.md** §\"Codex authors Phase 5 — enforced, not just instructed\" becomes implementer-neutral\n  and states FR-2/FR-3. §\"Exit-code dispatch for 5d/5e\" lists `exec agy` as an implementer\n  dispatch beside its review role.\n- **`h-mad/references/agy-runtime.md`** §\"The TDD gate\" names the new gate and FR-5's check, and keeps\n  the halt.\n2000→- **`h-mad/references/state-schema.md`** gains the FR-1 rows. Its existing `fallback_agent` and\n  `codex_status` rows are rewritten, and so are the schema JSON's `fallback_agent` and\n  `codex_status` `description`s, all four per FR-1.\n- **`h-mad/references/failure-recovery.md`** gets the 5d `red_wrong_reason` row carve-out per\n  FR-9.\n- **SKILL.md, the Phase-5 orchestrator steps:** the `implementation_authors` append on accepting\n  a 5e GREEN or fix dispatch, and the attribution read from it before each review leg, per FR-1\n  and FR-7 (D-15).\n- **SKILL.md, Phase 7 closure step:** the report carries every `WARNING` line that\n  `h_mad_phase7_preconditions.py` prints, the FR-7 warnings included.\n2010→- **SKILL.md §\"Teammate audit leg — when codex is unavailable\"**, the bullet that begins\n  `- **It does not license Claude to write Phase 5 production code`. Today it says \"but the\n  declaration you just made does\". Under FR-2 that is false when agy is available: the\n  declaration routes the audit leg, but it moves Phase-5 authorship to agy, and FR-3 refuses\n  Claude (`agy-authorship`). The rewrite keeps the anchor prefix, which\n  `test_h_mad_tdd_gate_docs.py` `_skill_gate_bullet` requires exactly once, and the tokens\n  `ACTIVE`, `every`, `unavailable` and `exhausted`, which\n  `test_skill_gate_bullet_states_the_every_record_rule` requires. It states two things separately:\n  - **Audit-leg selection.** `codex_status` alone routes the 5b audit to the teammate leg.\n  - **Resolved authorship.** Claude may write Phase-5 production code only when FR-2's `resolved`\n2020→    is `claude` for every ACTIVE `step5` record on the chain. That means both peers are\n    unavailable and `fallback_agent` is absent, `null` or `claude`.\n  The replaced condition is \"the `codex_status` declaration licenses Claude\". The new condition is\n  \"`resolved` = `claude`\", and the bullet names `h_mad_tdd_judge.py implementer` as the way to read\n  it.\n- **Audit legs in Phases 3/4/5b are unchanged.** `codex_status` still routes the second audit\n  surface to the teammate leg (SKILL.md §\"Teammate audit leg — when codex is unavailable\").\n  `agy_status` has no audit-routing consumer.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n2030→\n- **AC-1.1:** The schema accepts each FR-1 field at each listed value and rejects a value outside\n  it. A record with none of the fields validates unchanged. The `fallback_agent` description in\n  the schema JSON and its `state-schema.md` row state FR-2's both-peers condition. The\n  `codex_status` description and row no longer say that an unavailable Codex permits Claude to\n  author, and state authorship as FR-2's resolution. `test_h_mad_state_fallback_agent.py` stays\n  green. For `implementer_switch.ts`, \"the listed value\" is FR-1's narrowed profile, leap second\n  excluded (D-14); its fixtures are AC-1.2's.\n- **AC-1.2:** A record whose `implementer_switch.ts` is `not-a-date`, `2026-10-16`,\n  `2026-02-30T00:00:00Z` or the leap second `2016-12-31T23:59:60Z` (D-14) is refused on the library path and, under the test seam that forces it,\n2040→  on the bundled path. Each value is its own fixture, run once per path. A `.ts` in the writer's\n  form `%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ` is accepted on both paths. If the mechanism uses the library's format\n  checker, one fixture runs with `rfc3339_validator` made unimportable and still refuses\n  `not-a-date`.\n- **AC-1.3 (D-15):** `implementation_authors` is pinned on the library path and, under the same\n  test seam as AC-1.2, on the bundled path; each fixture runs once per path:\n  - accepted: `[]`; one entry per agent `codex`, `agy`, `grok`, `claude`, each its own fixture;\n    and `[{\"module\":\"m\",\"agent\":\"agy\"},{\"module\":\"m\",\"agent\":\"codex\"},{\"module\":\"m\",\"agent\":\"agy\"}]`\n    (duplicates and order kept);\n  - refused, each its own fixture: an unknown agent (`\"agent\":\"gemini\"`), a missing `agent`, a\n2050→    missing `module`, `\"module\":\"\"`, an extra key, a non-object entry, and a non-array value;\n  - a record with no `implementation_authors` validates unchanged.\n  An append of a refused entry through the Record mechanism leaves the store byte-identical.\n- **AC-2.1:** The resolution function: for every combination of {`implementer` absent / `codex` /\n  `agy` / invalid} × {`codex_avail`, `agy_avail` ∈ true/false}, `resolved` and `switch` match FR-2.\n  Each switch reason is produced by its own fixture, with only that cause present.\n- **AC-2.2:** The `implementer` verb prints exactly one line for each of the six token rows, and\n  for `record=failed`. `--record` writes `implementer_switch` once and does not rewrite it on an\n  identical re-run.\n- **AC-3.1:** Each of the five gate-specific refusal kinds is produced by its own fixture. In\n2060→  addition, `implementer-invalid` outranks `codex-authorship`, a peer refusal outranks\n  `fallback-invalid`, and the chain fold blocks on the FIRST eligible record.\n- **AC-3.2:** The `codex-authorship`, `fallback-grok` and `fallback-invalid` message bytes equal\n  those at `a8d9ba99`. Every existing `test_h_mad_tdd_gate_*.py` stays green, and the only change\n  allowed is FR-3's stub-line exception. In the two files FR-3 names, a string literal that stubs\n  a `TDD-STATE: active` line, and the constants it is composed from, are re-shaped to the new\n  line. Every assertion, expected kind, reason substring, test name and parametrisation id stays\n  byte-identical (checked by diffing the file against `a8d9ba99`). Every other\n  `test_h_mad_tdd_gate_*.py` is unmodified. A new fixture shows that a line in today's shape now\n  refuses `judge-error`. In `test_h_mad_tdd_judge.py`, `ACTIVE_RE` is the only changed literal, and\n2070→  the real-line assertions FR-3 lists stay byte-identical and green (checked by the same diff).\n- **AC-4.1:** With the scan reading `inactive`, the agy gate allows every tool, except that a\n  class-(b) production `.py` target whose chain reads `unreadable` still denies (the `chmod 000`\n  `docs/` chain fixture). With it `active`, it covers:\n  - each class with at least one member;\n  - an unknown tool name → class (d) deny;\n  - a class-(b) payload with no path key → deny, and the same with the key's value `\"\"` → deny;\n  - a production `.py` without a failing test → deny, with a judge kind;\n  - with a failing test → allow;\n  - `/tmp` and `artifactDirectoryPath` non-`.py` → allow, and `.py` there → deny;\n2080→  - a class-(b) target naming an existing directory (`.` and `src/`) → deny `kind=judge-error`,\n    and the same target with the scan reading `inactive` → allow (D-16).\n  With the scan reading `unknown` (a `docs/.bkit-memory.json` that is not JSON), each deny above\n  that does not need the judge still fires (class (d), no path key, `\"\"`, out-of-root `.py`, a\n  directory target, a `run_command` FR-6 refuses, and the `workspacePaths` cross-check), each from its own fixture.\n  The **target differential** runs one corpus of target shapes through both gates and shows they\n  agree on every shape except those FR-4's §\"Deliberate differences\" names, where the agy gate\n  refuses.\n- **AC-4.2:** Every exit path prints exactly one JSON object: malformed stdin, an internal\n  exception and an unreadable state each still yield parseable stdout. Every deny path the design\n2090→  enumerates is driven by its own fixture, and each fixture asserts that `reason` ends with\n  exactly one `kind=<kind>` whose value is in the design's closed list (FR-4 §\"Every deny carries\n  a kind\"); a deny path with no fixture, or a kind outside the list, fails the AC.\n- **AC-5.1:** The wiring check emits each `UNWIRED` reason from its own fixture. `--agent agy`\n  writes no prompt and halts on each of them.\n- **AC-5.2:** Each row of FR-5's exit-code table is pinned by its own fixture, and each fixture\n  asserts the stdout token and the exit code separately:\n  - an `UNWIRED` fixture (one per reason, reusing AC-5.1's) shows check exit 0, and assembler\n    `ASSEMBLE-TDD: HALT agy_tdd_hook_unverified` with exit 0;\n  - a `hooks.json` holding invalid JSON shows check `UNREADABLE` with exit 2, and the assembler\n2100→    HALT with exit 2;\n  - a check stubbed to print no token shows the assembler `HALT agy_tdd_hook_unverified check=no-token`\n    with exit 2;\n  - `WIRED` shows exit 0 on both;\n  - the agy gate's `--self-check` with a verdict `FAIL` forced prints that `FAIL` token on stdout\n    with exit 0, and the wiring check reads it as `UNWIRED reason=self-check-failed`.\n\n  A mutation that returns 2 on `UNWIRED` is killed, and so is one that returns 2 on a self-check\n  verdict `FAIL`.\n- **AC-6.1:** The shell policy is imported, not copied: one definition in the tree. The Codex gate's\n2110→  existing shell tests pass unmodified. Every mutation row re-pointed under FR-6 runs\n  `MUTATION: ALL_CAUGHT` against the shared module, `h_mad_target_identity.py` (D-12). The agy gate refuses each `unsafe` command in the Codex\n  gate's `--self-check` list.\n- **AC-7.1:** `h_mad_phase7_preconditions.py` emits each of the three FR-7 warnings from its own\n  fixture, and none of them moves `ready`. Adding a `review_independence` entry to a record that\n  already holds two leaves both earlier entries byte-identical. An entry that fails FR-1 is\n  refused, and the store is unchanged.\n- **AC-7.2:** `review_independence_unrecorded` fires, with no `leg=6a-prime` entry, for each\n  implementer `codex` (field absent included), `agy`, `grok` and `claude`, each from its own\n  fixture (`grok` and `claude` through `implementer_switch.to`, the only FR-1 routing field that takes them; `implementation_authors` is an authorship record and does not trigger the warning), and for each `archreview` value `READY_TO_MERGE`, `WITH_FIXES` and `NO`. It does not fire\n2120→  when a `leg=6a-prime` entry exists, nor when `archreview` is absent, `SKIPPED_NO_PANE` or\n  `SKIPPED_OPERATOR_OVERRIDE`.\n- **AC-7.3 (v1.8):** FR-7 §\"Attribution\" is pinned by fixtures, each RED against the mutant it\n  names and GREEN on the rule:\n  - **routing is not authorship:** `implementer_switch.to=agy` stored, the module's code from a\n    Codex dispatch, a Codex reviewer → `implementer=codex`, `independent=false`, and\n    `review_non_independent` fires. Mutant: attribution read from `implementer_switch.to`.\n  - **multi-author, recovered (E2):** authors agy then Codex; a grok 5e-review →\n    `implementer=codex`, `independent=true`; a Codex 5e-verify → `implementer=codex`,\n    `independent=false`; then, with the dispatch context discarded, an agy 6a-prime →\n2130→    `implementer=agy`, `independent=false`. Mutant: authors recovered from earlier entries'\n    `implementer` values only. The recovered set's source is `implementation_authors` (FR-1,\n    D-15), holding `{m, agy}` then `{m, codex}`.\n  - **pre-field feature (D-15):** the same reviews on a record with no `implementation_authors`\n    → the 6a-prime leg writes no entry for the module. Mutant: an absent field read as an empty\n    author set, so the agy reviewer reads as independent.\n  - **RED is not authorship (E4):** an agy 5d RED dispatch then a Codex 5e GREEN; an agy reviewer →\n    `independent=true`, `implementer=codex`. Mutant: a 5d RED dispatch counted as an author.\n  - **unaccepted dispatches author nothing:** a rejected agy GREEN then an accepted Codex GREEN;\n    an agy reviewer → `independent=true`.\n2140→  If the design leaves attribution as orchestrator prose with no code to drive, this AC has no\n  surface to run on; that is owed to the impl-plan as an OPEN-DECISION, not waived.\n- **AC-8.1:** The probe directory holds the payload capture, the real refusal, and the RED/GREEN\n  readings with re-run, revert and mutation results, each stamped with the sha and the\n  `agy --version`. The default timeout in `h_mad_assemble_tdd.py` equals FR-8's formula over\n  those readings. The readings come from FR-8's post-merge stage (D-13), and the feature is not\n  recorded COMPLETE before AC-8.1 and AC-8.2 pass.\n- **AC-8.2:** The probe directory holds the forced-handler-timeout reading from FR-8 step 2a,\n  stamped with the sha, `agy --version` and the T it used. The reading has three parts: whether\n  the file exists, the wall time and agy's message. It names exactly one outcome from step 2a's\n2150→  table, and that outcome is consistent with the three readings. It also shows that the delay\n  hook was removed and that `.agents/hooks.json` matches its pre-probe bytes. A `session-error`\n  outcome does not satisfy this AC. If the outcome is `timeout-allows`, `agy-runtime.md` §\"The TDD\n  gate\" carries the fail-open residual, and the operator question is raised before the gate is\n  called verified.\n- **AC-9.1:** The prompt contains no implementer-specific opening. `test_prompt_file_is_agent_independent`\n  covers `codex`, `grok` and `agy`. The new-caller rule appears in RED item 1, in the WIRE-PIN\n  paragraph, in the GREEN variant, in the SKILL.md 5d halt text, in the `failure-recovery.md`\n  `step5d:red_wrong_reason:<module>` row and in the wiring-task header that `h_mad_assemble_tdd.py`\n  emits. `test_h_mad_wiring_task_shape_docs.py`, `test_h_mad_agent_definitions.py` and\n2160→  `test_h_mad_assemble_tdd.py` stay green. FR-9's sweep, re-run on the shipped tree, hits no file\n  that states the rule without the carve-out. The sweep is the union FR-9 defines: the line grep\n  over every tracked file and the joined reading, which takes in `.py` comment text. The re-run\n  reports `unparsed=N`. A nonzero `N` does not satisfy this AC until each unparsed file has been\n  read by hand.\n- **AC-9.2:** The FR-9 differential corpus runs all four cases (`new-caller`, `missing-callee`,\n  `both-missing`, `unrelated-import`) as fixture repositories with real pytest REDs. For each case\n  it records the RED's exception line, whether the callee resolves at the RED base (by import) and\n  both verdicts. The set of cases whose verdict changed is exactly `{new-caller}`.\n- **AC-9.3:** The FR-9 incident replay covers HemaSuite `notebooklm-integration-interface` T4\n2170→  WIRE-PIN 1 (`test_notebooklm_resolver.py` at `531a6296b`), T5 WIRE-PIN 1\n  (`test_notebooklm_initialize.py` at `7aa0d9dec`) and T6 WIRE-PIN 2\n  (`test_notebooklm_status_report.py` at `c2d671f6b`). For each case the committed probe holds:\n  - the direct-call form's RED reproduced at the task commit's parent: `AttributeError`/\n    `ImportError` on the caller, with the callee resolving by import. For T4 this is the committed\n    pin; for T5 and T6 it is the pin with the scaffold replaced, per FR-9;\n  - on that RED, the new rule's verdict **valid** and the old rule's verdict halt;\n  - for T5 and T6 only, the committed scaffolded pin's RED (`AssertionError`) and the old rule's\n    verdict on it, which is valid;\n  - a wire-cut re-run at the task commit in which the committed pin fails;\n2180→  - a citation of the original evidence (M4.1, M5.1, WIRE 2 revert).\n\n  It is stamped with both repositories' shas. A case that cannot be reproduced is recorded with\n  its failing command and counted. It does not satisfy the AC.\n- **AC-9.4:** The six copies of the new-caller rule (FR-9 surfaces 1–5 and the GREEN variant) are\n  bound to one authority. Either every surface is generated from or checked against one shared\n  source, or one test asserts that the carve-out text is equal across all six copies. The plan\n  names which mechanism. Whichever it is:\n  - **The carve-out text is the whole quoted block in FR-9**, all three sentences: the validity\n    condition, \"The RED report must name both.\", and the wire-cut obligation. A check that binds\n2190→    only the first sentence does not meet this AC.\n  - **Each copy is read from its own source location.** The span read for one copy contains no\n    other copy. Three readings do not meet this AC, because a healthy copy in the same span\n    masks a sick one: a contains-check over the whole assembled prompt (the template's copies\n    1, 2 and 6 are filled into it beside the header copy); a file-level contains-check over\n    `codex-implementer-prompt.md`, which holds three copies; and a file-level check over any\n    file that holds more than one copy. The design names each copy's locator. A locator states\n    what it does not pin, and the test fails, rather than passing, when a locator matches\n    nothing or more than one span.\n  - **The control is a mutation of exactly one copy, and it makes the check fail.** It is run\n2200→    once per copy, so six times, and once per sentence of the block in at least one copy. Each\n    mutation row's `find` is unique in its file, because `h_mad_mutation_harness.py`\n    `anchor_status` refuses a `find` that does not occur exactly once. A `find` therefore carries\n    copy-specific context, and the `.py` copy's `find` is written against the source bytes,\n    including the adjacent-literal split. A row the harness refuses is not a control.\n- **AC-X:** Every new guard carries a mutation row that bites (`MUTATION: ALL_CAUGHT` with a `test`\n  per row). The full suite passes per task.\n\n## Decisions on the brainstorm's open questions\n\n2210→- **D-1 `run_command`:** command-shape rule shared with Codex (FR-6). Rationale: the tree shows\n  that is what Codex parity is.\n- **D-2 Hook scope:** per project (`.agents/hooks.json`). Rationale:\n  - The Codex gate is wired per project (HemaSuite `.codex/hooks.json`; this repository has no\n    `.codex/`).\n  - A tracked file reaches every worktree.\n  - The handler cwd gives the root without trusting a payload field.\n  - No global hook fires in every agy session on the machine.\n\n  Residual: a project without the file has no agy gate. FR-5 turns that into a halt, not a silent\n2220→  pass.\n- **D-3 Payload cwd:** the root comes from the handler cwd (FR-4), with `workspacePaths` as a\n  cross-check.\n- **D-4 Timeout:** provisional 1500 s, then FR-8's formula. The hook-handler timeout is a separate\n  figure, derived from `JUDGE_BUDGET_S`.\n- **D-5 Availability:** CLI on PATH + `agy_status` + `HMAD_AGY_UNAVAILABLE`, symmetric with Codex.\n  No liveness probe: the gate runs on every write and must be offline and fast, and a probe spends\n  quota. An exhausted agy shows up when the dispatch fails, and the orchestrator records it with\n  `--set agy_status=exhausted`.\n- **D-6 Project default:** feature record only. A second source doubles the surface the gate\n2230→  resolves, and per-feature is the operator decision. A later default can seed the same field at\n  `--create`.\n- **D-7 Flag location:** a state field (FR-7). It is not on the extracted verdict, because that\n  line is `<KEY>: <VALUE>` and its consumers parse it.\n- **D-8 Audit legs:** unchanged (FR-10).\n- **D-9 Codex reviewing Codex (was OQ-1; operator decision, 2026-10-16):** yes. FR-7's flag fires\n  for any `reviewer == implementer`, Codex included, for example the 5e anti-gaming verifier\n  dispatched to Codex on a Codex-implemented feature. It is a warning only, never a block.\n  Consequence: Codex-implemented features can now carry a `review_non_independent` Phase-7\n  warning that has never appeared before.\n2240→- **D-10 `.agents/` exclusion (was OQ-2; operator decision, 2026-10-16):** narrow it. Applied in this\n  clone by the orchestrator on the operator's answer: the `.git/info/exclude` entry `/.agents/` is now `/.agents/skills/`.\n  Evidence at `d65374e9`: `git check-ignore -v .agents/hooks.json` prints nothing (exit 1, not\n  ignored), and `git check-ignore -v .agents/skills` still reports the `/.agents/skills/` entry.\n  D-2's tracked `.agents/hooks.json` therefore needs no `git add -f`.\n  - **Residual / prerequisite.** `.git/info/exclude` is per-clone local configuration and is not\n    versioned. Every other clone of this repository, and every other project that adopts the agy\n    gate, needs the same narrowing (or a `git add -f`) before its `.agents/hooks.json` can be\n    tracked. Linked worktrees share the main clone's `info/exclude` (git's common dir), so they\n    inherit the narrowing from the clone they belong to. Where the file is not tracked, FR-5's\n2250→    check reads `UNWIRED reason=no-hooks-file` in any worktree that lacks it, so the gap is a halt,\n    not a silent pass.\n- **D-11 `review_independence_unrecorded` for every implementer (operator decision, 2026-10-16):**\n  widened from agy-only to `codex|agy|grok|claude`, matching D-9 (FR-7). Warning only, never a\n  block. Rationale: it fires only when the recording step was skipped, so a feature that follows\n  the new flow stays quiet; without it, a missing record would hide a Codex-on-Codex self-review,\n  which D-9 now flags when it IS recorded.\n  - **Pre-existing features — settled, no grandfather rule.** The check applies to every feature\n    whose Phase-7 check runs after this ships. Archived features are untouched because nothing\n    re-runs the check over them (FR-7). A grandfather rule would need a signal separating \"began\n2260→    before this shipped\" from \"skipped the recording step\", and the only signal the record offers\n    — absence of `review_independence` — is the skip itself, so any such rule would also silence\n    the case the warning exists for. A warning on an in-flight feature costs one backfilled entry\n    or one report line.\n- **D-12 Shared shell-policy module (was the design's OQ-A; operator decision, 2026-10-16):** the\n  FR-6 policy moves into the existing `h-mad/scripts/h_mad_target_identity.py`, not a new module.\n  The design's reason: `test_h_mad_codex_tdd_gate_judge.py` `test_codex_gate_judge_timeout_reason`\n  builds a tree by copying only the Codex gate, a judge stub and `h_mad_target_identity.py`\n  (`grep -n -A12 'def test_codex_gate_judge_timeout_reason' h-mad/tests/test_h_mad_codex_tdd_gate_judge.py`),\n  so a new module would be missing there, and FR-6 forbids editing that test.\n2270→- **D-13 FR-8 timing (was the design's OQ-B; operator decision, 2026-10-16):** FR-8 runs as a\n  post-merge stage after Phase 7's integrate, and the feature is recorded COMPLETE only after it.\n  Reason: the tracer must run the gate at its installed path, which is main.\n- **D-14 Leap-second `.ts` refused (orchestrator decision, design audit cycle 1, 2026-10-16):**\n  FR-1 narrows `implementer_switch.ts` to seconds `00`–`59`. Reason: state timestamps are written\n  by h-mad's own writer through Python `datetime`, which cannot produce a seconds field of `60`.\n- **D-15 Durable author record (OD-E2, operator decision, 2026-10-16: option (a)):** FR-1 gains\n  `implementation_authors`, an optional array of `{module, agent}` entries appended (never\n  removed or altered) through FR-7's Record mechanism, one per accepted 5e GREEN or accepted fix\n  dispatch and never for a 5d RED. FR-7 §\"Attribution\" reads a module's author set from it.\n2280→  Reason: design v1.2 (OD-E2) showed AC-7.3's \"multi-author, recovered\" fixture could not pass\n  without a durable author record, because the assembler's prompt, out and log files are under\n  `/tmp` and its slug names no agent. The fixture stays as written and now has its source. The\n  rejected option (b) was to amend the fixture to \"no entry\".\n- **D-16 Directory target refused under active step5 (OD-2, operator decision, 2026-10-16):** a\n  class-(b) target that names an existing directory is refused with `kind=judge-error` when the\n  scan reads `active` or `unknown`, and allowed when it reads `inactive` (FR-4 deliberate\n  difference 6). Reason: impl-plan v1.1 Task 6 showed that `canonicalise` returns no name and\n  no `unresolvable` flag for `.`, `src/`, `src` and the root, so the class-(b) sequence would\n  otherwise allow a directory target under `active`. Residual: a trailing-`/` path that names no\n2290→  existing directory is not covered and is judged as a file name.\n\n## Open questions for the operator\n\nNone — OQ-1/OQ-2 settled 2026-10-16; the design's OQ-A/OQ-B settled 2026-10-16 (D-12, D-13);\nthe design's OD-E2 settled 2026-10-16 (D-15); the impl-plan's OD-2 settled 2026-10-16 (D-16).\n\n## Non-goals\n\n- Per-task implementer selection. It can come later on the same field.\n2300→- Refusing a peer's production write in that peer's own gate when the other peer is the resolved\n  implementer, for example a Codex verifier dispatch writing production code under\n  `implementer=agy`. Residual: the test-first judge still applies, and the per-dispatch\n  `git status --short` diff (agy-runtime.md §\"Author and reviewer roles\") is what shows it.\n- A write-time gate for grok, and authorship rules for agy as the orchestrating host.\n- Routing audit legs by `agy_status`.\n\n## Owed by other documents\n\nNone of these are edited by this spec.\n2310→- `h-mad/references/agy-runtime.md` says there is no `SessionStart` on two surfaces:\n  - §\"Hooks\" (\"There is **no `SessionStart`**\");\n  - the §\"Construct mapping\" table's `hook-event` row (\"there is no `SessionStart`\").\n\n  agy's current hooks.md lists `SessionStart` as a supported event. Both surfaces need a re-check\n  against the installed agy (`agy --version` prints 1.4.0 at `200ffd52`), they are corrected\n  together, and the correction stamps the version it was checked against.\n  (`grep -n 'SessionStart' h-mad/references/agy-runtime.md` at `200ffd52`: 2 matching lines, one\n  under each heading.) Residual: this is a content predicate, so a third restatement added later\n  is found only by re-running the grep.\n2320→- **Design (v1.8 items).** FR-4: enumerate the closed kind list and give every deny path a member\n  (E6); its claim that `unknown` \"denies `kind=judge-error`, as the Codex gate does for a shell\n  command\" is false at `cff71b49` — the Codex gate's `unknown` shell deny carries no `kind=`\n  token. FR-7: name where the complete author set is kept (E2) and correct \"the GREEN dispatch the\n  orchestrator accepted at 5d\" to 5e (E4).\n- **Design (v1.9, D-15).** FR-1: add `implementation_authors` with FR-1's entry schema to the\n  schema JSON and a `state-schema.md` row. FR-7: retire \"the complete set is kept in the\n  procedure\" and OD-E2 in favour of reading the set from `implementation_authors`; the\n  `--append` mechanism (DO-3) must accept this key with the same guarantees, and the \"When the\n  dispatch list is lost\" rule becomes FR-7's three closed unrecoverable cases.\n2330→- **Design (spec v1.10).** FR-4: record D-16 as a directory check before the target is judged,\n  and add it to §\"Deliberate differences\" as item 6; FR-4 `--self-check`: a verdict `FAIL` exits\n  0 (replacing \"a nonzero exit\"), and the Codex gate's `FAIL shell-policy` is corrected in impl-plan\n  Task 4; FR-2: adopt the halts `step5:implementer_invalid` and `step5:implementer_unresolved`.\n- **Impl-plan.** AC-7.3's surface (FR-7 §\"Attribution\" must have code to drive, or the AC is an\n  OPEN-DECISION), now including the pre-field fixture; AC-1.3's fixtures; AC-4.1's `unknown`\n  fixtures and AC-4.2's per-deny-path kind fixtures.\n- **Plan.** If the plan carries FR-1's field list or AC-7.3's fixtures, it gains\n  `implementation_authors`, AC-1.3 and the pre-field fixture.\n\n2340→## Version History\n- v1.0: Initial spec (2026-10-16) from brainstorm v1.1. Settles 8 open questions (D-1..D-8): run_command takes the Codex command-shape rule, not a stated residual; per-project .agents/hooks.json; root from handler cwd; provisional 1500 s timeout then tracer formula; PATH+status+env availability; feature-only implementer; flag in state; audit legs unchanged. Folds brief fix 3 (#8) WIRE-PIN new-caller RED case as FR-9. OQ-1 (Codex-reviews-Codex flag), OQ-2 (.agents exclude) for the operator.\n- v1.1: Operator decisions 2026-10-16 (2026-10-16). OQ-1 -> D-9: FR-7 flag fires for any reviewer == implementer, Codex reviewing Codex included; warning only. OQ-2 -> D-10: operator narrowed .git/info/exclude /.agents/ to /.agents/skills/ (check-ignore evidence at d65374e9), no git add -f; per-clone residual stated. Open questions section now empty.\n- v1.2: Operator decision 2026-10-16 -> D-11: FR-7 review_independence_unrecorded widened from agy-only to every implementer (codex|agy|grok|claude), matching D-9; warning only. Trigger pinned to a 6a-prime leg that ran (archreview READY_TO_MERGE|WITH_FIXES|NO) with no leg=6a-prime entry; other archreview values already surface. Pre-existing features settled: no grandfather rule, archived features untouched (no batch caller). AC-7.2 added.\n- v1.3: Plan-author findings against v1.2 (2026-10-16). FR-3: a missing state-line field refuses judge-error (tree precedent: missing-field fixture), so the two gate stub-line fixtures are re-shaped with assertions byte-identical — the AC-3.2 exception; test_h_mad_tdd_judge.py ACTIVE_RE and line-quoting mutation rows follow the line. FR-7: writer has no append (--set replaces); add-without-loss is a requirement, mechanism a design obligation; AC-7.1 gains the two-entry fixture. FR-6: rows re-pointed to the shared module, then ALL_CAUGHT; moved names stay bound on the Codex gate module. FR-9/AC-9.1/FR-10: failure-recovery.md red_wrong_reason row; rule surfaces closed with sweep. FR-1/FR-10/AC-1.1: fallback_agent description on schema JSON and state-schema.md, pinned literals named. Owed: second SessionStart surface (Construct-mapping hook-event row). All ACs reformatted to the h_mad_ac_census.py grammar (- **AC-n.m:**).\n- v1.4: Plan audit cycle 1 findings owned by the spec (2026-10-16). F1 -> FR-5 exit-code table: AGY-GATE WIRED/UNWIRED (all seven reasons, no-hooks-file included) exit 0 and the assembler's agy_tdd_hook_unverified verdict HALT exits 0; UNREADABLE and a no-token check exit 2; existing assembler HALTs keep exit 2 (residual); AC-5.2. F2 -> FR-1: .ts is RFC 3339 date-time; neither validator path enforces format today (probed), so refusal on both paths is the requirement and the mechanism a design obligation; rfc3339-validator hazard; residual for started_ts/owner_heartbeat_ts/halt_ts; AC-1.2. F3 -> FR-9 differential corpus over four missing-symbol cases, changed set == {new-caller}; AC-9.2. F4 -> FR-9 incident replay of HemaSuite notebooklm F-3 (T4/T5 WIRE-PIN 1, T6 WIRE-PIN 2) with wire-cut re-run; AC-9.3. F5 -> FR-8 step 2a forced handler timeout with four classified outcomes; step 2 now settles two unknowns; FR-5 re-run trigger includes it; AC-8.2. F6 -> no contradiction; FR-3 now lists the real-line assertions (two in test_h_mad_tdd_gate_fallback_agent.py, four tests in test_h_mad_tdd_judge.py) as placement constraints; AC-3.2 extended.\n- v1.5: Plan audit cycle 2 findings owned by the spec, final corrective revision after the round cap (2026-10-16). B -> FR-10 adds the SKILL.md teammate-audit bullet: audit-leg selection vs resolved authorship (Claude only when resolved=claude), anchor prefix and the four pinned tokens kept. C -> FR-9 surface census gains surface 5, the h_mad_assemble_tdd.py assemble() wiring header (split string literals), and its pin test_h_mad_assemble_tdd.py TestWiringShape.test_the_pin_is_told_what_its_red_must_be; the census method now joins Python adjacent literals via ast and collapses markdown whitespace, measured at 9c3b799d; residual: run-time-built strings. AC-9.1 lists the header. D -> FR-9 replay and AC-9.3 name all three pins with files and commits (531a6296b, 7aa0d9dec, c2d671f6b); T5/T6 replay evaluates the direct-call / two-argument getattr form; the committed scaffolded RED (AssertionError) is recorded as old-verdict valid, correcting the false old-verdict=halt claim. A -> new AC-9.4: the six rule copies bound to one authority, with a per-copy control.\n- v1.6: Advisory delta review of plan v1.3 (608e430), spec-owned parts (2026-10-16). Should-fix 3 -> FR-9: the joined reading (ast str constants) is blind to non-str text in a .py file; axis named with three members (comments, files ast.parse rejects, bytes literals). The sweep is now the union of the line grep and the joined reading, which adds joined .py comment tokens; parse failures are reported as unparsed=N and read by hand; residual: run-time-built strings and a bytes literal split across lines. Measured at 5f72c2f3: union 7 files, line-grep-minus-joined 0, comment hits 0 (incidental), unparsed=0. AC-9.1 re-run is the union and reports unparsed=N. Must-fix 1 (spec side) and Should-fix 4 -> AC-9.4: the carve-out text is the whole three-sentence FR-9 block; each copy is read from its own source location, never from the assembled prompt or a file-level contains-check over a file holding more than one copy; a locator matching zero or several spans fails the test; the control mutates exactly one copy, runs once per copy and once per sentence in at least one copy, with a find unique in its file (mutation harness anchor_status), the .py find written against source bytes including the literal split.\n- v1.7: Design audit cycle 1, spec-owned items (2026-10-16). S1 (codex C1) -> FR-1/AC-1.1/AC-1.2 + D-14: .ts narrowed to seconds 00-59, a leap second (2016-12-31T23:59:60Z) refused on both paths; reason: every .ts comes from Python datetime via the writer, which cannot hold second 60 (orchestrator decision). S2 (codex C6) -> FR-7 Attribution: an entry's implementer is the author of the reviewed code, from its successful implementation dispatch, never implementer_switch.to (historical) or the implementer field; multi-author code is non-independent if the reviewer is any author. S3 -> FR-1/FR-10/AC-1.1: codex_status schema description and state-schema.md row rewritten to FR-2 authorship (no test pins the text; the fallback_agent-after-codex_status order pin stated); FR-8 Version: 1.4.0 at 200ffd52, each reading stamps its version; Owed SessionStart re-check re-stamped 1.4.0 at 200ffd52; FR-6/AC-6.1 shared module = h_mad_target_identity.py (D-12, OQ-A); FR-8/AC-8.1 post-merge stage, COMPLETE only after it (D-13, OQ-B). Open questions: OQ-A/OQ-B settled.\n- v1.8: Design audit cycle 2, final corrective revision after the round cap, not re-audited (2026-10-16). S1/E6 -> FR-4 'Every deny carries a kind': rule over every deny path, closed list owned by the design, spec names only judge-error, tool-not-admitted and the judge's Verdict kind; Codex gate has 6 of 13 kindless _deny call sites at cff71b49. E1 -> FR-4 'The activity reading': three-valued scan (active/unknown/inactive), every step5-gated deny also fires on unknown, class-(b) production .py still read through read_chain under inactive; workspacePaths and Tool classes sentences re-worded. E5 -> FR-4 Target keys: a non-empty-string value counts as no path key. S4 -> FR-4 'Deliberate differences from the Codex gate': design's four adopted plus the kind rule as a fifth. S2/E2/E4 -> FR-7 Attribution sub-bullets (5e GREEN + accepted fixes, never 5d RED; complete author set, never a projection) and new AC-7.3. S5 -> adopted: reviewer none of the authors names the latest accepted dispatch's author. S3 -> leap-second ValueError quote drops the version-dependent tail. AC-4.1/AC-4.2 extended; FR-6 states the unknown-scan shell deny; Owed gains design and impl-plan items.\n2350→- v1.9: Operator decision OD-E2 (a) (2026-10-16) -> D-15: FR-1 gains implementation_authors, an optional array of {module, agent} (agent codex|agy|grok|claude; module non-empty string; no extra keys), appended never altered through FR-7's Record mechanism on accepting a 5e GREEN or fix dispatch, never 5d RED or a halted/timed-out/rejected dispatch; duplicates kept, array order = acceptance order. New AC-1.3 (valid entries accepted, unknown agent and other malformed shapes refused, both validator paths). FR-7 Attribution reads the author set from the field on every leg; unrecoverable cases closed to three (field absent: pre-field features; no entry for the module: a record-step defect; malformed) -> no entry. AC-7.3 multi-author fixture unchanged with its source named; pre-field fixture added. AC-7.2 'only FR-1 field' narrowed to routing field. FR-10 SKILL.md Phase-5 step bullet. Owed: design FR-1/FR-7 (retire procedure-kept set and OD-E2), impl-plan AC-1.3 + pre-field fixture, plan mirror.\n- v1.10: Owed lines from impl-plan v1.1 (Phase 5b), final corrective revision, not re-audited (2026-10-16). P1 -> D-16 (OD-2, operator): a class-(b) target naming an existing directory denies kind=judge-error under active/unknown, allowed under inactive; FR-4 deliberate difference 6, AC-4.1 fixture; residual: trailing-/ path naming no existing directory. P2 -> FR-2 halts named: step5:implementer_invalid (invalid), step5:implementer_unresolved (no-record, unreadable), step5:implementer_switch_unrecorded (record=failed); adopted from impl-plan Task 16; failure-recovery.md rows not added (residual). P3 -> FR-5: the gate's --self-check verdict FAIL prints its token on stdout and exits 0, operational error nonzero; Codex gate FAIL shell-policy corrected in impl-plan Task 4; Codex FAIL parser residual; AC-5.2 gains the self-check fixture and mutant. P4 -> FR-9 Hook line keeps Claude's h-mad-tdd-gate.sh (test_hook_line_names_both_gates). Owed: design FR-4/FR-2 lines.\n\n\nAudit rubric (TWO axes, both mandatory):\n\nAxis A — Generic adversarial:\n- Contradictions inside the doc\n- Gaps (missing error paths, untestable AC, unstated assumptions)\n- Weak claims (load-bearing decisions without justification)\n2360→- Scope creep (work outside the stated feature boundary)\n\nAxis B — Invariant compliance (any breach = must-fix, you cannot downgrade).\nTwo layers, both binding:\n\n### Base invariants (workflow-universal; non-overridable by any project file — but the operator `## Acknowledged-not-fixed` sidecar still applies to base findings):\n\n# H-MAD Base Invariants — Axis B (workflow-universal)\n\n> Shipped with the `/h-mad` skill. The orchestrator inlines this file verbatim into the\n2370→> `INLINE_BASE_INVARIANTS` slot (name written bare — a bracketed mention inside an inlined\n> file survives substitution and reaches the reviewer as an unfilled-looking placeholder)\n> of every plan / design / impl-plan audit, **before** the\n> per-project `<PROJECT_ROOT>/.h-mad/invariants.md` domain layer. These rules apply to every\n> project that uses `/h-mad` and are **non-overridable by any project file**: a project's\n> invariants file may add domain rules but may not downgrade or delete a base rule. The\n> operator `## Acknowledged-not-fixed` sidecar escape hatch still applies to base findings\n> (a base item listed under that section in a sidecar `.audit.v<N+1>.md` is a conscious,\n> audited deferral — it is excluded from the gate count exactly like any other layer's item).\n>\n2380→> Axis A (generic adversarial review) is covered by the audit-prompt template — not repeated here.\n\n## Audit-gate signal discipline\n- Any gate/check whose verdict the orchestrator consumes MUST communicate PASS/FAIL via an\n  explicit **stdout token** and **exit 0** on a normal verdict (PASS or FAIL). Using a non-zero\n  process exit to mean \"FAIL\" is a violation — a non-zero exit registers as a Claude Code tool\n  failure (`PostToolUseFailure`) and leaks into coexisting plugins' error handling. A non-zero\n  exit is permitted ONLY for genuine operational errors (missing/unreadable input).\n\n## Single-source contract\n2390→- A rule applied by more than one surface (e.g. an audit blocking-item count read by both the\n  orchestrator gate step and a precondition checker) MUST have exactly one authoritative\n  implementation that all surfaces call, OR a test asserting byte-equivalence across surfaces.\n  Independent re-implementations that can silently diverge are a violation.\n- A contract stated in a template (e.g. an empty-section sentinel) MUST match what the consuming\n  gate logic treats as that case. Template guidance and gate behavior disagreeing is a violation.\n\n## Standalone / no plugin dependency\n- The skill MUST NOT acquire a **runtime dependency** on any other plugin (OMC, bkit,\n  context-mode, etc.) or external skill (spec-kit, b-mad, pdca). Coexistence accommodations\n2400→  (sharing a state filename, conforming doc structure to an external validator's required\n  sections) are allowed; *requiring another plugin installed at runtime* is a violation.\n\n## No new external dependency\n- Scripts MUST use only Python stdlib plus tooling already depended upon by the skill (the\n  agent dispatch substrate (cmux or orca, via `hmad-dispatch`); `jq` where the existing hook\n  already uses it; `pytest`).\n  Introducing a new third-party package or new CLI is a violation.\n- **Dispatched agent CLIs are not script dependencies.** `codex`, `agy` and `grok` are optional\n  runtime *agents*: reached only through a `hmad-dispatch` verb (`exec`, `send`, …), never imported,\n2410→  required, or invoked by a script or test for its own work — tests reach them through stubs, and an\n  absent agent CLI is a dispatch-time condition the verb reports, never a failure of the skill.\n  The list is closed: adding an agent to it is an operator decision recorded here, not something a\n  document or a dispatch may assume. (`grok` added 2026-09-28, operator decision on\n  `grok-codex-fallback` design audit v2.)\n\n## Portable time bounds\n- A time-bounded command MUST NOT be written as `timeout <s> <cmd>` or `gtimeout <s> <cmd>`.\n  **Neither is a macOS system component**, so the form is unportable in both directions.\n  A stock box ships neither and the call fails at 127; a box carrying `brew install coreutils`\n2420→  runs it fine and the bound silently rests on a CLI h-mad cannot assume is there.\n  **What your box has is not an input to this rule.** h-mad already owns a time-bounder that\n  reachable wherever `hmad-dispatch` is, so the form is forbidden unconditionally — not because\n  of what happens downstream. (Reachable, not universal: where the wrapper itself cannot be\n  called the rule below still applies — halt, do not drop the bound.) Do not reason from a local probe to an exemption. For anything committed\n  or dispatched the form is additionally a new external CLI dependency (§\"No new external\n  dependency\"); a downstream 127 is how that eventually surfaces, never the reason.\n  Do not settle the question by probing: a local `command -v timeout` that succeeds is not\n  licence, it proves only that this box has coreutils, and it means the 127 that would have\n  exposed the improvisation never fires. Use `hmad-dispatch run --timeout <s> -- <cmd...>`,\n2430→  which exits 124 at the deadline.\n- Where a 127 does fire, its aftermath is worse than the 127 itself. Measured: the reflex after `timeout: command not found`\n  is to re-run the same command **unbounded** and call it \"checking directly\". That does not fail\n  at the deadline, it hangs the phase, and in every log h-mad reads a hang and slow work are the\n  same bytes. A plan, a design, or a prompt that leaves an agent no reachable time-bounder is a\n  violation; the correct behaviour when none exists is to **halt**, not to drop the bound.\n\n## Doc-template superset compliance\n- Generated phase documents whose type is validated by an external doc-structure validator\n  (e.g. bkit PDCA: `plan` → `docs/01-plan/...`, `design` → `docs/02-design/...`,\n2440→  `report` → `docs/04-report/...`) MUST be supersets that satisfy that validator's required\n  sections AND retain the existing h-mad sections. Dropping an h-mad section or failing the\n  validator is a violation.\n- The contract binds the **saved document**, not only the template it came from. Where such a\n  validator reclassifies a document by a substring of its *content* — bkit's `isPlanPlus` promotes\n  a plan to a larger required-section list on seeing `Plan-Plus`, `Plan Plus`, `plan-plus`,\n  `Brainstorming-Enhanced`, or `Intent Discovery` anywhere in the file — a generated document\n  carrying that substring is a violation even though the template is clean. Guard it mechanically\n  (`scripts/h_mad_doc_shape_check.py`); the literals are unremarkable prose, so an author cannot\n  be expected to avoid them from memory.\n2450→\n## Operator-override preservation\n- The `## Acknowledged-not-fixed` sidecar override mechanism MUST remain functional for all\n  layers (base + project). Any gate change that causes overridden items to still count as\n  blocking, or that ignores the sidecar, is a violation.\n\n## Backward compatibility\n- A change to the audit gate MUST preserve the PASS verdict for audit docs that passed before\n  the change. Flipping a historically-passing audit to FAIL (absent a real new blocking item)\n  is a violation.\n2460→\n## Marker discipline\n- Orchestrator phase transitions and halts MUST emit `[H-MAD]` log markers so a run is\n  diagnosable from logs alone. Silent state transitions are a violation.\n\n## Mutation verification\n- A step that mutates state (a git operation, a file write, a remote/CLI call) MUST verify the\n  mutation by **re-reading the resulting state**; an **exit code is not evidence that a mutation\n  occurred**. A plan, design, or implementation that treats a zero exit — or an echoed success\n  string such as `Sent N bytes` or `{\"ok\":true}` — as proof of the intended effect is a violation.\n2470→- The failure this blocks is silent and looks exactly like success: two `zsh` no-ops (backtick\n  execution inside `-m`, a leading-dash path) both exited 0 while doing nothing, and a dispatch\n  reported `Sent 7293 bytes` into a dead pane. Where a command reports on its own behaviour, the\n  check must read the *thing it was supposed to change*, from a separate call.\n- **When several mutations target the SAME line, vary one field and keep the anchor shared.** A\n  guard whose failure has separable parts — exit code, stream routing, message content — needs one\n  mutation per part, each replacing the same anchor. That shape is what proves a content assertion\n  load-bearing: the mutants that keep exit and stream and strip only the text are exactly the ones a\n  returncode-only test survives. Two discriminators worth writing into the spec: a\n  first-vs-last-occurrence mutant survives whenever the sought item is last in BOTH regions, so the\n2480→  discriminating fixture must put the decoys between the markers and leave the tail empty; and\n  `survived` has four distinct causes — missing guard, equivalent mutant, weak test, and a mutant\n  that never ran — which the verdict token collapses into one word. Diagnose which before acting.\n\n## Test discrimination\n- A test or guard MUST be **observed failing against the unfixed code** before it is trusted.\n  For a regression test, revert the fix and re-run; for a guard, stub it to its permissive value\n  and re-run the suite. **Zero failures is a finding, not a reassurance** — it means the check is\n  unenforced, not that the code is safe. Keeping a check that has never been seen to fail is a\n  violation.\n2490→- A check that cannot fail is decoration, and it is worse than no check because it reports\n  coverage that does not exist. Two guards shipped green this way and were caught only by\n  stubbing: one passed solely because an unrelated helper stripped the env var its subject read,\n  and a documentation test passed with the documented guidance deleted, because both of its\n  component words already appeared in nearby prose. Neither was visible to review or to a green run.\n- The mutation must itself be verified (§\"Mutation verification\"): a `.replace()` that matches\n  nothing exits 0 and reports the guard as enforced.\n- **A criterion that cannot discriminate in EITHER direction is vacuous, and it survives both a\n  green run and a mutation battery.** The stronger case of the bullet above: not \"this check never\n  fails\" but \"this check returns the same observation whether the behaviour is present or absent\".\n2500→  Measured on AC-2.5b, which spied on `is_cli_available` *returning* `False` — the forced-`True`\n  mutation emits an identical record and returns the same value, so neither the passing run nor the\n  mutated run could tell the two states apart. AC-7.5c was the same shape. **Neither audit leg saw\n  either one**; the revising author found AC-2.5b while fixing an unrelated must. A document audit\n  reads prose and cannot ask \"does this pin discriminate?\", so the question has to be asked where the\n  criterion is written: **name the observation that differs between the implemented and unimplemented\n  states, and if you cannot name one, the criterion is decoration** — delete it or replace it with a\n  positive assertion only the implemented behaviour produces. Spying on a return value that both\n  states produce is the recurring form.\n- **Mutating a path-resolution function can disable the suite's own isolation.** Tests usually\n2510→  isolate by pointing an env override at a temp path, and that override is honoured by a branch —\n  the same kind of branch a mutation deletes. Stubbing the override branch in `_pin_file` redirected\n  every pin write in the suite onto the developer's live session file and replaced two real agent\n  handles, while the run reported 642 passed. Before mutating anything that decides *where* state is\n  written, snapshot the real target and restore it, or run in a sandboxed working directory.\n- **A stub must model the step the real system CONSUMES.** The rules above are about asserting the\n  right thing; this one is about the fixture telling the truth. A stub that replays state the real\n  system consumes once makes a test pass before the fix exists: an orca stub replayed an acked\n  delivery forever, so a sibling-cache test re-matched from the queue, pinned nothing, and passed\n  against unmodified code. Cardinality is part of the model — a fake that writes a path once where\n2520→  production writes it twice let a verb never dispatch with 57 tests green. Before trusting a stub,\n  ask which of its effects are destructive in production and make the stub destroy them too.\n\n## Verifying a review finding\n- **A review finding has three separable parts — facts, concern, prescription — and they fail\n  INDEPENDENTLY.** Before applying a prescription, diff it against the RED tests and the spec's\n  acceptance criteria. A finding that matches the design doc but breaks tests means the DESIGN\n  drifted, not the implementation. Measured: one pass's prescription broke a guard (1 failed, 53\n  passed) while its concern was entirely real, and another review's prescription was backwards.\n- Test a prescription by applying it as a mutation and reverting it. When two passes dissent, ask\n2530→  what the codebase ALREADY does — twice the house pattern beat both readings.\n\n## Guard narrowing\n- When a change **deliberately makes a guard accept something it used to reject**, the relaxation\n  MUST be shown to be *exactly* the intended case: run a corpus of inputs through the old and new\n  logic and diff the verdicts, then account for **every** input whose verdict softened. A green\n  suite is not evidence here — it encodes the cases someone already thought of, which is the wrong\n  population when the question is \"what else did this let through?\". This is the inverse of\n  §\"Mutation verification\": that proves a guard still bites, this proves a loosening did not widen.\n- The relaxation MUST rest on a **guarantee of the thing being parsed**, not on a heuristic that\n2540→  re-implements it. Narrowing a scanner by \"ignoring quoted regions\" means re-deriving the target\n  language's quoting rules in a regex, which is how a bypass gets introduced; narrowing it by a rule\n  the language itself guarantees is safe. Verify that guarantee **against the real interpreter**,\n  not its documentation (§\"Assumption verification\").\n- Both halves were load-bearing when this was written. Narrowing a heredoc scanner so it stopped\n  denying inert prose, a 23-input differential corpus flagged one case as a regression; running the\n  shape through real `bash` showed the body genuinely never expands, so the *test expectation* was\n  wrong and the code was right. Without the corpus that case ships as either an unnoticed hole or a\n  \"fix\" applied to correct behaviour — and nothing else would have distinguished them. The same run\n  confirmed the intended relaxations numbered exactly two, and that the remaining 21 verdicts were\n2550→  untouched.\n- A narrowing is scoped by **which checks consult the reduced input**, not by the reduction itself.\n  Exempting the quoted region from *one* class of check while every other class kept scanning the\n  full input is what kept the change from widening; a blanket exemption would have disabled\n  detections that were still correct for reasons unrelated to the false positive.\n\n## Connection enforcement\n- A task whose deliverable is a **connection** — a call site, an import, a registration, a route, a\n  flag or value propagated across a boundary — MUST ship a test that **fails when the connection\n  alone is removed and the callee is left intact**. A test that exercises the callee directly is not\n2560→  evidence that the callee is reached. Shipping a connection with no such test is a violation.\n- **A whole-module revert cannot establish this**, because it removes both sides: the RED split\n  returns identically for a wired and an unwired build. The same blindness runs through every other\n  layer — a RED phase goes red because the callee is *absent*, an anti-gaming audit finds a\n  callee-scoped unit test perfectly discriminating, and a review of the diff sees a call site that is\n  *present* and therefore reads as correct. **Presence is not enforcement.** Measured across two\n  consecutive wiring tasks: each shipped its single load-bearing decision untested through every\n  audit cycle and through the RED phase, and only a mutation scoped to the connection caught it.\n- Mutate the connection in **both directions**: remove it → the wire test must fail; force it to fire\n  unconditionally → the fall-through/negative test must fail. One direction certifies a connection\n2570→  that exists but is unconditional. Verify the mutation landed before trusting the run\n  (§\"Mutation verification\") — a revert that never happened reports as a pass.\n\n## Incident replay\n- A fix motivated by a specific observed incident MUST be **replayed against the real artifacts\n  already on disk** that motivated it, not only against cases authored alongside the fix.\n  **Synthetic cases alone are a violation** whenever such artifacts exist and are reachable.\n- Cases written next to a fix inherit the author's model of the bug, so they agree with it by\n  construction. A detector validated on 14 handcrafted samples rejected the real historical label\n  it was written to accept; the same replay then measured the true rate (7 of 13), which reclassified\n2580→  the defect from a one-off into the majority case. Replay is how a fix is told from a belief.\n\n## Assumption verification\n- Every **load-bearing assumption** in a plan or design — an API's accepted inputs, a command's\n  output shape, a boundary, a default — MUST be **executed as a throwaway command before it is\n  written into the design**, and the design **cites the observed output**. An assumption asserted\n  without evidence, where evidence was one command away, is a violation.\n- Design review cannot catch a wrong assumption: it reads as reasonable, and the implementation\n  and its tests are then both built from the same wrong model, so they agree with each other and\n  pass. Tracer-bulleting the assumptions of one feature confirmed a `--porcelain` boundary,\n2590→  confirmed a base-ref chain, and found a truncation hole — all before any code existed. Separately,\n  a selector grammar assumed from a wrapper's own code was wrong in a way that had already let a\n  destructive verb run unguarded.\n- The evidence belongs in the document, not only in the author's terminal. A cited output is\n  checkable by a reviewer; \"I verified this\" is not.\n- **Attributing a failure to a cause requires the controlled PAIR, not just the repro.** Run the\n  case with the blamed step and the case without it, and require the observable to differ. A repro\n  that reproduces confirms the symptom, never the cause. Measured four times: pane readiness was\n  blamed for an `injected:false` that a missing `--inject` flag fully explained, and that causality\n  shipped in a doc and a PR body; a title-only reading concluded \"no agents running\" and the\n2600→  operator corrected it; and two carried repros were each falsified by a control that removed the\n  step they blamed. Re-run the MEASUREMENT as well as the claim — a brief's conclusion can be right\n  while its method is wrong.\n\n- **A contract to RECOGNISE something needs a real instance of it, captured from the running\n  system, before any gate scores it clean** — a banner, a log line, a response shape.\n  `pin-agents-tail-banner` passed 2663 tests, 49 mutations and 53 audit cycles while its matcher\n  hit 0 of 5 real agent banners: all 12 corpus positives were written by hand. The fixture corpus must include at\n  least one captured sample with its capture command cited; an idealised fixture is not a sample.\n\n2610→## Behavioural premises carry their command\n- A premise about **behaviour** — \"X currently does Y\", \"this guard fires when Z\", \"the suite\n  collects N\", \"the parser rejects W\" — MUST carry the exact command that produced it, inline and\n  runnable, beside the observed output. An output recorded without its command is not\n  re-derivable, and a premise that cannot be cheaply re-run gets re-READ instead, cycle after\n  cycle, by every reviewer in turn. Measured: one false behavioural premise survived **10** audit\n  cycles that way.\n- Narrower than §\"Assumption verification\" and it fails at a different moment: there the evidence\n  was never gathered; here it was gathered once, written down as a conclusion, and thereby made\n  uncheckable. Both readers are then reasoning about prose.\n2620→- **The command belongs in the document even when the answer is one number**, because the number\n  alone does not say what was counted. Measured: two readers measuring \"the same\" census got **3**\n  and **23**, having run different commands, and neither could tell which was wrong until the\n  command was written down beside it. `git grep -l '```' -- '*.py'` is the census; \"21 files\" is\n  a claim about one.\n- A premise whose command has become expensive to re-run is not exempt. Say so, and say what the\n  cheap proxy is — an unrunnable premise is a premise no later cycle can falsify.\n\n## Counts a dispatch reports\n- **Re-derive every COUNT a dispatched agent reports; never carry it into a document, a commit\n2630→  message or a verdict on the agent's word.** A report that is right about its actions can still be\n  wrong about its numbers, and the two failures look identical from outside — the prose is accurate,\n  the figures are confident, and nothing in the report signals which is which.\n- Measured 2026-08-26: an `exec codex` report stated an anchor sweep as `mutations=238` when the\n  tree held **244**, with every spec byte-identical to HEAD and every other figure in the same\n  report checking out. Nothing had changed; the number was invented. The cheap control existed the\n  whole time — `--check-anchors` prints the count in about a second.\n- This is narrower than §\"Assumption verification\" and it applies at a different moment: there the\n  risk is an assumption written without evidence, here it is evidence that was produced, looks\n  right, and is false. Prefer a count you can re-derive with one command over any count a\n2640→  dispatch hands you, and when a count is load-bearing for a verdict, re-derive it before recording\n  the verdict rather than after.\n\n## Wrapper–runtime reconciliation\n- A wrapper verb over an **external runtime's CLI** MUST be exercised **live against that runtime**\n  — a full create → list → remove cycle, or the verb's own equivalent round trip — before it ships,\n  and its **output-key extraction fixed against the observed envelope**. Shipping a verb whose\n  response shape is known only from stub fixtures is a violation, however green the suite is.\n- Stub fixtures encode the author's model of the envelope, so the wrapper and its tests agree with\n  each other and both are wrong together — the Incident-replay gap, applied to response parsing.\n2650→  Four occurrences: a create-response `.id` read from the envelope (a per-request correlation uuid\n  that always exists, so it yielded a plausible but useless handle) in two different verbs; then\n  probing a live response for a delivery-id field surfaced `run_required` — the whole structured\n  path had been **dead at its first mutation**, and no stub test could see it because the stub had\n  no concept of a Run binding. Later the same day the real ack key proved to be `deliveryId` while\n  the extraction chain led with `delivery_id`, and the only test covering that loop pinned the\n  spelling the runtime never sends.\n- The live probe is what finds these, not the review: each was invisible to design review, to the\n  suite, and to a careful reading of the vendor guide. Where a live run is impossible (destructive\n  verb, no credentials), say so in the doc and name what stayed unverified — an unmeasured shape is\n2660→  an open risk, not a pass.\n\n## Regression provenance\n- When a change makes an existing test fail, the plan or design MUST establish whether that test\n  **asserts current behaviour that the change is fixing** before proposing to edit it. **Changing an\n  existing test to pass is a violation unless** the doc states that the test pinned a defect (or\n  stale contract) as correct, and cites what it asserted.\n- The reflex \"make the failing test pass\" preserves the bug when the test *was* the bug. Three tests\n  in one session asserted the defect as an acceptance criterion — a forwarded selector a live\n  runtime rejects, a create-response handle the pane never has, a cwd-relative path — and each would\n2670→  have survived a naive \"adjust the test\" edit. A red pre-existing test is evidence about the test as\n  often as about the change.\n\n## Both halves of a doc change\n- Removing a documented instruction, flag, or capability MUST be paired with **the executable\n  replacement landed** (or an explicit statement that the capability is intentionally dropped, with\n  the reason). A test asserting only that the old text is *gone* passes for a deletion that silently\n  lost the capability.\n- An unexecutable instruction was deleted in one session; a test checking only its absence would\n  have gone green while the run lost the ability it named. The gate is: assert the new path works in\n2680→  the same change that removes the old one.\n\n## Reimplementation parity\n- Replacing a third-party dependency (validator, parser, formatter) with an in-tree implementation\n  MUST ship a **differential test asserting identical results against the original** across a corpus\n  that covers every construct in use, AND against the **real artifacts on disk**. Shipping the\n  reimplementation with tests that exercise only itself is a violation.\n- A hand-rolled reimplementation is worth exactly what its differential test catches. A bundled\n  JSON-Schema validator agreed with the library on a construct-complete corpus and on the live state\n  records; the traps it had to match (annotation-only `format`, `bool` is not `integer`) were\n2690→  invisible without the library beside it. Parity on a synthetic corpus alone repeats the\n  Incident-replay gap: the corpus shares the author's model of the reimplementation.\n\n---\n\n## How agy uses this file\nagy reads this verbatim as the **base** portion of the Axis B rubric, before the project layer.\nAny finding that violates a base rule is auto-classified `## Must-fix` and cannot be downgraded.\n\n\n2700→### Project invariants (domain rules for this repository):\n\n# H-MAD Project Invariants — Axis B (BrightGold70/skills repo, domain layer)\n\n> Inlined by `/h-mad` as the **project (domain)** portion of Axis B, AFTER the\n> workflow-universal `invariants.base.md`. The base layer already enforces the\n> universal rules (audit-gate signal discipline, single-source contract,\n> no-plugin-dependency, doc-template superset compliance, operator-override\n> preservation, backward-compatibility, marker discipline) — they are NOT repeated\n> here. This file holds only rules specific to this repository (a collection of\n2710→> Claude Code skills: `SKILL.md` + `references/*.md` + `scripts/*.py` + `hooks/*.sh`).\n\n## Skill self-containment\n- A skill MUST remain runnable from a bare clone: no import of another skill's\n  internals, no hardcoded path outside the skill's own directory (except documented\n  host install locations: `~/.claude/...` for Claude Code, `~/.agents/skills/...` for\n  Codex, `~/.gemini/config/skills/...` for the Antigravity CLI (agy), each overridable\n  by its documented environment variable). Cross-skill coupling is a violation.\n  (Operator decision 2026-09-28, multi-host-runtime.)\n\n2720→## Skill manifest integrity\n- Every skill's `SKILL.md` MUST carry valid frontmatter with `name` and `description`.\n  A skill whose entry behavior is changed without updating its `SKILL.md` contract is\n  a violation.\n\n---\n\n## How agy uses this file\nagy reads this as the domain portion of Axis B, after the base layer. Domain-rule\nviolations are `## Must-fix`. If this repo has no domain-specific concern for a given\n2730→feature, this layer may legitimately add nothing beyond the base rules.\n\n\nAxis C — Spec reconciliation.\n\nThe spec is the source of truth for what this feature must do. A plan or design\nis derived from it and may legitimately argue for a narrower reading — but it\nmay not do so silently, because every downstream phase then measures the\nimplementation against a spec the design already walked away from.\n\n2740→Read the spec above and reconcile it against the target document **by\nidentifier**, not by impression:\n\n**Design audits — every acceptance criterion.** For each `AC-N.M` in the spec,\nclassify the design as exactly one of:\n\n| Classification | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `implemented-as-written` | the design covers the AC in its spec form |\n| `restated` | the design covers it in a different, usually narrower form |\n2750→| `absent` | the design does not address it at all |\n\n**Plan audits — every functional requirement.** Same three classifications at\n`FR-N` granularity. A plan is a strategy document and is not expected to restate\neach AC; it *is* expected to address every FR or explicitly defer it.\n\nReporting rules:\n\n- For every `restated` item, **quote both forms** — the spec's wording and the\n  design's — so the divergence is auditable rather than asserted. Then say which\n2760→  is narrower and in what respect.\n- `restated` and `absent` are **must-fix**. This is not a judgement that the\n  design is wrong: a narrowing may be well-argued and may be the right call. It\n  is a judgement that the divergence must be explicit and must land in the spec\n  before the gate clears, so the operator decides deliberately rather than\n  discovering it at verification.\n- An AC whose narrowing the spec **already reflects** is\n  `implemented-as-written`, not `restated`. That is the loop closing correctly.\n- Do not infer coverage from a section heading or a passing reference in a risk\n  or rationale table. An AC is covered when the document says what will satisfy\n2770→  it.\n\nReport Axis C as a table in your `## Summary`, then raise each `restated` or\n`absent` item as its own `## Must-fix` bullet.\n\nEffort contract (binding on you either way; MEASURED only on some dispatches):\n\n- **Sweep the whole document. Do not stop at the first blocking finding.** Measured\n  over 83 design / 74 plan / 34 impl-plan cycles on one feature: the median pass\n  returned **one** must-fix against a prompt asking for all of them, which turns every\n2780→  defect into its own round trip and is most of why those numbers are that large.\n- **Read the tree before asserting what is in it.** The documents are inlined above,\n  which is exactly why a pass can look thorough having opened nothing. Their claims are\n  *about* the tree — a `path:symbol`, a signature, a test name, a count against its own\n  list — and those are the claims that turn out to be wrong.\n- **When you re-derive a published figure, four things must match or your reading answers a\n  different question. Each has been got wrong here by an auditor, not only by the orchestrator:**\n  - *Commit.* Presence at a sha is not provenance. \"X was introduced in revision N\" needs the\n    needle PRESENT at N's landing commit **and ABSENT at N-1's** — two readings return N and\n    N+1 alike. `git rev-list <base>..<sha> -- <path>` first: if that commit does not touch the\n2790→    file, it is not the figure's provenance.\n  - *Corpus.* **Run the command the document DEFINES, not one that reproduces the document's\n    number.** A head-scoped series verified whole-blob agreed for four shas and then did not;\n    the gating auditor re-ran it, reported it reproduced, and the scope was wrong the whole\n    time. Agreement with a published value is evidence about arithmetic, never about scope.\n  - *Grammar.* `grep -c` answers \"how many lines contain this string\", never \"how many\n    instances of this concept exist\". Print the matching lines and classify them before you\n    publish a count — a Python kwarg and a diagnostic field spell identically. Over markdown,\n    collapse newlines and admit the inline-code delimiters (`` ` ``): a needle that cannot\n    match across a backtick reported 2 sites where there are 4.\n2800→  - *Shell.* Take each control in its own invocation; a `cd` in a chained command is not\n    scoped to that command.\n- **If a document publishes a screen with `# expect 0`, RUN IT** rather than reporting that it\n  is present. Three sheet entries certified \"no scoped census moved\" over a commit at which the\n  document's own trip-wire read 8.\n- **An ABSENCE claim is a figure.** \"No corpus instance …\", \"the corpus has none\", \"nothing in\n  this repository\" — hold each to the same command and sha as a count; both that shipped here\n  were false. A zero bounded only by some *unrelated* constraint is a defect: the document says\n  why it is zero and whether that reason is load-bearing.\n- **A stated PROPERTY of a screen is a claim about code — execute it.** \"No working-file edit can\n2810→  move this\" was false of the shipped line. Do the thing it says cannot move it, and re-run. A\n  control over an ALTERNATION tests the alternation, never its branches: a healthy sibling covers\n  a sick one (one boundary repair was half-applied three rounds running and passed every time).\n- **Your report is held to this too.** Every count carries its UNIT (`grep -n` lines are not\n  occurrences); a population you prescribe is derived once and cited twice; and your PRESCRIPTION\n  is a claim about the tree — one prescribed naming an enclosing function for a line that has\n  none. Check it or mark it unchecked, and give every alternative repair rather than the best-\n  reading one.\n- **A pass at or below two successful tool calls cannot certify a clean.** Writing the\n  report and its marker costs those two by itself, so at that floor nothing was read.\n2820→  When your transcript is captured — an `audit-cycle` dispatch — the combiner enforces\n  this and scores the cycle `UNVERIFIED` rather than clean. When it is not captured,\n  **nothing measures you and the rule binds you anyway**: say what you read. Findings\n  count at any effort; this bounds what a *clean* is worth, never what a finding is.\n- **State your evidence** on its own line in `## Summary`: `Evidence: <N> files opened,\n  <M> greps run.`\n\nOutput framing (mandatory — the orchestrator extracts on these markers):\n\nEmit your report bracketed by these two lines, each alone on its line, with\n2830→nothing before the first or after the second:\n\n    AUDIT-agy-coder-fallback-plan-v1-BEGIN\n    ...your report...\n    AUDIT-agy-coder-fallback-plan-v1-END\n\nYour report is read by scraping this terminal, and the previous cycle's report\nis usually still visible above the prompt. The markers are how the orchestrator\ntells your new report from that old one — omit them and the audit cannot be\nscored. Emit them even if you have nothing to report.\n2840→\nOutput schema (exact, no other top-level sections):\n\n## Summary\n<2-3 sentences>\n\n## Must-fix\n- <issue> — <why it breaks invariant or creates a hard gap>\n  *(empty section: write the single word `None` on its own line — NOT a `- None` bullet; a leading `- ` would be counted as a blocking item)*\n\n2850→## Should-fix\n- <issue> — <why it matters but isn't a hard gate>\n  *(empty section: write the single word `None` on its own line — NOT a `- None` bullet; a leading `- ` would be counted as a blocking item)*\n\n## Nit\n- <style/clarity issue>\n  *(empty section: write the single word `None` on its own line — NOT a `- None` bullet; a leading `- ` would be counted as a blocking item)*\n\nClass rule (Must-fix and Should-fix): **every bullet carries a `class:` continuation line**, `build` or\n`measurement`, under the bullet and never as a `- ` bullet of its own (a leading `- ` is a second\n2860→finding). The test is one question: **would the code or tests a 5d/5e implementer writes differ if this finding were fixed?** Yes → `class: build` (an interface, a contract, a\npredicate, a fixture, a test's assertion or RED/GREEN count, a false premise about the platform). No →\n`class: measurement` (a published number, stamp, ledger row, sha series, pointer or self-count that is\nstale or mis-derived; the tree is unchanged by fixing it). An untagged bullet is scored as `build`, so\nomitting the line never softens a finding — it only costs the orchestrator the triage. Put the `class:` line directly under its bullet (before or after `quote:`); do not indent a sub-bullet between a finding and its `class:` line, which leaves the finding untagged.\n\n    - <issue> — <why>\n      class: build\n      quote: <path/to/file.md> › `<span copied verbatim from that file>`\n\n2870→Quoting rule (Must-fix and Should-fix): **if a finding asserts what a document says, prove it.**\nAdd an indented `quote:` line under that bullet naming the file and carrying the span copied\nverbatim out of it:\n\n    - <issue> — <why it breaks the invariant>\n      quote: <path/to/file.md> › `<span copied verbatim from that file>`\n\nThe `quote:` line is a continuation of its bullet, never a new `- ` bullet — a leading `- ` would\nbe scored as a second finding. Use one `quote:` line per document the finding names.\n\n2880→This exists because backticks alone are ambiguous. Reviewers correctly backtick three other kinds\nof span — code they are **proposing** as the fix, test inputs they **constructed**, and commands\nthey **ran** — and those legitimately do not occur in the document. Measured over 37 must-fixes:\n43% of *real* findings carry a backticked span absent from the document, so absence alone proves\nnothing and no automatic check can be built on it. `quote:` is the marker that separates a claim\nabout the document from everything else you are entitled to write in backticks.\n\nA finding that asserts document content with no `quote:` line is not rejected automatically, but it\nis the first thing the orchestrator re-derives from source — and a `quote:` span that does not occur\nin the file it names is a finding withdrawn, not a finding to argue.","content_concise":"1890→    consecutive `#` lines is seen only when the comment text is joined. So the sweep also reads\n    each `.py` file's comment tokens (`tokenize`, with the `#` stripped), joined and\n    whitespace-collapsed.\n  - **A file that `ast.parse` rejects.** It is never skipped silently. The sweep reports it by name\n    with a count (`unparsed=N`), its line-grep and comment readings still apply, and a nonzero\n    count means the file is read by hand before the sweep counts as clean.\n  - **Bytes literals** (`b\"…\"`) are not `str` constants. The line grep covers them on one line.\n    A bytes literal split across lines is the residual of this axis.\n\n  **The sweep is therefore the union** of the line grep over every tracked file and the joined\n1900→  reading (the `.py` `str` constants, the joined `.py` comment text, and the whitespace-collapsed\n  text of every other file). One measurement at `5f72c2f3` (h-mad last changed at `083a8233`):\n  joined reading 7 files, line grep 6 files, line grep minus joined reading 0 files, union\n  7 files; comment-token hits 0 files, joined-comment hits 0 files, `unparsed=0`. The comment\n  zero is incidental: no comment in `h-mad/` restates the rule today. It is not a property of the\n  sweep, and it does not license dropping the comment reading.\n\n  Residual: a later surface that restates the rule is not covered until this sweep is re-run. A\n  phrase built at run time (an f-string field, `%`/`.format` interpolation, or a `+` join of names\n  rather than literals) is not a `str` constant, so no reading in the sweep sees it.\n1910→  Such a surface is found only by reading the code that builds the dispatch text. A bytes literal\n  split across lines is the other residual, as stated above.\n\n- **The relaxation is shown to be exactly the intended case (differential corpus).** The carve-out\n  makes the 5d RED check accept a RED it used to halt, so `h-mad/invariants.base.md` §\"Guard\n  narrowing\" applies. The rule has no code implementation. At `e7730f78`,\n  `git ls-files h-mad | grep -v '\\.md$' | xargs grep -ln 'red_wrong_reason\\|missing.symbol'`\n  hits only two test files (`test_h_mad_wire_pin_gate.py` and `test_h_mad_wiring_task_shape_docs.py`),\n  and no script. So the corpus runs over **RED evidence**, not over a function. Each case is\n  a fixture repository with a RED base, a WIRE-PIN test and the real pytest RED it produces. The\n1920→  four kinds of missing symbol are:\n\n  | case | missing symbol in the RED | callee at the RED base | old verdict | new verdict |\n  |---|---|---|---|---|\n  | `new-caller` | the caller the task creates | resolves | halt `red_wrong_reason` | **valid** |\n  | `missing-callee` | the callee | absent | halt | halt |\n  | `both-missing` | the caller the task creates (the callee is also absent) | absent | halt | halt |\n  | `unrelated-import` | a symbol the task neither creates nor calls (for example an unrelated module failing to import) | resolves | halt | halt |\n\n  - For each case the probe records the RED's exception line, whether the callee resolves at\n1930→    the RED base (shown by importing it at that commit, not by reading the source), and the\n    verdict under the old rule text and under the new one. The design names how a verdict is\n    derived from rule text: either a classifier function that both texts' prose describes, or a\n    stated derivation per case.\n  - **Pass:** the set of cases whose verdict changed is exactly `{new-caller}`. Any other member\n    is a widening, and it is fixed in the rule text, never in the table.\n  - The four rows are one per kind of missing symbol on the RED. The residual axis is the\n    exception type: the corpus produces each case's RED as the exception the fixture actually\n    raises, and a case whose RED is `NameError` is a fifth fixture only when the new rule's text\n    treats `NameError` differently from `ImportError`/`AttributeError`.\n1940→- **Incident replay against the real artifacts.** The carve-out was motivated by a real incident,\n  so `h-mad/invariants.base.md` §\"Incident replay\" applies, and the synthetic corpus above does not\n  replace the replay. The artifacts are in the HemaSuite checkout (`/Users/kimhawk/orca/HemaSuite`):\n  - `docs/archive/2026-10/notebooklm-integration-interface/notebooklm-integration-interface.analysis.md`,\n    item **F-3 — WIRE-PIN RED on a new caller (T4/T5/T6 WIRE 2)**. It names the cases and their\n    wire-cut evidence: M4.1, M5.1 and the WIRE 2 inline revert;\n  - the same directory's `notebooklm-integration-interface.impl-plan.md`, whose WIRE-PIN table\n    maps each case to its pin and mutation row;\n  - the three pins, each in `hematology-paper-writer/tests/` at its task commit:\n\n1950→    | case | pin (test file `::` test) | task commit (subject locator) |\n    |---|---|---|\n    | T4 WIRE-PIN 1 | `test_notebooklm_resolver.py::test_resolver_calls_registry_arm_notebook_id_once` | `531a6296b` (`git log --all --oneline --grep='Task 4 FR-1 resolver'`) |\n    | T5 WIRE-PIN 1 | `test_notebooklm_initialize.py::test_initialize_entry_point_resolves_through_registry_arm` | `7aa0d9dec` (`--grep='Task 5 FR-5 initialize'`) |\n    | T6 WIRE-PIN 2 | `test_notebooklm_status_report.py::test_status_entry_point_resolves_through_registry_arm` | `c2d671f6b` (`--grep='Task 6 CLI surface'`) |\n\n    Each task commit's parent is its RED base. The pin names come from the impl-plan's\n    `**WIRE-PIN N**:` lines, and each file was checked with `git show --stat <commit>`.\n\n  **Which form of the pin the replay evaluates.** The committed pins do not share one form:\n1960→  - T4 looks the new caller up with two-argument `getattr(integration, \"resolve_registry_notebook\")`.\n    At the RED base this raises `AttributeError`, so the committed pin's RED is a missing symbol.\n  - T5 and T6 use the impl-plan's \"WIRE-PIN RED scaffold\". It is a three-argument\n    `getattr(..., None)` (T5: `getattr(integration, \"initialize_project_notebook\", None)`; T6:\n    `getattr(integration.NotebookLMIntegration(), \"generate_setup_report\", None)`) inside\n    `except (Exception, SystemExit)`, followed by an assertion on the spy. Their committed RED is\n    an `AssertionError`, which the old rule already accepts.\n\n  So for T5 and T6 the replay evaluates the **direct-call form** of the pin: the scaffold is\n  replaced by a two-argument `getattr` on the same name (or a direct call), the `try`/`except` is\n1970→  removed, and the spy assertion is unchanged. That is the form whose RED is the missing caller,\n  and it is the form the carve-out is about. The replay also records each committed pin's own RED,\n  for the record. For T4 the committed pin is already in the direct-call form.\n\n  For each of the three cases, the replay:\n  1. reproduces the direct-call form's RED at the RED base, with the pin from the task commit\n     (rewritten as above for T5 and T6). The RED must be `AttributeError`/`ImportError` on the\n     caller, and the callee must resolve at the RED base (shown by import);\n  2. applies the new rule to that RED and records its verdict, which must be **valid**;\n  3. records the old rule's verdict on that RED, which must be halt. For T5 and T6 it also records\n1980→     the committed scaffolded pin's RED (`AssertionError`) and the old verdict on it, which is\n     **valid**: the scaffold is how those tasks avoided the halt;\n  4. re-executes the wire-cut at the task commit with the committed pin: delete the call only, keep\n     the caller and the callee, and confirm the pin fails. It also locates the original evidence\n     (M4.1, M5.1, the WIRE 2 revert) and cites it beside the re-run.\n\n  - The replay is a committed probe under `docs/03-analysis/probes/agy-coder-fallback/`, stamped\n    with this repository's sha and the HemaSuite sha it read.\n  - A case whose RED does not reproduce, or whose original evidence cannot be located, is recorded\n    with the command that failed. It is never dropped from the count.\n1990→  - **Residual.** The locators are commit subjects and archive paths. If HemaSuite rewrites\n    history or moves the archive, re-locate with `git log --all --grep='notebooklm-integration-interface'`.\n\n### FR-10 — Documentation surfaces\n\n- **SKILL.md** §\"Codex authors Phase 5 — enforced, not just instructed\" becomes implementer-neutral\n  and states FR-2/FR-3. §\"Exit-code dispatch for 5d/5e\" lists `exec agy` as an implementer\n  dispatch beside its review role.\n- **`h-mad/references/agy-runtime.md`** §\"The TDD gate\" names the new gate and FR-5's check, and keeps\n  the halt.\n2000→- **`h-mad/references/state-schema.md`** gains the FR-1 rows. Its existing `fallback_agent` and\n  `codex_status` rows are rewritten, and so are the schema JSON's `fallback_agent` and\n  `codex_status` `description`s, all four per FR-1.\n- **`h-mad/references/failure-recovery.md`** gets the 5d `red_wrong_reason` row carve-out per\n  FR-9.\n- **SKILL.md, the Phase-5 orchestrator steps:** the `implementation_authors` append on accepting\n  a 5e GREEN or fix dispatch, and the attribution read from it before each review leg, per FR-1\n  and FR-7 (D-15).\n- **SKILL.md, Phase 7 closure step:** the report carries every `WARNING` line that\n  `h_mad_phase7_preconditions.py` prints, the FR-7 warnings included.\n2010→- **SKILL.md §\"Teammate audit leg — when codex is unavailable\"**, the bullet that begins\n  `- **It does not license Claude to write Phase 5 production code`. Today it says \"but the\n  declaration you just made does\". Under FR-2 that is false when agy is available: the\n  declaration routes the audit leg, but it moves Phase-5 authorship to agy, and FR-3 refuses\n  Claude (`agy-authorship`). The rewrite keeps the anchor prefix, which\n  `test_h_mad_tdd_gate_docs.py` `_skill_gate_bullet` requires exactly once, and the tokens\n  `ACTIVE`, `every`, `unavailable` and `exhausted`, which\n  `test_skill_gate_bullet_states_the_every_record_rule` requires. It states two things separately:\n  - **Audit-leg selection.** `codex_status` alone routes the 5b audit to the teammate leg.\n  - **Resolved authorship.** Claude may write Phase-5 production code only when FR-2's `resolved`\n2020→    is `claude` for every ACTIVE `step5` record on the chain. That means both peers are\n    unavailable and `fallback_agent` is absent, `null` or `claude`.\n  The replaced condition is \"the `codex_status` declaration licenses Claude\". The new condition is\n  \"`resolved` = `claude`\", and the bullet names `h_mad_tdd_judge.py implementer` as the way to read\n  it.\n- **Audit legs in Phases 3/4/5b are unchanged.** `codex_status` still routes the second audit\n  surface to the teammate leg (SKILL.md §\"Teammate audit leg — when codex is unavailable\").\n  `agy_status` has no audit-routing consumer.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n2030→\n- **AC-1.1:** The schema accepts each FR-1 field at each listed value and rejects a value outside\n  it. A record with none of the fields validates unchanged. The `fallback_agent` description in\n  the schema JSON and its `state-schema.md` row state FR-2's both-peers condition. The\n  `codex_status` description and row no longer say that an unavailable Codex permits Claude to\n  author, and state authorship as FR-2's resolution. `test_h_mad_state_fallback_agent.py` stays\n  green. For `implementer_switch.ts`, \"the listed value\" is FR-1's narrowed profile, leap second\n  excluded (D-14); its fixtures are AC-1.2's.\n- **AC-1.2:** A record whose `implementer_switch.ts` is `not-a-date`, `2026-10-16`,\n  `2026-02-30T00:00:00Z` or the leap second `2016-12-31T23:59:60Z` (D-14) is refused on the library path and, under the test seam that forces it,\n2040→  on the bundled path. Each value is its own fixture, run once per path. A `.ts` in the writer's\n  form `%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ` is accepted on both paths. If the mechanism uses the library's format\n  checker, one fixture runs with `rfc3339_validator` made unimportable and still refuses\n  `not-a-date`.\n- **AC-1.3 (D-15):** `implementation_authors` is pinned on the library path and, under the same\n  test seam as AC-1.2, on the bundled path; each fixture runs once per path:\n  - accepted: `[]`; one entry per agent `codex`, `agy`, `grok`, `claude`, each its own fixture;\n    and `[{\"module\":\"m\",\"agent\":\"agy\"},{\"module\":\"m\",\"agent\":\"codex\"},{\"module\":\"m\",\"agent\":\"agy\"}]`\n    (duplicates and order kept);\n  - refused, each its own fixture: an unknown agent (`\"agent\":\"gemini\"`), a missing `agent`, a\n2050→    missing `module`, `\"module\":\"\"`, an extra key, a non-object entry, and a non-array value;\n  - a record with no `implementation_authors` validates unchanged.\n  An append of a refused entry through the Record mechanism leaves the store byte-identical.\n- **AC-2.1:** The resolution function: for every combination of {`implementer` absent / `codex` /\n  `agy` / invalid} × {`codex_avail`, `agy_avail` ∈ true/false}, `resolved` and `switch` match FR-2.\n  Each switch reason is produced by its own fixture, with only that cause present.\n- **AC-2.2:** The `implementer` verb prints exactly one line for each of the six token rows, and\n  for `record=failed`. `--record` writes `implementer_switch` once and does not rewrite it on an\n  identical re-run.\n- **AC-3.1:** Each of the five gate-specific refusal kinds is produced by its own fixture. In\n2060→  addition, `implementer-invalid` outranks `codex-authorship`, a peer refusal outranks\n  `fallback-invalid`, and the chain fold blocks on the FIRST eligible record.\n- **AC-3.2:** The `codex-authorship`, `fallback-grok` and `fallback-invalid` message bytes equal\n  those at `a8d9ba99`. Every existing `test_h_mad_tdd_gate_*.py` stays green, and the only change\n  allowed is FR-3's stub-line exception. In the two files FR-3 names, a string literal that stubs\n  a `TDD-STATE: active` line, and the constants it is composed from, are re-shaped to the new\n  line. Every assertion, expected kind, reason substring, test name and parametrisation id stays\n  byte-identical (checked by diffing the file against `a8d9ba99`). Every other\n  `test_h_mad_tdd_gate_*.py` is unmodified. A new fixture shows that a line in today's shape now\n  refuses `judge-error`. In `test_h_mad_tdd_judge.py`, `ACTIVE_RE` is the only changed literal, and\n2070→  the real-line assertions FR-3 lists stay byte-identical and green (checked by the same diff).\n- **AC-4.1:** With the scan reading `inactive`, the agy gate allows every tool, except that a\n  class-(b) production `.py` target whose chain reads `unreadable` still denies (the `chmod 000`\n  `docs/` chain fixture). With it `active`, it covers:\n  - each class with at least one member;\n  - an unknown tool name → class (d) deny;\n  - a class-(b) payload with no path key → deny, and the same with the key's value `\"\"` → deny;\n  - a production `.py` without a failing test → deny, with a judge kind;\n  - with a failing test → allow;\n  - `/tmp` and `artifactDirectoryPath` non-`.py` → allow, and `.py` there → deny;\n2080→  - a class-(b) target naming an existing directory (`.` and `src/`) → deny `kind=judge-error`,\n    and the same target with the scan reading `inactive` → allow (D-16).\n  With the scan reading `unknown` (a `docs/.bkit-memory.json` that is not JSON), each deny above\n  that does not need the judge still fires (class (d), no path key, `\"\"`, out-of-root `.py`, a\n  directory target, a `run_command` FR-6 refuses, and the `workspacePaths` cross-check), each from its own fixture.\n  The **target differential** runs one corpus of target shapes through both gates and shows they\n  agree on every shape except those FR-4's §\"Deliberate differences\" names, where the agy gate\n  refuses.\n- **AC-4.2:** Every exit path prints exactly one JSON object: malformed stdin, an internal\n  exception and an unreadable state each still yield parseable stdout. Every deny path the design\n2090→  enumerates is driven by its own fixture, and each fixture asserts that `reason` ends with\n  exactly one `kind=<kind>` whose value is in the design's closed list (FR-4 §\"Every deny carries\n  a kind\"); a deny path with no fixture, or a kind outside the list, fails the AC.\n- **AC-5.1:** The wiring check emits each `UNWIRED` reason from its own fixture. `--agent agy`\n  writes no prompt and halts on each of them.\n- **AC-5.2:** Each row of FR-5's exit-code table is pinned by its own fixture, and each fixture\n  asserts the stdout token and the exit code separately:\n  - an `UNWIRED` fixture (one per reason, reusing AC-5.1's) shows check exit 0, and assembler\n    `ASSEMBLE-TDD: HALT agy_tdd_hook_unverified` with exit 0;\n  - a `hooks.json` holding invalid JSON shows check `UNREADABLE` with exit 2, and the assembler\n2100→    HALT with exit 2;\n  - a check stubbed to print no token shows the assembler `HALT agy_tdd_hook_unverified check=no-token`\n    with exit 2;\n  - `WIRED` shows exit 0 on both;\n  - the agy gate's `--self-check` with a verdict `FAIL` forced prints that `FAIL` token on stdout\n    with exit 0, and the wiring check reads it as `UNWIRED reason=self-check-failed`.\n\n  A mutation that returns 2 on `UNWIRED` is killed, and so is one that returns 2 on a self-check\n  verdict `FAIL`.\n- **AC-6.1:** The shell policy is imported, not copied: one definition in the tree. The Codex gate's\n2110→  existing shell tests pass unmodified. Every mutation row re-pointed under FR-6 runs\n  `MUTATION: ALL_CAUGHT` against the shared module, `h_mad_target_identity.py` (D-12). The agy gate refuses each `unsafe` command in the Codex\n  gate's `--self-check` list.\n- **AC-7.1:** `h_mad_phase7_preconditions.py` emits each of the three FR-7 warnings from its own\n  fixture, and none of them moves `ready`. Adding a `review_independence` entry to a record that\n  already holds two leaves both earlier entries byte-identical. An entry that fails FR-1 is\n  refused, and the store is unchanged.\n- **AC-7.2:** `review_independence_unrecorded` fires, with no `leg=6a-prime` entry, for each\n  implementer `codex` (field absent included), `agy`, `grok` and `claude`, each from its own\n  fixture (`grok` and `claude` through `implementer_switch.to`, the only FR-1 routing field that takes them; `implementation_authors` is an authorship record and does not trigger the warning), and for each `archreview` value `READY_TO_MERGE`, `WITH_FIXES` and `NO`. It does not fire\n2120→  when a `leg=6a-prime` entry exists, nor when `archreview` is absent, `SKIPPED_NO_PANE` or\n  `SKIPPED_OPERATOR_OVERRIDE`.\n- **AC-7.3 (v1.8):** FR-7 §\"Attribution\" is pinned by fixtures, each RED against the mutant it\n  names and GREEN on the rule:\n  - **routing is not authorship:** `implementer_switch.to=agy` stored, the module's code from a\n    Codex dispatch, a Codex reviewer → `implementer=codex`, `independent=false`, and\n    `review_non_independent` fires. Mutant: attribution read from `implementer_switch.to`.\n  - **multi-author, recovered (E2):** authors agy then Codex; a grok 5e-review →\n    `implementer=codex`, `independent=true`; a Codex 5e-verify → `implementer=codex`,\n    `independent=false`; then, with the dispatch context discarded, an agy 6a-prime →\n2130→    `implementer=agy`, `independent=false`. Mutant: authors recovered from earlier entries'\n    `implementer` values only. The recovered set's source is `implementation_authors` (FR-1,\n    D-15), holding `{m, agy}` then `{m, codex}`.\n  - **pre-field feature (D-15):** the same reviews on a record with no `implementation_authors`\n    → the 6a-prime leg writes no entry for the module. Mutant: an absent field read as an empty\n    author set, so the agy reviewer reads as independent.\n  - **RED is not authorship (E4):** an agy 5d RED dispatch then a Codex 5e GREEN; an agy reviewer →\n    `independent=true`, `implementer=codex`. Mutant: a 5d RED dispatch counted as an author.\n  - **unaccepted dispatches author nothing:** a rejected agy GREEN then an accepted Codex GREEN;\n    an agy reviewer → `independent=true`.\n2140→  If the design leaves attribution as orchestrator prose with no code to drive, this AC has no\n  surface to run on; that is owed to the impl-plan as an OPEN-DECISION, not waived.\n- **AC-8.1:** The probe directory holds the payload capture, the real refusal, and the RED/GREEN\n  readings with re-run, revert and mutation results, each stamped with the sha and the\n  `agy --version`. The default timeout in `h_mad_assemble_tdd.py` equals FR-8's formula over\n  those readings. The readings come from FR-8's post-merge stage (D-13), and the feature is not\n  recorded COMPLETE before AC-8.1 and AC-8.2 pass.\n- **AC-8.2:** The probe directory holds the forced-handler-timeout reading from FR-8 step 2a,\n  stamped with the sha, `agy --version` and the T it used. The reading has three parts: whether\n  the file exists, the wall time and agy's message. It names exactly one outcome from step 2a's\n2150→  table, and that outcome is consistent with the three readings. It also shows that the delay\n  hook was removed and that `.agents/hooks.json` matches its pre-probe bytes. A `session-error`\n  outcome does not satisfy this AC. If the outcome is `timeout-allows`, `agy-runtime.md` §\"The TDD\n  gate\" carries the fail-open residual, and the operator question is raised before the gate is\n  called verified.\n- **AC-9.1:** The prompt contains no implementer-specific opening. `test_prompt_file_is_agent_independent`\n  covers `codex`, `grok` and `agy`. The new-caller rule appears in RED item 1, in the WIRE-PIN\n  paragraph, in the GREEN variant, in the SKILL.md 5d halt text, in the `failure-recovery.md`\n  `step5d:red_wrong_reason:<module>` row and in the wiring-task header that `h_mad_assemble_tdd.py`\n  emits. `test_h_mad_wiring_task_shape_docs.py`, `test_h_mad_agent_definitions.py` and\n2160→  `test_h_mad_assemble_tdd.py` stay green. FR-9's sweep, re-run on the shipped tree, hits no file\n  that states the rule without the carve-out. The sweep is the union FR-9 defines: the line grep\n  over every tracked file and the joined reading, which takes in `.py` comment text. The re-run\n  reports `unparsed=N`. A nonzero `N` does not satisfy this AC until each unparsed file has been\n  read by hand.\n- **AC-9.2:** The FR-9 differential corpus runs all four cases (`new-caller`, `missing-callee`,\n  `both-missing`, `unrelated-import`) as fixture repositories with real pytest REDs. For each case\n  it records the RED's exception line, whether the callee resolves at the RED base (by import) and\n  both verdicts. The set of cases whose verdict changed is exactly `{new-caller}`.\n- **AC-9.3:** The FR-9 incident replay covers HemaSuite `notebooklm-integration-interface` T4\n2170→  WIRE-PIN 1 (`test_notebooklm_resolver.py` at `531a6296b`), T5 WIRE-PIN 1\n  (`test_notebooklm_initialize.py` at `7aa0d9dec`) and T6 WIRE-PIN 2\n  (`test_notebooklm_status_report.py` at `c2d671f6b`). For each case the committed probe holds:\n  - the direct-call form's RED reproduced at the task commit's parent: `AttributeError`/\n    `ImportError` on the caller, with the callee resolving by import. For T4 this is the committed\n    pin; for T5 and T6 it is the pin with the scaffold replaced, per FR-9;\n  - on that RED, the new rule's verdict **valid** and the old rule's verdict halt;\n  - for T5 and T6 only, the committed scaffolded pin's RED (`AssertionError`) and the old rule's\n    verdict on it, which is valid;\n  - a wire-cut re-run at the task commit in which the committed pin fails;\n2180→  - a citation of the original evidence (M4.1, M5.1, WIRE 2 revert).\n\n  It is stamped with both repositories' shas. A case that cannot be reproduced is recorded with\n  its failing command and counted. It does not satisfy the AC.\n- **AC-9.4:** The six copies of the new-caller rule (FR-9 surfaces 1–5 and the GREEN variant) are\n  bound to one authority. Either every surface is generated from or checked against one shared\n  source, or one test asserts that the carve-out text is equal across all six copies. The plan\n  names which mechanism. Whichever it is:\n  - **The carve-out text is the whole quoted block in FR-9**, all three sentences: the validity\n    condition, \"The RED report must name both.\", and the wire-cut obligation. A check that binds\n2190→    only the first sentence does not meet this AC.\n  - **Each copy is read from its own source location.** The span read for one copy contains no\n    other copy. Three readings do not meet this AC, because a healthy copy in the same span\n    masks a sick one: a contains-check over the whole assembled prompt (the template's copies\n    1, 2 and 6 are filled into it beside the header copy); a file-level contains-check over\n    `codex-implementer-prompt.md`, which holds three copies; and a file-level check over any\n    file that holds more than one copy. The design names each copy's locator. A locator states\n    what it does not pin, and the test fails, rather than passing, when a locator matches\n    nothing or more than one span.\n  - **The control is a mutation of exactly one copy, and it makes the check fail.** It is run\n2200→    once per copy, so six times, and once per sentence of the block in at least one copy. Each\n    mutation row's `find` is unique in its file, because `h_mad_mutation_harness.py`\n    `anchor_status` refuses a `find` that does not occur exactly once. A `find` therefore carries\n    copy-specific context, and the `.py` copy's `find` is written against the source bytes,\n    including the adjacent-literal split. A row the harness refuses is not a control.\n- **AC-X:** Every new guard carries a mutation row that bites (`MUTATION: ALL_CAUGHT` with a `test`\n  per row). The full suite passes per task.\n\n## Decisions on the brainstorm's open questions\n\n2210→- **D-1 `run_command`:** command-shape rule shared with Codex (FR-6). Rationale: the tree shows\n  that is what Codex parity is.\n- **D-2 Hook scope:** per project (`.agents/hooks.json`). Rationale:\n  - The Codex gate is wired per project (HemaSuite `.codex/hooks.json`; this repository has no\n    `.codex/`).\n  - A tracked file reaches every worktree.\n  - The handler cwd gives the root without trusting a payload field.\n  - No global hook fires in every agy session on the machine.\n\n  Residual: a project without the file has no agy gate. FR-5 turns that into a halt, not a silent\n2220→  pass.\n- **D-3 Payload cwd:** the root comes from the handler cwd (FR-4), with `workspacePaths` as a\n  cross-check.\n- **D-4 Timeout:** provisional 1500 s, then FR-8's formula. The hook-handler timeout is a separate\n  figure, derived from `JUDGE_BUDGET_S`.\n- **D-5 Availability:** CLI on PATH + `agy_status` + `HMAD_AGY_UNAVAILABLE`, symmetric with Codex.\n  No liveness probe: the gate runs on every write and must be offline and fast, and a probe spends\n  quota. An exhausted agy shows up when the dispatch fails, and the orchestrator records it with\n  `--set agy_status=exhausted`.\n- **D-6 Project default:** feature record only. A second source doubles the surface the gate\n2230→  resolves, and per-feature is the operator decision. A later default can seed the same field at\n  `--create`.\n- **D-7 Flag location:** a state field (FR-7). It is not on the extracted verdict, because that\n  line is `<KEY>: <VALUE>` and its consumers parse it.\n- **D-8 Audit legs:** unchanged (FR-10).\n- **D-9 Codex reviewing Codex (was OQ-1; operator decision, 2026-10-16):** yes. FR-7's flag fires\n  for any `reviewer == implementer`, Codex included, for example the 5e anti-gaming verifier\n  dispatched to Codex on a Codex-implemented feature. It is a warning only, never a block.\n  Consequence: Codex-implemented features can now carry a `review_non_independent` Phase-7\n  warning that has never appeared before.\n2240→- **D-10 `.agents/` exclusion (was OQ-2; operator decision, 2026-10-16):** narrow it. Applied in this\n  clone by the orchestrator on the operator's answer: the `.git/info/exclude` entry `/.agents/` is now `/.agents/skills/`.\n  Evidence at `d65374e9`: `git check-ignore -v .agents/hooks.json` prints nothing (exit 1, not\n  ignored), and `git check-ignore -v .agents/skills` still reports the `/.agents/skills/` entry.\n  D-2's tracked `.agents/hooks.json` therefore needs no `git add -f`.\n  - **Residual / prerequisite.** `.git/info/exclude` is per-clone local configuration and is not\n    versioned. Every other clone of this repository, and every other project that adopts the agy\n    gate, needs the same narrowing (or a `git add -f`) before its `.agents/hooks.json` can be\n    tracked. Linked worktrees share the main clone's `info/exclude` (git's common dir), so they\n    inherit the narrowing from the clone they belong to. Where the file is not tracked, FR-5's\n2250→    check reads `UNWIRED reason=no-hooks-file` in any worktree that lacks it, so the gap is a halt,\n    not a silent pass.\n- **D-11 `review_independence_unrecorded` for every implementer (operator decision, 2026-10-16):**\n  widened from agy-only to `codex|agy|grok|claude`, matching D-9 (FR-7). Warning only, never a\n  block. Rationale: it fires only when the recording step was skipped, so a feature that follows\n  the new flow stays quiet; without it, a missing record would hide a Codex-on-Codex self-review,\n  which D-9 now flags when it IS recorded.\n  - **Pre-existing features — settled, no grandfather rule.** The check applies to every feature\n    whose Phase-7 check runs after this ships. Archived features are untouched because nothing\n    re-runs the check over them (FR-7). A grandfather rule would need a signal separating \"began\n2260→    before this shipped\" from \"skipped the recording step\", and the only signal the record offers\n    — absence of `review_independence` — is the skip itself, so any such rule would also silence\n    the case the warning exists for. A warning on an in-flight feature costs one backfilled entry\n    or one report line.\n- **D-12 Shared shell-policy module (was the design's OQ-A; operator decision, 2026-10-16):** the\n  FR-6 policy moves into the existing `h-mad/scripts/h_mad_target_identity.py`, not a new module.\n  The design's reason: `test_h_mad_codex_tdd_gate_judge.py` `test_codex_gate_judge_timeout_reason`\n  builds a tree by copying only the Codex gate, a judge stub and `h_mad_target_identity.py`\n  (`grep -n -A12 'def test_codex_gate_judge_timeout_reason' h-mad/tests/test_h_mad_codex_tdd_gate_judge.py`),\n  so a new module would be missing there, and FR-6 forbids editing that test.\n2270→- **D-13 FR-8 timing (was the design's OQ-B; operator decision, 2026-10-16):** FR-8 runs as a\n  post-merge stage after Phase 7's integrate, and the feature is recorded COMPLETE only after it.\n  Reason: the tracer must run the gate at its installed path, which is main.\n- **D-14 Leap-second `.ts` refused (orchestrator decision, design audit cycle 1, 2026-10-16):**\n  FR-1 narrows `implementer_switch.ts` to seconds `00`–`59`. Reason: state timestamps are written\n  by h-mad's own writer through Python `datetime`, which cannot produce a seconds field of `60`.\n- **D-15 Durable author record (OD-E2, operator decision, 2026-10-16: option (a)):** FR-1 gains\n  `implementation_authors`, an optional array of `{module, agent}` entries appended (never\n  removed or altered) through FR-7's Record mechanism, one per accepted 5e GREEN or accepted fix\n  dispatch and never for a 5d RED. FR-7 §\"Attribution\" reads a module's author set from it.\n2280→  Reason: design v1.2 (OD-E2) showed AC-7.3's \"multi-author, recovered\" fixture could not pass\n  without a durable author record, because the assembler's prompt, out and log files are under\n  `/tmp` and its slug names no agent. The fixture stays as written and now has its source. The\n  rejected option (b) was to amend the fixture to \"no entry\".\n- **D-16 Directory target refused under active step5 (OD-2, operator decision, 2026-10-16):** a\n  class-(b) target that names an existing directory is refused with `kind=judge-error` when the\n  scan reads `active` or `unknown`, and allowed when it reads `inactive` (FR-4 deliberate\n  difference 6). Reason: impl-plan v1.1 Task 6 showed that `canonicalise` returns no name and\n  no `unresolvable` flag for `.`, `src/`, `src` and the root, so the class-(b) sequence would\n  otherwise allow a directory target under `active`. Residual: a trailing-`/` path that names no\n2290→  existing directory is not covered and is judged as a file name.\n\n## Open questions for the operator\n\nNone — OQ-1/OQ-2 settled 2026-10-16; the design's OQ-A/OQ-B settled 2026-10-16 (D-12, D-13);\nthe design's OD-E2 settled 2026-10-16 (D-15); the impl-plan's OD-2 settled 2026-10-16 (D-16).\n\n## Non-goals\n\n- Per-task implementer selection. It can come later on the same field.\n2300→- Refusing a peer's production write in that peer's own gate when the other peer is the resolved\n  implementer, for example a Codex verifier dispatch writing production code under\n  `implementer=agy`. Residual: the test-first judge still applies, and the per-dispatch\n  `git status --short` diff (agy-runtime.md §\"Author and reviewer roles\") is what shows it.\n- A write-time gate for grok, and authorship rules for agy as the orchestrating host.\n- Routing audit legs by `agy_status`.\n\n## Owed by other documents\n\nNone of these are edited by this spec.\n2310→- `h-mad/references/agy-runtime.md` says there is no `SessionStart` on two surfaces:\n  - §\"Hooks\" (\"There is **no `SessionStart`**\");\n  - the §\"Construct mapping\" table's `hook-event` row (\"there is no `SessionStart`\").\n\n  agy's current hooks.md lists `SessionStart` as a supported event. Both surfaces need a re-check\n  against the installed agy (`agy --version` prints 1.4.0 at `200ffd52`), they are corrected\n  together, and the correction stamps the version it was checked against.\n  (`grep -n 'SessionStart' h-mad/references/agy-runtime.md` at `200ffd52`: 2 matching lines, one\n  under each heading.) Residual: this is a content predicate, so a third restatement added later\n  is found only by re-running the grep.\n2320→- **Design (v1.8 items).** FR-4: enumerate the closed kind list and give every deny path a member\n  (E6); its claim that `unknown` \"denies `kind=judge-error`, as the Codex gate does for a shell\n  command\" is false at `cff71b49` — the Codex gate's `unknown` shell deny carries no `kind=`\n  token. FR-7: name where the complete author set is kept (E2) and correct \"the GREEN dispatch the\n  orchestrator accepted at 5d\" to 5e (E4).\n- **Design (v1.9, D-15).** FR-1: add `implementation_authors` with FR-1's entry schema to the\n  schema JSON and a `state-schema.md` row. FR-7: retire \"the complete set is kept in the\n  procedure\" and OD-E2 in favour of reading the set from `implementation_authors`; the\n  `--append` mechanism (DO-3) must accept this key with the same guarantees, and the \"When the\n  dispatch list is lost\" rule becomes FR-7's three closed unrecoverable cases.\n2330→- **Design (spec v1.10).** FR-4: record D-16 as a directory check before the target is judged,\n  and add it to §\"Deliberate differences\" as item 6; FR-4 `--self-check`: a verdict `FAIL` exits\n  0 (replacing \"a nonzero exit\"), and the Codex gate's `FAIL shell-policy` is corrected in impl-plan\n  Task 4; FR-2: adopt the halts `step5:implementer_invalid` and `step5:implementer_unresolved`.\n- **Impl-plan.** AC-7.3's surface (FR-7 §\"Attribution\" must have code to drive, or the AC is an\n  OPEN-DECISION), now including the pre-field fixture; AC-1.3's fixtures; AC-4.1's `unknown`\n  fixtures and AC-4.2's per-deny-path kind fixtures.\n- **Plan.** If the plan carries FR-1's field list or AC-7.3's fixtures, it gains\n  `implementation_authors`, AC-1.3 and the pre-field fixture.\n\n2340→## Version History\n- v1.0: Initial spec (2026-10-16) from brainstorm v1.1. Settles 8 open questions (D-1..D-8): run_command takes the Codex command-shape rule, not a stated residual; per-project .agents/hooks.json; root from handler cwd; provisional 1500 s timeout then tracer formula; PATH+status+env availability; feature-only implementer; flag in state; audit legs unchanged. Folds brief fix 3 (#8) WIRE-PIN new-caller RED case as FR-9. OQ-1 (Codex-reviews-Codex flag), OQ-2 (.agents exclude) for the operator.\n- v1.1: Operator decisions 2026-10-16 (2026-10-16). OQ-1 -> D-9: FR-7 flag fires for any reviewer == implementer, Codex reviewing Codex included; warning only. OQ-2 -> D-10: operator narrowed .git/info/exclude /.agents/ to /.agents/skills/ (check-ignore evidence at d65374e9), no git add -f; per-clone residual stated. Open questions section now empty.\n- v1.2: Operator decision 2026-10-16 -> D-11: FR-7 review_independence_unrecorded widened from agy-only to every implementer (codex|agy|grok|claude), matching D-9; warning only. Trigger pinned to a 6a-prime leg that ran (archreview READY_TO_MERGE|WITH_FIXES|NO) with no leg=6a-prime entry; other archreview values already surface. Pre-existing features settled: no grandfather rule, archived features untouched (no batch caller). AC-7.2 added.\n- v1.3: Plan-author findings against v1.2 (2026-10-16). FR-3: a missing state-line field refuses judge-error (tree precedent: missing-field fixture), so the two gate stub-line fixtures are re-shaped with assertions byte-identical — the AC-3.2 exception; test_h_mad_tdd_judge.py ACTIVE_RE and line-quoting mutation rows follow the line. FR-7: writer has no append (--set replaces); add-without-loss is a requirement, mechanism a design obligation; AC-7.1 gains the two-entry fixture. FR-6: rows re-pointed to the shared module, then ALL_CAUGHT; moved names stay bound on the Codex gate module. FR-9/AC-9.1/FR-10: failure-recovery.md red_wrong_reason row; rule surfaces closed with sweep. FR-1/FR-10/AC-1.1: fallback_agent description on schema JSON and state-schema.md, pinned literals named. Owed: second SessionStart surface (Construct-mapping hook-event row). All ACs reformatted to the h_mad_ac_census.py grammar (- **AC-n.m:**).\n- v1.4: Plan audit cycle 1 findings owned by the spec (2026-10-16). F1 -> FR-5 exit-code table: AGY-GATE WIRED/UNWIRED (all seven reasons, no-hooks-file included) exit 0 and the assembler's agy_tdd_hook_unverified verdict HALT exits 0; UNREADABLE and a no-token check exit 2; existing assembler HALTs keep exit 2 (residual); AC-5.2. F2 -> FR-1: .ts is RFC 3339 date-time; neither validator path enforces format today (probed), so refusal on both paths is the requirement and the mechanism a design obligation; rfc3339-validator hazard; residual for started_ts/owner_heartbeat_ts/halt_ts; AC-1.2. F3 -> FR-9 differential corpus over four missing-symbol cases, changed set == {new-caller}; AC-9.2. F4 -> FR-9 incident replay of HemaSuite notebooklm F-3 (T4/T5 WIRE-PIN 1, T6 WIRE-PIN 2) with wire-cut re-run; AC-9.3. F5 -> FR-8 step 2a forced handler timeout with four classified outcomes; step 2 now settles two unknowns; FR-5 re-run trigger includes it; AC-8.2. F6 -> no contradiction; FR-3 now lists the real-line assertions (two in test_h_mad_tdd_gate_fallback_agent.py, four tests in test_h_mad_tdd_judge.py) as placement constraints; AC-3.2 extended.\n- v1.5: Plan audit cycle 2 findings owned by the spec, final corrective revision after the round cap (2026-10-16). B -> FR-10 adds the SKILL.md teammate-audit bullet: audit-leg selection vs resolved authorship (Claude only when resolved=claude), anchor prefix and the four pinned tokens kept. C -> FR-9 surface census gains surface 5, the h_mad_assemble_tdd.py assemble() wiring header (split string literals), and its pin test_h_mad_assemble_tdd.py TestWiringShape.test_the_pin_is_told_what_its_red_must_be; the census method now joins Python adjacent literals via ast and collapses markdown whitespace, measured at 9c3b799d; residual: run-time-built strings. AC-9.1 lists the header. D -> FR-9 replay and AC-9.3 name all three pins with files and commits (531a6296b, 7aa0d9dec, c2d671f6b); T5/T6 replay evaluates the direct-call / two-argument getattr form; the committed scaffolded RED (AssertionError) is recorded as old-verdict valid, correcting the false old-verdict=halt claim. A -> new AC-9.4: the six rule copies bound to one authority, with a per-copy control.\n- v1.6: Advisory delta review of plan v1.3 (608e430), spec-owned parts (2026-10-16). Should-fix 3 -> FR-9: the joined reading (ast str constants) is blind to non-str text in a .py file; axis named with three members (comments, files ast.parse rejects, bytes literals). The sweep is now the union of the line grep and the joined reading, which adds joined .py comment tokens; parse failures are reported as unparsed=N and read by hand; residual: run-time-built strings and a bytes literal split across lines. Measured at 5f72c2f3: union 7 files, line-grep-minus-joined 0, comment hits 0 (incidental), unparsed=0. AC-9.1 re-run is the union and reports unparsed=N. Must-fix 1 (spec side) and Should-fix 4 -> AC-9.4: the carve-out text is the whole three-sentence FR-9 block; each copy is read from its own source location, never from the assembled prompt or a file-level contains-check over a file holding more than one copy; a locator matching zero or several spans fails the test; the control mutates exactly one copy, runs once per copy and once per sentence in at least one copy, with a find unique in its file (mutation harness anchor_status), the .py find written against source bytes including the literal split.\n- v1.7: Design audit cycle 1, spec-owned items (2026-10-16). S1 (codex C1) -> FR-1/AC-1.1/AC-1.2 + D-14: .ts narrowed to seconds 00-59, a leap second (2016-12-31T23:59:60Z) refused on both paths; reason: every .ts comes from Python datetime via the writer, which cannot hold second 60 (orchestrator decision). S2 (codex C6) -> FR-7 Attribution: an entry's implementer is the author of the reviewed code, from its successful implementation dispatch, never implementer_switch.to (historical) or the implementer field; multi-author code is non-independent if the reviewer is any author. S3 -> FR-1/FR-10/AC-1.1: codex_status schema description and state-schema.md row rewritten to FR-2 authorship (no test pins the text; the fallback_agent-after-codex_status order pin stated); FR-8 Version: 1.4.0 at 200ffd52, each reading stamps its version; Owed SessionStart re-check re-stamped 1.4.0 at 200ffd52; FR-6/AC-6.1 shared module = h_mad_target_identity.py (D-12, OQ-A); FR-8/AC-8.1 post-merge stage, COMPLETE only after it (D-13, OQ-B). Open questions: OQ-A/OQ-B settled.\n- v1.8: Design audit cycle 2, final corrective revision after the round cap, not re-audited (2026-10-16). S1/E6 -> FR-4 'Every deny carries a kind': rule over every deny path, closed list owned by the design, spec names only judge-error, tool-not-admitted and the judge's Verdict kind; Codex gate has 6 of 13 kindless _deny call sites at cff71b49. E1 -> FR-4 'The activity reading': three-valued scan (active/unknown/inactive), every step5-gated deny also fires on unknown, class-(b) production .py still read through read_chain under inactive; workspacePaths and Tool classes sentences re-worded. E5 -> FR-4 Target keys: a non-empty-string value counts as no path key. S4 -> FR-4 'Deliberate differences from the Codex gate': design's four adopted plus the kind rule as a fifth. S2/E2/E4 -> FR-7 Attribution sub-bullets (5e GREEN + accepted fixes, never 5d RED; complete author set, never a projection) and new AC-7.3. S5 -> adopted: reviewer none of the authors names the latest accepted dispatch's author. S3 -> leap-second ValueError quote drops the version-dependent tail. AC-4.1/AC-4.2 extended; FR-6 states the unknown-scan shell deny; Owed gains design and impl-plan items.\n2350→- v1.9: Operator decision OD-E2 (a) (2026-10-16) -> D-15: FR-1 gains implementation_authors, an optional array of {module, agent} (agent codex|agy|grok|claude; module non-empty string; no extra keys), appended never altered through FR-7's Record mechanism on accepting a 5e GREEN or fix dispatch, never 5d RED or a halted/timed-out/rejected dispatch; duplicates kept, array order = acceptance order. New AC-1.3 (valid entries accepted, unknown agent and other malformed shapes refused, both validator paths). FR-7 Attribution reads the author set from the field on every leg; unrecoverable cases closed to three (field absent: pre-field features; no entry for the module: a record-step defect; malformed) -> no entry. AC-7.3 multi-author fixture unchanged with its source named; pre-field fixture added. AC-7.2 'only FR-1 field' narrowed to routing field. FR-10 SKILL.md Phase-5 step bullet. Owed: design FR-1/FR-7 (retire procedure-kept set and OD-E2), impl-plan AC-1.3 + pre-field fixture, plan mirror.\n- v1.10: Owed lines from impl-plan v1.1 (Phase 5b), final corrective revision, not re-audited (2026-10-16). P1 -> D-16 (OD-2, operator): a class-(b) target naming an existing directory denies kind=judge-error under active/unknown, allowed under inactive; FR-4 deliberate difference 6, AC-4.1 fixture; residual: trailing-/ path naming no existing directory. P2 -> FR-2 halts named: step5:implementer_invalid (invalid), step5:implementer_unresolved (no-record, unreadable), step5:implementer_switch_unrecorded (record=failed); adopted from impl-plan Task 16; failure-recovery.md rows not added (residual). P3 -> FR-5: the gate's --self-check verdict FAIL prints its token on stdout and exits 0, operational error nonzero; Codex gate FAIL shell-policy corrected in impl-plan Task 4; Codex FAIL parser residual; AC-5.2 gains the self-check fixture and mutant. P4 -> FR-9 Hook line keeps Claude's h-mad-tdd-gate.sh (test_hook_line_names_both_gates). Owed: design FR-4/FR-2 lines.\n\n\nAudit rubric (TWO axes, both mandatory):\n\nAxis A — Generic adversarial:\n- Contradictions inside the doc\n- Gaps (missing error paths, untestable AC, unstated assumptions)\n- Weak claims (load-bearing decisions without justification)\n2360→- Scope creep (work outside the stated feature boundary)\n\nAxis B — Invariant compliance (any breach = must-fix, you cannot downgrade).\nTwo layers, both binding:\n\n### Base invariants (workflow-universal; non-overridable by any project file — but the operator `## Acknowledged-not-fixed` sidecar still applies to base findings):\n\n# H-MAD Base Invariants — Axis B (workflow-universal)\n\n> Shipped with the `/h-mad` skill. The orchestrator inlines this file verbatim into the\n2370→> `INLINE_BASE_INVARIANTS` slot (name written bare — a bracketed mention inside an inlined\n> file survives substitution and reaches the reviewer as an unfilled-looking placeholder)\n> of every plan / design / impl-plan audit, **before** the\n> per-project `<PROJECT_ROOT>/.h-mad/invariants.md` domain layer. These rules apply to every\n> project that uses `/h-mad` and are **non-overridable by any project file**: a project's\n> invariants file may add domain rules but may not downgrade or delete a base rule. The\n> operator `## Acknowledged-not-fixed` sidecar escape hatch still applies to base findings\n> (a base item listed under that section in a sidecar `.audit.v<N+1>.md` is a conscious,\n> audited deferral — it is excluded from the gate count exactly like any other layer's item).\n>\n2380→> Axis A (generic adversarial review) is covered by the audit-prompt template — not repeated here.\n\n## Audit-gate signal discipline\n- Any gate/check whose verdict the orchestrator consumes MUST communicate PASS/FAIL via an\n  explicit **stdout token** and **exit 0** on a normal verdict (PASS or FAIL). Using a non-zero\n  process exit to mean \"FAIL\" is a violation — a non-zero exit registers as a Claude Code tool\n  failure (`PostToolUseFailure`) and leaks into coexisting plugins' error handling. A non-zero\n  exit is permitted ONLY for genuine operational errors (missing/unreadable input).\n\n## Single-source contract\n2390→- A rule applied by more than one surface (e.g. an audit blocking-item count read by both the\n  orchestrator gate step and a precondition checker) MUST have exactly one authoritative\n  implementation that all surfaces call, OR a test asserting byte-equivalence across surfaces.\n  Independent re-implementations that can silently diverge are a violation.\n- A contract stated in a template (e.g. an empty-section sentinel) MUST match what the consuming\n  gate logic treats as that case. Template guidance and gate behavior disagreeing is a violation.\n\n## Standalone / no plugin dependency\n- The skill MUST NOT acquire a **runtime dependency** on any other plugin (OMC, bkit,\n  context-mode, etc.) or external skill (spec-kit, b-mad, pdca). Coexistence accommodations\n2400→  (sharing a state filename, conforming doc structure to an external validator's required\n  sections) are allowed; *requiring another plugin installed at runtime* is a violation.\n\n## No new external dependency\n- Scripts MUST use only Python stdlib plus tooling already depended upon by the skill (the\n  agent dispatch substrate (cmux or orca, via `hmad-dispatch`); `jq` where the existing hook\n  already uses it; `pytest`).\n  Introducing a new third-party package or new CLI is a violation.\n- **Dispatched agent CLIs are not script dependencies.** `codex`, `agy` and `grok` are optional\n  runtime *agents*: reached only through a `hmad-dispatch` verb (`exec`, `send`, …), never imported,\n2410→  required, or invoked by a script or test for its own work — tests reach them through stubs, and an\n  absent agent CLI is a dispatch-time condition the verb reports, never a failure of the skill.\n  The list is closed: adding an agent to it is an operator decision recorded here, not something a\n  document or a dispatch may assume. (`grok` added 2026-09-28, operator decision on\n  `grok-codex-fallback` design audit v2.)\n\n## Portable time bounds\n- A time-bounded command MUST NOT be written as `timeout <s> <cmd>` or `gtimeout <s> <cmd>`.\n  **Neither is a macOS system component**, so the form is unportable in both directions.\n  A stock box ships neither and the call fails at 127; a box carrying `brew install coreutils`\n2420→  runs it fine and the bound silently rests on a CLI h-mad cannot assume is there.\n  **What your box has is not an input to this rule.** h-mad already owns a time-bounder that\n  reachable wherever `hmad-dispatch` is, so the form is forbidden unconditionally — not because\n  of what happens downstream. (Reachable, not universal: where the wrapper itself cannot be\n  called the rule below still applies — halt, do not drop the bound.) Do not reason from a local probe to an exemption. For anything committed\n  or dispatched the form is additionally a new external CLI dependency (§\"No new external\n  dependency\"); a downstream 127 is how that eventually surfaces, never the reason.\n  Do not settle the question by probing: a local `command -v timeout` that succeeds is not\n  licence, it proves only that this box has coreutils, and it means the 127 that would have\n  exposed the improvisation never fires. Use `hmad-dispatch run --timeout <s> -- <cmd...>`,\n2430→  which exits 124 at the deadline.\n- Where a 127 does fire, its aftermath is worse than the 127 itself. Measured: the reflex after `timeout: command not found`\n  is to re-run the same command **unbounded** and call it \"checking directly\". That does not fail\n  at the deadline, it hangs the phase, and in every log h-mad reads a hang and slow work are the\n  same bytes. A plan, a design, or a prompt that leaves an agent no reachable time-bounder is a\n  violation; the correct behaviour when none exists is to **halt**, not to drop the bound.\n\n## Doc-template superset compliance\n- Generated phase documents whose type is validated by an external doc-structure validator\n  (e.g. bkit PDCA: `plan` → `docs/01-plan/...`, `design` → `docs/02-design/...`,\n2440→  `report` → `docs/04-report/...`) MUST be supersets that satisfy that validator's required\n  sections AND retain the existing h-mad sections. Dropping an h-mad section or failing the\n  validator is a violation.\n- The contract binds the **saved document**, not only the template it came from. Where such a\n  validator reclassifies a document by a substring of its *content* — bkit's `isPlanPlus` promotes\n  a plan to a larger required-section list on seeing `Plan-Plus`, `Plan Plus`, `plan-plus`,\n  `Brainstorming-Enhanced`, or `Intent Discovery` anywhere in the file — a generated document\n  carrying that substring is a violation even though the template is clean. Guard it mechanically\n  (`scripts/h_mad_doc_shape_check.py`); the literals are unremarkable prose, so an author cannot\n  be expected to avoid them from memory.\n2450→\n## Operator-override preservation\n- The `## Acknowledged-not-fixed` sidecar override mechanism MUST remain functional for all\n  layers (base + project). Any gate change that causes overridden items to still count as\n  blocking, or that ignores the sidecar, is a violation.\n\n## Backward compatibility\n- A change to the audit gate MUST preserve the PASS verdict for audit docs that passed before\n  the change. Flipping a historically-passing audit to FAIL (absent a real new blocking item)\n  is a violation.\n2460→\n## Marker discipline\n- Orchestrator phase transitions and halts MUST emit `[H-MAD]` log markers so a run is\n  diagnosable from logs alone. Silent state transitions are a violation.\n\n## Mutation verification\n- A step that mutates state (a git operation, a file write, a remote/CLI call) MUST verify the\n  mutation by **re-reading the resulting state**; an **exit code is not evidence that a mutation\n  occurred**. A plan, design, or implementation that treats a zero exit — or an echoed success\n  string such as `Sent N bytes` or `{\"ok\":true}` — as proof of the intended effect is a violation.\n2470→- The failure this blocks is silent and looks exactly like success: two `zsh` no-ops (backtick\n  execution inside `-m`, a leading-dash path) both exited 0 while doing nothing, and a dispatch\n  reported `Sent 7293 bytes` into a dead pane. Where a command reports on its own behaviour, the\n  check must read the *thing it was supposed to change*, from a separate call.\n- **When several mutations target the SAME line, vary one field and keep the anchor shared.** A\n  guard whose failure has separable parts — exit code, stream routing, message content — needs one\n  mutation per part, each replacing the same anchor. That shape is what proves a content assertion\n  load-bearing: the mutants that keep exit and stream and strip only the text are exactly the ones a\n  returncode-only test survives. Two discriminators worth writing into the spec: a\n  first-vs-last-occurrence mutant survives whenever the sought item is last in BOTH regions, so the\n2480→  discriminating fixture must put the decoys between the markers and leave the tail empty; and\n  `survived` has four distinct causes — missing guard, equivalent mutant, weak test, and a mutant\n  that never ran — which the verdict token collapses into one word. Diagnose which before acting.\n\n## Test discrimination\n- A test or guard MUST be **observed failing against the unfixed code** before it is trusted.\n  For a regression test, revert the fix and re-run; for a guard, stub it to its permissive value\n  and re-run the suite. **Zero failures is a finding, not a reassurance** — it means the check is\n  unenforced, not that the code is safe. Keeping a check that has never been seen to fail is a\n  violation.\n2490→- A check that cannot fail is decoration, and it is worse than no check because it reports\n  coverage that does not exist. Two guards shipped green this way and were caught only by\n  stubbing: one passed solely because an unrelated helper stripped the env var its subject read,\n  and a documentation test passed with the documented guidance deleted, because both of its\n  component words already appeared in nearby prose. Neither was visible to review or to a green run.\n- The mutation must itself be verified (§\"Mutation verification\"): a `.replace()` that matches\n  nothing exits 0 and reports the guard as enforced.\n- **A criterion that cannot discriminate in EITHER direction is vacuous, and it survives both a\n  green run and a mutation battery.** The stronger case of the bullet above: not \"this check never\n  fails\" but \"this check returns the same observation whether the behaviour is present or absent\".\n2500→  Measured on AC-2.5b, which spied on `is_cli_available` *returning* `False` — the forced-`True`\n  mutation emits an identical record and returns the same value, so neither the passing run nor the\n  mutated run could tell the two states apart. AC-7.5c was the same shape. **Neither audit leg saw\n  either one**; the revising author found AC-2.5b while fixing an unrelated must. A document audit\n  reads prose and cannot ask \"does this pin discriminate?\", so the question has to be asked where the\n  criterion is written: **name the observation that differs between the implemented and unimplemented\n  states, and if you cannot name one, the criterion is decoration** — delete it or replace it with a\n  positive assertion only the implemented behaviour produces. Spying on a return value that both\n  states produce is the recurring form.\n- **Mutating a path-resolution function can disable the suite's own isolation.** Tests usually\n2510→  isolate by pointing an env override at a temp path, and that override is honoured by a branch —\n  the same kind of branch a mutation deletes. Stubbing the override branch in `_pin_file` redirected\n  every pin write in the suite onto the developer's live session file and replaced two real agent\n  handles, while the run reported 642 passed. Before mutating anything that decides *where* state is\n  written, snapshot the real target and restore it, or run in a sandboxed working directory.\n- **A stub must model the step the real system CONSUMES.** The rules above are about asserting the\n  right thing; this one is about the fixture telling the truth. A stub that replays state the real\n  system consumes once makes a test pass before the fix exists: an orca stub replayed an acked\n  delivery forever, so a sibling-cache test re-matched from the queue, pinned nothing, and passed\n  against unmodified code. Cardinality is part of the model — a fake that writes a path once where\n2520→  production writes it twice let a verb never dispatch with 57 tests green. Before trusting a stub,\n  ask which of its effects are destructive in production and make the stub destroy them too.\n\n## Verifying a review finding\n- **A review finding has three separable parts — facts, concern, prescription — and they fail\n  INDEPENDENTLY.** Before applying a prescription, diff it against the RED tests and the spec's\n  acceptance criteria. A finding that matches the design doc but breaks tests means the DESIGN\n  drifted, not the implementation. Measured: one pass's prescription broke a guard (1 failed, 53\n  passed) while its concern was entirely real, and another review's prescription was backwards.\n- Test a prescription by applying it as a mutation and reverting it. When two passes dissent, ask\n2530→  what the codebase ALREADY does — twice the house pattern beat both readings.\n\n## Guard narrowing\n- When a change **deliberately makes a guard accept something it used to reject**, the relaxation\n  MUST be shown to be *exactly* the intended case: run a corpus of inputs through the old and new\n  logic and diff the verdicts, then account for **every** input whose verdict softened. A green\n  suite is not evidence here — it encodes the cases someone already thought of, which is the wrong\n  population when the question is \"what else did this let through?\". This is the inverse of\n  §\"Mutation verification\": that proves a guard still bites, this proves a loosening did not widen.\n- The relaxation MUST rest on a **guarantee of the thing being parsed**, not on a heuristic that\n2540→  re-implements it. Narrowing a scanner by \"ignoring quoted regions\" means re-deriving the target\n  language's quoting rules in a regex, which is how a bypass gets introduced; narrowing it by a rule\n  the language itself guarantees is safe. Verify that guarantee **against the real interpreter**,\n  not its documentation (§\"Assumption verification\").\n- Both halves were load-bearing when this was written. Narrowing a heredoc scanner so it stopped\n  denying inert prose, a 23-input differential corpus flagged one case as a regression; running the\n  shape through real `bash` showed the body genuinely never expands, so the *test expectation* was\n  wrong and the code was right. Without the corpus that case ships as either an unnoticed hole or a\n  \"fix\" applied to correct behaviour — and nothing else would have distinguished them. The same run\n  confirmed the intended relaxations numbered exactly two, and that the remaining 21 verdicts were\n2550→  untouched.\n- A narrowing is scoped by **which checks consult the reduced input**, not by the reduction itself.\n  Exempting the quoted region from *one* class of check while every other class kept scanning the\n  full input is what kept the change from widening; a blanket exemption would have disabled\n  detections that were still correct for reasons unrelated to the false positive.\n\n## Connection enforcement\n- A task whose deliverable is a **connection** — a call site, an import, a registration, a route, a\n  flag or value propagated across a boundary — MUST ship a test that **fails when the connection\n  alone is removed and the callee is left intact**. A test that exercises the callee directly is not\n2560→  evidence that the callee is reached. Shipping a connection with no such test is a violation.\n- **A whole-module revert cannot establish this**, because it removes both sides: the RED split\n  returns identically for a wired and an unwired build. The same blindness runs through every other\n  layer — a RED phase goes red because the callee is *absent*, an anti-gaming audit finds a\n  callee-scoped unit test perfectly discriminating, and a review of the diff sees a call site that is\n  *present* and therefore reads as correct. **Presence is not enforcement.** Measured across two\n  consecutive wiring tasks: each shipped its single load-bearing decision untested through every\n  audit cycle and through the RED phase, and only a mutation scoped to the connection caught it.\n- Mutate the connection in **both directions**: remove it → the wire test must fail; force it to fire\n  unconditionally → the fall-through/negative test must fail. One direction certifies a connection\n2570→  that exists but is unconditional. Verify the mutation landed before trusting the run\n  (§\"Mutation verification\") — a revert that never happened reports as a pass.\n\n## Incident replay\n- A fix motivated by a specific observed incident MUST be **replayed against the real artifacts\n  already on disk** that motivated it, not only against cases authored alongside the fix.\n  **Synthetic cases alone are a violation** whenever such artifacts exist and are reachable.\n- Cases written next to a fix inherit the author's model of the bug, so they agree with it by\n  construction. A detector validated on 14 handcrafted samples rejected the real historical label\n  it was written to accept; the same replay then measured the true rate (7 of 13), which reclassified\n2580→  the defect from a one-off into the majority case. Replay is how a fix is told from a belief.\n\n## Assumption verification\n- Every **load-bearing assumption** in a plan or design — an API's accepted inputs, a command's\n  output shape, a boundary, a default — MUST be **executed as a throwaway command before it is\n  written into the design**, and the design **cites the observed output**. An assumption asserted\n  without evidence, where evidence was one command away, is a violation.\n- Design review cannot catch a wrong assumption: it reads as reasonable, and the implementation\n  and its tests are then both built from the same wrong model, so they agree with each other and\n  pass. Tracer-bulleting the assumptions of one feature confirmed a `--porcelain` boundary,\n2590→  confirmed a base-ref chain, and found a truncation hole — all before any code existed. Separately,\n  a selector grammar assumed from a wrapper's own code was wrong in a way that had already let a\n  destructive verb run unguarded.\n- The evidence belongs in the document, not only in the author's terminal. A cited output is\n  checkable by a reviewer; \"I verified this\" is not.\n- **Attributing a failure to a cause requires the controlled PAIR, not just the repro.** Run the\n  case with the blamed step and the case without it, and require the observable to differ. A repro\n  that reproduces confirms the symptom, never the cause. Measured four times: pane readiness was\n  blamed for an `injected:false` that a missing `--inject` flag fully explained, and that causality\n  shipped in a doc and a PR body; a title-only reading concluded \"no agents running\" and the\n2600→  operator corrected it; and two carried repros were each falsified by a control that removed the\n  step they blamed. Re-run the MEASUREMENT as well as the claim — a brief's conclusion can be right\n  while its method is wrong.\n\n- **A contract to RECOGNISE something needs a real instance of it, captured from the running\n  system, before any gate scores it clean** — a banner, a log line, a response shape.\n  `pin-agents-tail-banner` passed 2663 tests, 49 mutations and 53 audit cycles while its matcher\n  hit 0 of 5 real agent banners: all 12 corpus positives were written by hand. The fixture corpus must include at\n  least one captured sample with its capture command cited; an idealised fixture is not a sample.\n\n2610→## Behavioural premises carry their command\n- A premise about **behaviour** — \"X currently does Y\", \"this guard fires when Z\", \"the suite\n  collects N\", \"the parser rejects W\" — MUST carry the exact command that produced it, inline and\n  runnable, beside the observed output. An output recorded without its command is not\n  re-derivable, and a premise that cannot be cheaply re-run gets re-READ instead, cycle after\n  cycle, by every reviewer in turn. Measured: one false behavioural premise survived **10** audit\n  cycles that way.\n- Narrower than §\"Assumption verification\" and it fails at a different moment: there the evidence\n  was never gathered; here it was gathered once, written down as a conclusion, and thereby made\n  uncheckable. Both readers are then reasoning about prose.\n2620→- **The command belongs in the document even when the answer is one number**, because the number\n  alone does not say what was counted. Measured: two readers measuring \"the same\" census got **3**\n  and **23**, having run different commands, and neither could tell which was wrong until the\n  command was written down beside it. `git grep -l '```' -- '*.py'` is the census; \"21 files\" is\n  a claim about one.\n- A premise whose command has become expensive to re-run is not exempt. Say so, and say what the\n  cheap proxy is — an unrunnable premise is a premise no later cycle can falsify.\n\n## Counts a dispatch reports\n- **Re-derive every COUNT a dispatched agent reports; never carry it into a document, a commit\n2630→  message or a verdict on the agent's word.** A report that is right about its actions can still be\n  wrong about its numbers, and the two failures look identical from outside — the prose is accurate,\n  the figures are confident, and nothing in the report signals which is which.\n- Measured 2026-08-26: an `exec codex` report stated an anchor sweep as `mutations=238` when the\n  tree held **244**, with every spec byte-identical to HEAD and every other figure in the same\n  report checking out. Nothing had changed; the number was invented. The cheap control existed the\n  whole time — `--check-anchors` prints the count in about a second.\n- This is narrower than §\"Assumption verification\" and it applies at a different moment: there the\n  risk is an assumption written without evidence, here it is evidence that was produced, looks\n  right, and is false. Prefer a count you can re-derive with one command over any count a\n2640→  dispatch hands you, and when a count is load-bearing for a verdict, re-derive it before recording\n  the verdict rather than after.\n\n## Wrapper–runtime reconciliation\n- A wrapper verb over an **external runtime's CLI** MUST be exercised **live against that runtime**\n  — a full create → list → remove cycle, or the verb's own equivalent round trip — before it ships,\n  and its **output-key extraction fixed against the observed envelope**. Shipping a verb whose\n  response shape is known only from stub fixtures is a violation, however green the suite is.\n- Stub fixtures encode the author's model of the envelope, so the wrapper and its tests agree with\n  each other and both are wrong together — the Incident-replay gap, applied to response parsing.\n2650→  Four occurrences: a create-response `.id` read from the envelope (a per-request correlation uuid\n  that always exists, so it yielded a plausible but useless handle) in two different verbs; then\n  probing a live response for a delivery-id field surfaced `run_required` — the whole structured\n  path had been **dead at its first mutation**, and no stub test could see it because the stub had\n  no concept of a Run binding. Later the same day the real ack key proved to be `deliveryId` while\n  the extraction chain led with `delivery_id`, and the only test covering that loop pinned the\n  spelling the runtime never sends.\n- The live probe is what finds these, not the review: each was invisible to design review, to the\n  suite, and to a careful reading of the vendor guide. Where a live run is impossible (destructive\n  verb, no credentials), say so in the doc and name what stayed unverified — an unmeasured shape is\n2660→  an open risk, not a pass.\n\n## Regression provenance\n- When a change makes an existing test fail, the plan or design MUST establish whether that test\n  **asserts current behaviour that the change is fixing** before proposing to edit it. **Changing an\n  existing test to pass is a violation unless** the doc states that the test pinned a defect (or\n  stale contract) as correct, and cites what it asserted.\n- The reflex \"make the failing test pass\" preserves the bug when the test *was* the bug. Three tests\n  in one session asserted the defect as an acceptance criterion — a forwarded selector a live\n  runtime rejects, a create-response handle the pane never has, a cwd-relative path — and each would\n2670→  have survived a naive \"adjust the test\" edit. A red pre-existing test is evidence about the test as\n  often as about the change.\n\n## Both halves of a doc change\n- Removing a documented instruction, flag, or capability MUST be paired with **the executable\n  replacement landed** (or an explicit statement that the capability is intentionally dropped, with\n  the reason). A test asserting only that the old text is *gone* passes for a deletion that silently\n  lost the capability.\n- An unexecutable instruction was deleted in one session; a test checking only its absence would\n  have gone green while the run lost the ability it named. The gate is: assert the new path works in\n2680→  the same change that removes the old one.\n\n## Reimplementation parity\n- Replacing a third-party dependency (validator, parser, formatter) with an in-tree implementation\n  MUST ship a **differential test asserting identical results against the original** across a corpus\n  that covers every construct in use, AND against the **real artifacts on disk**. Shipping the\n  reimplementation with tests that exercise only itself is a violation.\n- A hand-rolled reimplementation is worth exactly what its differential test catches. A bundled\n  JSON-Schema validator agreed with the library on a construct-complete corpus and on the live state\n  records; the traps it had to match (annotation-only `format`, `bool` is not `integer`) were\n2690→  invisible without the library beside it. Parity on a synthetic corpus alone repeats the\n  Incident-replay gap: the corpus shares the author's model of the reimplementation.\n\n---\n\n## How agy uses this file\nagy reads this verbatim as the **base** portion of the Axis B rubric, before the project layer.\nAny finding that violates a base rule is auto-classified `## Must-fix` and cannot be downgraded.\n\n\n2700→### Project invariants (domain rules for this repository):\n\n# H-MAD Project Invariants — Axis B (BrightGold70/skills repo, domain layer)\n\n> Inlined by `/h-mad` as the **project (domain)** portion of Axis B, AFTER the\n> workflow-universal `invariants.base.md`. The base layer already enforces the\n> universal rules (audit-gate signal discipline, single-source contract,\n> no-plugin-dependency, doc-template superset compliance, operator-override\n> preservation, backward-compatibility, marker discipline) — they are NOT repeated\n> here. This file holds only rules specific to this repository (a collection of\n2710→> Claude Code skills: `SKILL.md` + `references/*.md` + `scripts/*.py` + `hooks/*.sh`).\n\n## Skill self-containment\n- A skill MUST remain runnable from a bare clone: no import of another skill's\n  internals, no hardcoded path outside the skill's own directory (except documented\n  host install locations: `~/.claude/...` for Claude Code, `~/.agents/skills/...` for\n  Codex, `~/.gemini/config/skills/...` for the Antigravity CLI (agy), each overridable\n  by its documented environment variable). Cross-skill coupling is a violation.\n  (Operator decision 2026-09-28, multi-host-runtime.)\n\n2720→## Skill manifest integrity\n- Every skill's `SKILL.md` MUST carry valid frontmatter with `name` and `description`.\n  A skill whose entry behavior is changed without updating its `SKILL.md` contract is\n  a violation.\n\n---\n\n## How agy uses this file\nagy reads this as the domain portion of Axis B, after the base layer. Domain-rule\nviolations are `## Must-fix`. If this repo has no domain-specific concern for a given\n2730→feature, this layer may legitimately add nothing beyond the base rules.\n\n\nAxis C — Spec reconciliation.\n\nThe spec is the source of truth for what this feature must do. A plan or design\nis derived from it and may legitimately argue for a narrower reading — but it\nmay not do so silently, because every downstream phase then measures the\nimplementation against a spec the design already walked away from.\n\n2740→Read the spec above and reconcile it against the target document **by\nidentifier**, not by impression:\n\n**Design audits — every acceptance criterion.** For each `AC-N.M` in the spec,\nclassify the design as exactly one of:\n\n| Classification | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `implemented-as-written` | the design covers the AC in its spec form |\n| `restated` | the design covers it in a different, usually narrower form |\n2750→| `absent` | the design does not address it at all |\n\n**Plan audits — every functional requirement.** Same three classifications at\n`FR-N` granularity. A plan is a strategy document and is not expected to restate\neach AC; it *is* expected to address every FR or explicitly defer it.\n\nReporting rules:\n\n- For every `restated` item, **quote both forms** — the spec's wording and the\n  design's — so the divergence is auditable rather than asserted. Then say which\n2760→  is narrower and in what respect.\n- `restated` and `absent` are **must-fix**. This is not a judgement that the\n  design is wrong: a narrowing may be well-argued and may be the right call. It\n  is a judgement that the divergence must be explicit and must land in the spec\n  before the gate clears, so the operator decides deliberately rather than\n  discovering it at verification.\n- An AC whose narrowing the spec **already reflects** is\n  `implemented-as-written`, not `restated`. That is the loop closing correctly.\n- Do not infer coverage from a section heading or a passing reference in a risk\n  or rationale table. An AC is covered when the document says what will satisfy\n2770→  it.\n\nReport Axis C as a table in your `## Summary`, then raise each `restated` or\n`absent` item as its own `## Must-fix` bullet.\n\nEffort contract (binding on you either way; MEASURED only on some dispatches):\n\n- **Sweep the whole document. Do not stop at the first blocking finding.** Measured\n  over 83 design / 74 plan / 34 impl-plan cycles on one feature: the median pass\n  returned **one** must-fix against a prompt asking for all of them, which turns every\n2780→  defect into its own round trip and is most of why those numbers are that large.\n- **Read the tree before asserting what is in it.** The documents are inlined above,\n  which is exactly why a pass can look thorough having opened nothing. Their claims are\n  *about* the tree — a `path:symbol`, a signature, a test name, a count against its own\n  list — and those are the claims that turn out to be wrong.\n- **When you re-derive a published figure, four things must match or your reading answers a\n  different question. Each has been got wrong here by an auditor, not only by the orchestrator:**\n  - *Commit.* Presence at a sha is not provenance. \"X was introduced in revision N\" needs the\n    needle PRESENT at N's landing commit **and ABSENT at N-1's** — two readings return N and\n    N+1 alike. `git rev-list <base>..<sha> -- <path>` first: if that commit does not touch the\n2790→    file, it is not the figure's provenance.\n  - *Corpus.* **Run the command the document DEFINES, not one that reproduces the document's\n    number.** A head-scoped series verified whole-blob agreed for four shas and then did not;\n    the gating auditor re-ran it, reported it reproduced, and the scope was wrong the whole\n    time. Agreement with a published value is evidence about arithmetic, never about scope.\n  - *Grammar.* `grep -c` answers \"how many lines contain this string\", never \"how many\n    instances of this concept exist\". Print the matching lines and classify them before you\n    publish a count — a Python kwarg and a diagnostic field spell identically. Over markdown,\n    collapse newlines and admit the inline-code delimiters (`` ` ``): a needle that cannot\n    match across a backtick reported 2 sites where there are 4.\n2800→  - *Shell.* Take each control in its own invocation; a `cd` in a chained command is not\n    scoped to that command.\n- **If a document publishes a screen with `# expect 0`, RUN IT** rather than reporting that it\n  is present. Three sheet entries certified \"no scoped census moved\" over a commit at which the\n  document's own trip-wire read 8.\n- **An ABSENCE claim is a figure.** \"No corpus instance …\", \"the corpus has none\", \"nothing in\n  this repository\" — hold each to the same command and sha as a count; both that shipped here\n  were false. A zero bounded only by some *unrelated* constraint is a defect: the document says\n  why it is zero and whether that reason is load-bearing.\n- **A stated PROPERTY of a screen is a claim about code — execute it.** \"No working-file edit can\n2810→  move this\" was false of the shipped line. Do the thing it says cannot move it, and re-run. A\n  control over an ALTERNATION tests the alternation, never its branches: a healthy sibling covers\n  a sick one (one boundary repair was half-applied three rounds running and passed every time).\n- **Your report is held to this too.** Every count carries its UNIT (`grep -n` lines are not\n  occurrences); a population you prescribe is derived once and cited twice; and your PRESCRIPTION\n  is a claim about the tree — one prescribed naming an enclosing function for a line that has\n  none. Check it or mark it unchecked, and give every alternative repair rather than the best-\n  reading one.\n- **A pass at or below two successful tool calls cannot certify a clean.** Writing the\n  report and its marker costs those two by itself, so at that floor nothing was read.\n2820→  When your transcript is captured — an `audit-cycle` dispatch — the combiner enforces\n  this and scores the cycle `UNVERIFIED` rather than clean. When it is not captured,\n  **nothing measures you and the rule binds you anyway**: say what you read. Findings\n  count at any effort; this bounds what a *clean* is worth, never what a finding is.\n- **State your evidence** on its own line in `## Summary`: `Evidence: <N> files opened,\n  <M> greps run.`\n\nOutput framing (mandatory — the orchestrator extracts on these markers):\n\nEmit your report bracketed by these two lines, each alone on its line, with\n2830→nothing before the first or after the second:\n\n    AUDIT-agy-coder-fallback-plan-v1-BEGIN\n    ...your report...\n    AUDIT-agy-coder-fallback-plan-v1-END\n\nYour report is read by scraping this terminal, and the previous cycle's report\nis usually still visible above the prompt. The markers are how the orchestrator\ntells your new report from that old one — omit them and the audit cannot be\nscored. Emit them even if you have nothing to report.\n2840→\nOutput schema (exact, no other top-level sections):\n\n## Summary\n<2-3 sentences>\n\n## Must-fix\n- <issue> — <why it breaks invariant or creates a hard gap>\n  *(empty section: write the single word `None` on its own line — NOT a `- None` bullet; a leading `- ` would be counted as a blocking item)*\n\n2850→## Should-fix\n- <issue> — <why it matters but isn't a hard gate>\n  *(empty section: write the single word `None` on its own line — NOT a `- None` bullet; a leading `- ` would be counted as a blocking item)*\n\n## Nit\n- <style/clarity issue>\n  *(empty section: write the single word `None` on its own line — NOT a `- None` bullet; a leading `- ` would be counted as a blocking item)*\n\nClass rule (Must-fix and Should-fix): **every bullet carries a `class:` continuation line**, `build` or\n`measurement`, under the bullet and never as a `- ` bullet of its own (a leading `- ` is a second\n2860→finding). The test is one question: **would the code or tests a 5d/5e implementer writes differ if this finding were fixed?** Yes → `class: build` (an interface, a contract, a\npredicate, a fixture, a test's assertion or RED/GREEN count, a false premise about the platform). No →\n`class: measurement` (a published number, stamp, ledger row, sha series, pointer or self-count that is\nstale or mis-derived; the tree is unchanged by fixing it). An untagged bullet is scored as `build`, so\nomitting the line never softens a finding — it only costs the orchestrator the triage. Put the `class:` line directly under its bullet (before or after `quote:`); do not indent a sub-bullet between a finding and its `class:` line, which leaves the finding untagged.\n\n    - <issue> — <why>\n      class: build\n      quote: <path/to/file.md> › `<span copied verbatim from that file>`\n\n2870→Quoting rule (Must-fix and Should-fix): **if a finding asserts what a document says, prove it.**\nAdd an indented `quote:` line under that bullet naming the file and carrying the span copied\nverbatim out of it:\n\n    - <issue> — <why it breaks the invariant>\n      quote: <path/to/file.md> › `<span copied verbatim from that file>`\n\nThe `quote:` line is a continuation of its bullet, never a new `- ` bullet — a leading `- ` would\nbe scored as a second finding. Use one `quote:` line per document the finding names.\n\n2880→This exists because backticks alone are ambiguous. Reviewers correctly backtick three other kinds\nof span — code they are **proposing** as the fix, test inputs they **constructed**, and commands\nthey **ran** — and those legitimately do not occur in the document. Measured over 37 must-fixes:\n43% of *real* findings carry a backticked span absent from the document, so absence alone proves\nnothing and no automatic check can be built on it. `quote:` is the marker that separates a claim\nabout the document from everything else you are entitled to write in backticks.\n\nA finding that asserts document content with no `quote:` line is not rejected automatically, but it\nis the first thing the orchestrator re-derives from source — and a `quote:` span that does not occur\nin the file it names is a finding withdrawn, not a finding to argue.","absolute_path":"/Users/kimhawk/.grok/sessions/%2FUsers%2Fkimhawk%2F.local%2Fstate%2Fh-mad%2Fgrok-audit-surface%2Frun-ab2c85dc2e25-20261024T132452Z%2Froots%2Fgas-root-d7-s2v1-jev07wj7/01a16e78-8aa5-7402-bb74-bd092272bd65/prompts/prompt_0.txt","offset":1890,"limit":1000,"raw_output":"    consecutive `#` lines is seen only when the comment text is joined. So the sweep also reads\n    each `.py` file's comment tokens (`tokenize`, with the `#` stripped), joined and\n    whitespace-collapsed.\n  - **A file that `ast.parse` rejects.** It is never skipped silently. The sweep reports it by name\n    with a count (`unparsed=N`), its line-grep and comment readings still apply, and a nonzero\n    count means the file is read by hand before the sweep counts as clean.\n  - **Bytes literals** (`b\"…\"`) are not `str` constants. The line grep covers them on one line.\n    A bytes literal split across lines is the residual of this axis.\n\n  **The sweep is therefore the union** of the line grep over every tracked file and the joined\n  reading (the `.py` `str` constants, the joined `.py` comment text, and the whitespace-collapsed\n  text of every other file). One measurement at `5f72c2f3` (h-mad last changed at `083a8233`):\n  joined reading 7 files, line grep 6 files, line grep minus joined reading 0 files, union\n  7 files; comment-token hits 0 files, joined-comment hits 0 files, `unparsed=0`. The comment\n  zero is incidental: no comment in `h-mad/` restates the rule today. It is not a property of the\n  sweep, and it does not license dropping the comment reading.\n\n  Residual: a later surface that restates the rule is not covered until this sweep is re-run. A\n  phrase built at run time (an f-string field, `%`/`.format` interpolation, or a `+` join of names\n  rather than literals) is not a `str` constant, so no reading in the sweep sees it.\n  Such a surface is found only by reading the code that builds the dispatch text. A bytes literal\n  split across lines is the other residual, as stated above.\n\n- **The relaxation is shown to be exactly the intended case (differential corpus).** The carve-out\n  makes the 5d RED check accept a RED it used to halt, so `h-mad/invariants.base.md` §\"Guard\n  narrowing\" applies. The rule has no code implementation. At `e7730f78`,\n  `git ls-files h-mad | grep -v '\\.md$' | xargs grep -ln 'red_wrong_reason\\|missing.symbol'`\n  hits only two test files (`test_h_mad_wire_pin_gate.py` and `test_h_mad_wiring_task_shape_docs.py`),\n  and no script. So the corpus runs over **RED evidence**, not over a function. Each case is\n  a fixture repository with a RED base, a WIRE-PIN test and the real pytest RED it produces. The\n  four kinds of missing symbol are:\n\n  | case | missing symbol in the RED | callee at the RED base | old verdict | new verdict |\n  |---|---|---|---|---|\n  | `new-caller` | the caller the task creates | resolves | halt `red_wrong_reason` | **valid** |\n  | `missing-callee` | the callee | absent | halt | halt |\n  | `both-missing` | the caller the task creates (the callee is also absent) | absent | halt | halt |\n  | `unrelated-import` | a symbol the task neither creates nor calls (for example an unrelated module failing to import) | resolves | halt | halt |\n\n  - For each case the probe records the RED's exception line, whether the callee resolves at\n    the RED base (shown by importing it at that commit, not by reading the source), and the\n    verdict under the old rule text and under the new one. The design names how a verdict is\n    derived from rule text: either a classifier function that both texts' prose describes, or a\n    stated derivation per case.\n  - **Pass:** the set of cases whose verdict changed is exactly `{new-caller}`. Any other member\n    is a widening, and it is fixed in the rule text, never in the table.\n  - The four rows are one per kind of missing symbol on the RED. The residual axis is the\n    exception type: the corpus produces each case's RED as the exception the fixture actually\n    raises, and a case whose RED is `NameError` is a fifth fixture only when the new rule's text\n    treats `NameError` differently from `ImportError`/`AttributeError`.\n- **Incident replay against the real artifacts.** The carve-out was motivated by a real incident,\n  so `h-mad/invariants.base.md` §\"Incident replay\" applies, and the synthetic corpus above does not\n  replace the replay. The artifacts are in the HemaSuite checkout (`/Users/kimhawk/orca/HemaSuite`):\n  - `docs/archive/2026-10/notebooklm-integration-interface/notebooklm-integration-interface.analysis.md`,\n    item **F-3 — WIRE-PIN RED on a new caller (T4/T5/T6 WIRE 2)**. It names the cases and their\n    wire-cut evidence: M4.1, M5.1 and the WIRE 2 inline revert;\n  - the same directory's `notebooklm-integration-interface.impl-plan.md`, whose WIRE-PIN table\n    maps each case to its pin and mutation row;\n  - the three pins, each in `hematology-paper-writer/tests/` at its task commit:\n\n    | case | pin (test file `::` test) | task commit (subject locator) |\n    |---|---|---|\n    | T4 WIRE-PIN 1 | `test_notebooklm_resolver.py::test_resolver_calls_registry_arm_notebook_id_once` | `531a6296b` (`git log --all --oneline --grep='Task 4 FR-1 resolver'`) |\n    | T5 WIRE-PIN 1 | `test_notebooklm_initialize.py::test_initialize_entry_point_resolves_through_registry_arm` | `7aa0d9dec` (`--grep='Task 5 FR-5 initialize'`) |\n    | T6 WIRE-PIN 2 | `test_notebooklm_status_report.py::test_status_entry_point_resolves_through_registry_arm` | `c2d671f6b` (`--grep='Task 6 CLI surface'`) |\n\n    Each task commit's parent is its RED base. The pin names come from the impl-plan's\n    `**WIRE-PIN N**:` lines, and each file was checked with `git show --stat <commit>`.\n\n  **Which form of the pin the replay evaluates.** The committed pins do not share one form:\n  - T4 looks the new caller up with two-argument `getattr(integration, \"resolve_registry_notebook\")`.\n    At the RED base this raises `AttributeError`, so the committed pin's RED is a missing symbol.\n  - T5 and T6 use the impl-plan's \"WIRE-PIN RED scaffold\". It is a three-argument\n    `getattr(..., None)` (T5: `getattr(integration, \"initialize_project_notebook\", None)`; T6:\n    `getattr(integration.NotebookLMIntegration(), \"generate_setup_report\", None)`) inside\n    `except (Exception, SystemExit)`, followed by an assertion on the spy. Their committed RED is\n    an `AssertionError`, which the old rule already accepts.\n\n  So for T5 and T6 the replay evaluates the **direct-call form** of the pin: the scaffold is\n  replaced by a two-argument `getattr` on the same name (or a direct call), the `try`/`except` is\n  removed, and the spy assertion is unchanged. That is the form whose RED is the missing caller,\n  and it is the form the carve-out is about. The replay also records each committed pin's own RED,\n  for the record. For T4 the committed pin is already in the direct-call form.\n\n  For each of the three cases, the replay:\n  1. reproduces the direct-call form's RED at the RED base, with the pin from the task commit\n     (rewritten as above for T5 and T6). The RED must be `AttributeError`/`ImportError` on the\n     caller, and the callee must resolve at the RED base (shown by import);\n  2. applies the new rule to that RED and records its verdict, which must be **valid**;\n  3. records the old rule's verdict on that RED, which must be halt. For T5 and T6 it also records\n     the committed scaffolded pin's RED (`AssertionError`) and the old verdict on it, which is\n     **valid**: the scaffold is how those tasks avoided the halt;\n  4. re-executes the wire-cut at the task commit with the committed pin: delete the call only, keep\n     the caller and the callee, and confirm the pin fails. It also locates the original evidence\n     (M4.1, M5.1, the WIRE 2 revert) and cites it beside the re-run.\n\n  - The replay is a committed probe under `docs/03-analysis/probes/agy-coder-fallback/`, stamped\n    with this repository's sha and the HemaSuite sha it read.\n  - A case whose RED does not reproduce, or whose original evidence cannot be located, is recorded\n    with the command that failed. It is never dropped from the count.\n  - **Residual.** The locators are commit subjects and archive paths. If HemaSuite rewrites\n    history or moves the archive, re-locate with `git log --all --grep='notebooklm-integration-interface'`.\n\n### FR-10 — Documentation surfaces\n\n- **SKILL.md** §\"Codex authors Phase 5 — enforced, not just instructed\" becomes implementer-neutral\n  and states FR-2/FR-3. §\"Exit-code dispatch for 5d/5e\" lists `exec agy` as an implementer\n  dispatch beside its review role.\n- **`h-mad/references/agy-runtime.md`** §\"The TDD gate\" names the new gate and FR-5's check, and keeps\n  the halt.\n- **`h-mad/references/state-schema.md`** gains the FR-1 rows. Its existing `fallback_agent` and\n  `codex_status` rows are rewritten, and so are the schema JSON's `fallback_agent` and\n  `codex_status` `description`s, all four per FR-1.\n- **`h-mad/references/failure-recovery.md`** gets the 5d `red_wrong_reason` row carve-out per\n  FR-9.\n- **SKILL.md, the Phase-5 orchestrator steps:** the `implementation_authors` append on accepting\n  a 5e GREEN or fix dispatch, and the attribution read from it before each review leg, per FR-1\n  and FR-7 (D-15).\n- **SKILL.md, Phase 7 closure step:** the report carries every `WARNING` line that\n  `h_mad_phase7_preconditions.py` prints, the FR-7 warnings included.\n- **SKILL.md §\"Teammate audit leg — when codex is unavailable\"**, the bullet that begins\n  `- **It does not license Claude to write Phase 5 production code`. Today it says \"but the\n  declaration you just made does\". Under FR-2 that is false when agy is available: the\n  declaration routes the audit leg, but it moves Phase-5 authorship to agy, and FR-3 refuses\n  Claude (`agy-authorship`). The rewrite keeps the anchor prefix, which\n  `test_h_mad_tdd_gate_docs.py` `_skill_gate_bullet` requires exactly once, and the tokens\n  `ACTIVE`, `every`, `unavailable` and `exhausted`, which\n  `test_skill_gate_bullet_states_the_every_record_rule` requires. It states two things separately:\n  - **Audit-leg selection.** `codex_status` alone routes the 5b audit to the teammate leg.\n  - **Resolved authorship.** Claude may write Phase-5 production code only when FR-2's `resolved`\n    is `claude` for every ACTIVE `step5` record on the chain. That means both peers are\n    unavailable and `fallback_agent` is absent, `null` or `claude`.\n  The replaced condition is \"the `codex_status` declaration licenses Claude\". The new condition is\n  \"`resolved` = `claude`\", and the bullet names `h_mad_tdd_judge.py implementer` as the way to read\n  it.\n- **Audit legs in Phases 3/4/5b are unchanged.** `codex_status` still routes the second audit\n  surface to the teammate leg (SKILL.md §\"Teammate audit leg — when codex is unavailable\").\n  `agy_status` has no audit-routing consumer.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- **AC-1.1:** The schema accepts each FR-1 field at each listed value and rejects a value outside\n  it. A record with none of the fields validates unchanged. The `fallback_agent` description in\n  the schema JSON and its `state-schema.md` row state FR-2's both-peers condition. The\n  `codex_status` description and row no longer say that an unavailable Codex permits Claude to\n  author, and state authorship as FR-2's resolution. `test_h_mad_state_fallback_agent.py` stays\n  green. For `implementer_switch.ts`, \"the listed value\" is FR-1's narrowed profile, leap second\n  excluded (D-14); its fixtures are AC-1.2's.\n- **AC-1.2:** A record whose `implementer_switch.ts` is `not-a-date`, `2026-10-16`,\n  `2026-02-30T00:00:00Z` or the leap second `2016-12-31T23:59:60Z` (D-14) is refused on the library path and, under the test seam that forces it,\n  on the bundled path. Each value is its own fixture, run once per path. A `.ts` in the writer's\n  form `%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ` is accepted on both paths. If the mechanism uses the library's format\n  checker, one fixture runs with `rfc3339_validator` made unimportable and still refuses\n  `not-a-date`.\n- **AC-1.3 (D-15):** `implementation_authors` is pinned on the library path and, under the same\n  test seam as AC-1.2, on the bundled path; each fixture runs once per path:\n  - accepted: `[]`; one entry per agent `codex`, `agy`, `grok`, `claude`, each its own fixture;\n    and `[{\"module\":\"m\",\"agent\":\"agy\"},{\"module\":\"m\",\"agent\":\"codex\"},{\"module\":\"m\",\"agent\":\"agy\"}]`\n    (duplicates and order kept);\n  - refused, each its own fixture: an unknown agent (`\"agent\":\"gemini\"`), a missing `agent`, a\n    missing `module`, `\"module\":\"\"`, an extra key, a non-object entry, and a non-array value;\n  - a record with no `implementation_authors` validates unchanged.\n  An append of a refused entry through the Record mechanism leaves the store byte-identical.\n- **AC-2.1:** The resolution function: for every combination of {`implementer` absent / `codex` /\n  `agy` / invalid} × {`codex_avail`, `agy_avail` ∈ true/false}, `resolved` and `switch` match FR-2.\n  Each switch reason is produced by its own fixture, with only that cause present.\n- **AC-2.2:** The `implementer` verb prints exactly one line for each of the six token rows, and\n  for `record=failed`. `--record` writes `implementer_switch` once and does not rewrite it on an\n  identical re-run.\n- **AC-3.1:** Each of the five gate-specific refusal kinds is produced by its own fixture. In\n  addition, `implementer-invalid` outranks `codex-authorship`, a peer refusal outranks\n  `fallback-invalid`, and the chain fold blocks on the FIRST eligible record.\n- **AC-3.2:** The `codex-authorship`, `fallback-grok` and `fallback-invalid` message bytes equal\n  those at `a8d9ba99`. Every existing `test_h_mad_tdd_gate_*.py` stays green, and the only change\n  allowed is FR-3's stub-line exception. In the two files FR-3 names, a string literal that stubs\n  a `TDD-STATE: active` line, and the constants it is composed from, are re-shaped to the new\n  line. Every assertion, expected kind, reason substring, test name and parametrisation id stays\n  byte-identical (checked by diffing the file against `a8d9ba99`). Every other\n  `test_h_mad_tdd_gate_*.py` is unmodified. A new fixture shows that a line in today's shape now\n  refuses `judge-error`. In `test_h_mad_tdd_judge.py`, `ACTIVE_RE` is the only changed literal, and\n  the real-line assertions FR-3 lists stay byte-identical and green (checked by the same diff).\n- **AC-4.1:** With the scan reading `inactive`, the agy gate allows every tool, except that a\n  class-(b) production `.py` target whose chain reads `unreadable` still denies (the `chmod 000`\n  `docs/` chain fixture). With it `active`, it covers:\n  - each class with at least one member;\n  - an unknown tool name → class (d) deny;\n  - a class-(b) payload with no path key → deny, and the same with the key's value `\"\"` → deny;\n  - a production `.py` without a failing test → deny, with a judge kind;\n  - with a failing test → allow;\n  - `/tmp` and `artifactDirectoryPath` non-`.py` → allow, and `.py` there → deny;\n  - a class-(b) target naming an existing directory (`.` and `src/`) → deny `kind=judge-error`,\n    and the same target with the scan reading `inactive` → allow (D-16).\n  With the scan reading `unknown` (a `docs/.bkit-memory.json` that is not JSON), each deny above\n  that does not need the judge still fires (class (d), no path key, `\"\"`, out-of-root `.py`, a\n  directory target, a `run_command` FR-6 refuses, and the `workspacePaths` cross-check), each from its own fixture.\n  The **target differential** runs one corpus of target shapes through both gates and shows they\n  agree on every shape except those FR-4's §\"Deliberate differences\" names, where the agy gate\n  refuses.\n- **AC-4.2:** Every exit path prints exactly one JSON object: malformed stdin, an internal\n  exception and an unreadable state each still yield parseable stdout. Every deny path the design\n  enumerates is driven by its own fixture, and each fixture asserts that `reason` ends with\n  exactly one `kind=<kind>` whose value is in the design's closed list (FR-4 §\"Every deny carries\n  a kind\"); a deny path with no fixture, or a kind outside the list, fails the AC.\n- **AC-5.1:** The wiring check emits each `UNWIRED` reason from its own fixture. `--agent agy`\n  writes no prompt and halts on each of them.\n- **AC-5.2:** Each row of FR-5's exit-code table is pinned by its own fixture, and each fixture\n  asserts the stdout token and the exit code separately:\n  - an `UNWIRED` fixture (one per reason, reusing AC-5.1's) shows check exit 0, and assembler\n    `ASSEMBLE-TDD: HALT agy_tdd_hook_unverified` with exit 0;\n  - a `hooks.json` holding invalid JSON shows check `UNREADABLE` with exit 2, and the assembler\n    HALT with exit 2;\n  - a check stubbed to print no token shows the assembler `HALT agy_tdd_hook_unverified check=no-token`\n    with exit 2;\n  - `WIRED` shows exit 0 on both;\n  - the agy gate's `--self-check` with a verdict `FAIL` forced prints that `FAIL` token on stdout\n    with exit 0, and the wiring check reads it as `UNWIRED reason=self-check-failed`.\n\n  A mutation that returns 2 on `UNWIRED` is killed, and so is one that returns 2 on a self-check\n  verdict `FAIL`.\n- **AC-6.1:** The shell policy is imported, not copied: one definition in the tree. The Codex gate's\n  existing shell tests pass unmodified. Every mutation row re-pointed under FR-6 runs\n  `MUTATION: ALL_CAUGHT` against the shared module, `h_mad_target_identity.py` (D-12). The agy gate refuses each `unsafe` command in the Codex\n  gate's `--self-check` list.\n- **AC-7.1:** `h_mad_phase7_preconditions.py` emits each of the three FR-7 warnings from its own\n  fixture, and none of them moves `ready`. Adding a `review_independence` entry to a record that\n  already holds two leaves both earlier entries byte-identical. An entry that fails FR-1 is\n  refused, and the store is unchanged.\n- **AC-7.2:** `review_independence_unrecorded` fires, with no `leg=6a-prime` entry, for each\n  implementer `codex` (field absent included), `agy`, `grok` and `claude`, each from its own\n  fixture (`grok` and `claude` through `implementer_switch.to`, the only FR-1 routing field that takes them; `implementation_authors` is an authorship record and does not trigger the warning), and for each `archreview` value `READY_TO_MERGE`, `WITH_FIXES` and `NO`. It does not fire\n  when a `leg=6a-prime` entry exists, nor when `archreview` is absent, `SKIPPED_NO_PANE` or\n  `SKIPPED_OPERATOR_OVERRIDE`.\n- **AC-7.3 (v1.8):** FR-7 §\"Attribution\" is pinned by fixtures, each RED against the mutant it\n  names and GREEN on the rule:\n  - **routing is not authorship:** `implementer_switch.to=agy` stored, the module's code from a\n    Codex dispatch, a Codex reviewer → `implementer=codex`, `independent=false`, and\n    `review_non_independent` fires. Mutant: attribution read from `implementer_switch.to`.\n  - **multi-author, recovered (E2):** authors agy then Codex; a grok 5e-review →\n    `implementer=codex`, `independent=true`; a Codex 5e-verify → `implementer=codex`,\n    `independent=false`; then, with the dispatch context discarded, an agy 6a-prime →\n    `implementer=agy`, `independent=false`. Mutant: authors recovered from earlier entries'\n    `implementer` values only. The recovered set's source is `implementation_authors` (FR-1,\n    D-15), holding `{m, agy}` then `{m, codex}`.\n  - **pre-field feature (D-15):** the same reviews on a record with no `implementation_authors`\n    → the 6a-prime leg writes no entry for the module. Mutant: an absent field read as an empty\n    author set, so the agy reviewer reads as independent.\n  - **RED is not authorship (E4):** an agy 5d RED dispatch then a Codex 5e GREEN; an agy reviewer →\n    `independent=true`, `implementer=codex`. Mutant: a 5d RED dispatch counted as an author.\n  - **unaccepted dispatches author nothing:** a rejected agy GREEN then an accepted Codex GREEN;\n    an agy reviewer → `independent=true`.\n  If the design leaves attribution as orchestrator prose with no code to drive, this AC has no\n  surface to run on; that is owed to the impl-plan as an OPEN-DECISION, not waived.\n- **AC-8.1:** The probe directory holds the payload capture, the real refusal, and the RED/GREEN\n  readings with re-run, revert and mutation results, each stamped with the sha and the\n  `agy --version`. The default timeout in `h_mad_assemble_tdd.py` equals FR-8's formula over\n  those readings. The readings come from FR-8's post-merge stage (D-13), and the feature is not\n  recorded COMPLETE before AC-8.1 and AC-8.2 pass.\n- **AC-8.2:** The probe directory holds the forced-handler-timeout reading from FR-8 step 2a,\n  stamped with the sha, `agy --version` and the T it used. The reading has three parts: whether\n  the file exists, the wall time and agy's message. It names exactly one outcome from step 2a's\n  table, and that outcome is consistent with the three readings. It also shows that the delay\n  hook was removed and that `.agents/hooks.json` matches its pre-probe bytes. A `session-error`\n  outcome does not satisfy this AC. If the outcome is `timeout-allows`, `agy-runtime.md` §\"The TDD\n  gate\" carries the fail-open residual, and the operator question is raised before the gate is\n  called verified.\n- **AC-9.1:** The prompt contains no implementer-specific opening. `test_prompt_file_is_agent_independent`\n  covers `codex`, `grok` and `agy`. The new-caller rule appears in RED item 1, in the WIRE-PIN\n  paragraph, in the GREEN variant, in the SKILL.md 5d halt text, in the `failure-recovery.md`\n  `step5d:red_wrong_reason:<module>` row and in the wiring-task header that `h_mad_assemble_tdd.py`\n  emits. `test_h_mad_wiring_task_shape_docs.py`, `test_h_mad_agent_definitions.py` and\n  `test_h_mad_assemble_tdd.py` stay green. FR-9's sweep, re-run on the shipped tree, hits no file\n  that states the rule without the carve-out. The sweep is the union FR-9 defines: the line grep\n  over every tracked file and the joined reading, which takes in `.py` comment text. The re-run\n  reports `unparsed=N`. A nonzero `N` does not satisfy this AC until each unparsed file has been\n  read by hand.\n- **AC-9.2:** The FR-9 differential corpus runs all four cases (`new-caller`, `missing-callee`,\n  `both-missing`, `unrelated-import`) as fixture repositories with real pytest REDs. For each case\n  it records the RED's exception line, whether the callee resolves at the RED base (by import) and\n  both verdicts. The set of cases whose verdict changed is exactly `{new-caller}`.\n- **AC-9.3:** The FR-9 incident replay covers HemaSuite `notebooklm-integration-interface` T4\n  WIRE-PIN 1 (`test_notebooklm_resolver.py` at `531a6296b`), T5 WIRE-PIN 1\n  (`test_notebooklm_initialize.py` at `7aa0d9dec`) and T6 WIRE-PIN 2\n  (`test_notebooklm_status_report.py` at `c2d671f6b`). For each case the committed probe holds:\n  - the direct-call form's RED reproduced at the task commit's parent: `AttributeError`/\n    `ImportError` on the caller, with the callee resolving by import. For T4 this is the committed\n    pin; for T5 and T6 it is the pin with the scaffold replaced, per FR-9;\n  - on that RED, the new rule's verdict **valid** and the old rule's verdict halt;\n  - for T5 and T6 only, the committed scaffolded pin's RED (`AssertionError`) and the old rule's\n    verdict on it, which is valid;\n  - a wire-cut re-run at the task commit in which the committed pin fails;\n  - a citation of the original evidence (M4.1, M5.1, WIRE 2 revert).\n\n  It is stamped with both repositories' shas. A case that cannot be reproduced is recorded with\n  its failing command and counted. It does not satisfy the AC.\n- **AC-9.4:** The six copies of the new-caller rule (FR-9 surfaces 1–5 and the GREEN variant) are\n  bound to one authority. Either every surface is generated from or checked against one shared\n  source, or one test asserts that the carve-out text is equal across all six copies. The plan\n  names which mechanism. Whichever it is:\n  - **The carve-out text is the whole quoted block in FR-9**, all three sentences: the validity\n    condition, \"The RED report must name both.\", and the wire-cut obligation. A check that binds\n    only the first sentence does not meet this AC.\n  - **Each copy is read from its own source location.** The span read for one copy contains no\n    other copy. Three readings do not meet this AC, because a healthy copy in the same span\n    masks a sick one: a contains-check over the whole assembled prompt (the template's copies\n    1, 2 and 6 are filled into it beside the header copy); a file-level contains-check over\n    `codex-implementer-prompt.md`, which holds three copies; and a file-level check over any\n    file that holds more than one copy. The design names each copy's locator. A locator states\n    what it does not pin, and the test fails, rather than passing, when a locator matches\n    nothing or more than one span.\n  - **The control is a mutation of exactly one copy, and it makes the check fail.** It is run\n    once per copy, so six times, and once per sentence of the block in at least one copy. Each\n    mutation row's `find` is unique in its file, because `h_mad_mutation_harness.py`\n    `anchor_status` refuses a `find` that does not occur exactly once. A `find` therefore carries\n    copy-specific context, and the `.py` copy's `find` is written against the source bytes,\n    including the adjacent-literal split. A row the harness refuses is not a control.\n- **AC-X:** Every new guard carries a mutation row that bites (`MUTATION: ALL_CAUGHT` with a `test`\n  per row). The full suite passes per task.\n\n## Decisions on the brainstorm's open questions\n\n- **D-1 `run_command`:** command-shape rule shared with Codex (FR-6). Rationale: the tree shows\n  that is what Codex parity is.\n- **D-2 Hook scope:** per project (`.agents/hooks.json`). Rationale:\n  - The Codex gate is wired per project (HemaSuite `.codex/hooks.json`; this repository has no\n    `.codex/`).\n  - A tracked file reaches every worktree.\n  - The handler cwd gives the root without trusting a payload field.\n  - No global hook fires in every agy session on the machine.\n\n  Residual: a project without the file has no agy gate. FR-5 turns that into a halt, not a silent\n  pass.\n- **D-3 Payload cwd:** the root comes from the handler cwd (FR-4), with `workspacePaths` as a\n  cross-check.\n- **D-4 Timeout:** provisional 1500 s, then FR-8's formula. The hook-handler timeout is a separate\n  figure, derived from `JUDGE_BUDGET_S`.\n- **D-5 Availability:** CLI on PATH + `agy_status` + `HMAD_AGY_UNAVAILABLE`, symmetric with Codex.\n  No liveness probe: the gate runs on every write and must be offline and fast, and a probe spends\n  quota. An exhausted agy shows up when the dispatch fails, and the orchestrator records it with\n  `--set agy_status=exhausted`.\n- **D-6 Project default:** feature record only. A second source doubles the surface the gate\n  resolves, and per-feature is the operator decision. A later default can seed the same field at\n  `--create`.\n- **D-7 Flag location:** a state field (FR-7). It is not on the extracted verdict, because that\n  line is `<KEY>: <VALUE>` and its consumers parse it.\n- **D-8 Audit legs:** unchanged (FR-10).\n- **D-9 Codex reviewing Codex (was OQ-1; operator decision, 2026-10-16):** yes. FR-7's flag fires\n  for any `reviewer == implementer`, Codex included, for example the 5e anti-gaming verifier\n  dispatched to Codex on a Codex-implemented feature. It is a warning only, never a block.\n  Consequence: Codex-implemented features can now carry a `review_non_independent` Phase-7\n  warning that has never appeared before.\n- **D-10 `.agents/` exclusion (was OQ-2; operator decision, 2026-10-16):** narrow it. Applied in this\n  clone by the orchestrator on the operator's answer: the `.git/info/exclude` entry `/.agents/` is now `/.agents/skills/`.\n  Evidence at `d65374e9`: `git check-ignore -v .agents/hooks.json` prints nothing (exit 1, not\n  ignored), and `git check-ignore -v .agents/skills` still reports the `/.agents/skills/` entry.\n  D-2's tracked `.agents/hooks.json` therefore needs no `git add -f`.\n  - **Residual / prerequisite.** `.git/info/exclude` is per-clone local configuration and is not\n    versioned. Every other clone of this repository, and every other project that adopts the agy\n    gate, needs the same narrowing (or a `git add -f`) before its `.agents/hooks.json` can be\n    tracked. Linked worktrees share the main clone's `info/exclude` (git's common dir), so they\n    inherit the narrowing from the clone they belong to. Where the file is not tracked, FR-5's\n    check reads `UNWIRED reason=no-hooks-file` in any worktree that lacks it, so the gap is a halt,\n    not a silent pass.\n- **D-11 `review_independence_unrecorded` for every implementer (operator decision, 2026-10-16):**\n  widened from agy-only to `codex|agy|grok|claude`, matching D-9 (FR-7). Warning only, never a\n  block. Rationale: it fires only when the recording step was skipped, so a feature that follows\n  the new flow stays quiet; without it, a missing record would hide a Codex-on-Codex self-review,\n  which D-9 now flags when it IS recorded.\n  - **Pre-existing features — settled, no grandfather rule.** The check applies to every feature\n    whose Phase-7 check runs after this ships. Archived features are untouched because nothing\n    re-runs the check over them (FR-7). A grandfather rule would need a signal separating \"began\n    before this shipped\" from \"skipped the recording step\", and the only signal the record offers\n    — absence of `review_independence` — is the skip itself, so any such rule would also silence\n    the case the warning exists for. A warning on an in-flight feature costs one backfilled entry\n    or one report line.\n- **D-12 Shared shell-policy module (was the design's OQ-A; operator decision, 2026-10-16):** the\n  FR-6 policy moves into the existing `h-mad/scripts/h_mad_target_identity.py`, not a new module.\n  The design's reason: `test_h_mad_codex_tdd_gate_judge.py` `test_codex_gate_judge_timeout_reason`\n  builds a tree by copying only the Codex gate, a judge stub and `h_mad_target_identity.py`\n  (`grep -n -A12 'def test_codex_gate_judge_timeout_reason' h-mad/tests/test_h_mad_codex_tdd_gate_judge.py`),\n  so a new module would be missing there, and FR-6 forbids editing that test.\n- **D-13 FR-8 timing (was the design's OQ-B; operator decision, 2026-10-16):** FR-8 runs as a\n  post-merge stage after Phase 7's integrate, and the feature is recorded COMPLETE only after it.\n  Reason: the tracer must run the gate at its installed path, which is main.\n- **D-14 Leap-second `.ts` refused (orchestrator decision, design audit cycle 1, 2026-10-16):**\n  FR-1 narrows `implementer_switch.ts` to seconds `00`–`59`. Reason: state timestamps are written\n  by h-mad's own writer through Python `datetime`, which cannot produce a seconds field of `60`.\n- **D-15 Durable author record (OD-E2, operator decision, 2026-10-16: option (a)):** FR-1 gains\n  `implementation_authors`, an optional array of `{module, agent}` entries appended (never\n  removed or altered) through FR-7's Record mechanism, one per accepted 5e GREEN or accepted fix\n  dispatch and never for a 5d RED. FR-7 §\"Attribution\" reads a module's author set from it.\n  Reason: design v1.2 (OD-E2) showed AC-7.3's \"multi-author, recovered\" fixture could not pass\n  without a durable author record, because the assembler's prompt, out and log files are under\n  `/tmp` and its slug names no agent. The fixture stays as written and now has its source. The\n  rejected option (b) was to amend the fixture to \"no entry\".\n- **D-16 Directory target refused under active step5 (OD-2, operator decision, 2026-10-16):** a\n  class-(b) target that names an existing directory is refused with `kind=judge-error` when the\n  scan reads `active` or `unknown`, and allowed when it reads `inactive` (FR-4 deliberate\n  difference 6). Reason: impl-plan v1.1 Task 6 showed that `canonicalise` returns no name and\n  no `unresolvable` flag for `.`, `src/`, `src` and the root, so the class-(b) sequence would\n  otherwise allow a directory target under `active`. Residual: a trailing-`/` path that names no\n  existing directory is not covered and is judged as a file name.\n\n## Open questions for the operator\n\nNone — OQ-1/OQ-2 settled 2026-10-16; the design's OQ-A/OQ-B settled 2026-10-16 (D-12, D-13);\nthe design's OD-E2 settled 2026-10-16 (D-15); the impl-plan's OD-2 settled 2026-10-16 (D-16).\n\n## Non-goals\n\n- Per-task implementer selection. It can come later on the same field.\n- Refusing a peer's production write in that peer's own gate when the other peer is the resolved\n  implementer, for example a Codex verifier dispatch writing production code under\n  `implementer=agy`. Residual: the test-first judge still applies, and the per-dispatch\n  `git status --short` diff (agy-runtime.md §\"Author and reviewer roles\") is what shows it.\n- A write-time gate for grok, and authorship rules for agy as the orchestrating host.\n- Routing audit legs by `agy_status`.\n\n## Owed by other documents\n\nNone of these are edited by this spec.\n- `h-mad/references/agy-runtime.md` says there is no `SessionStart` on two surfaces:\n  - §\"Hooks\" (\"There is **no `SessionStart`**\");\n  - the §\"Construct mapping\" table's `hook-event` row (\"there is no `SessionStart`\").\n\n  agy's current hooks.md lists `SessionStart` as a supported event. Both surfaces need a re-check\n  against the installed agy (`agy --version` prints 1.4.0 at `200ffd52`), they are corrected\n  together, and the correction stamps the version it was checked against.\n  (`grep -n 'SessionStart' h-mad/references/agy-runtime.md` at `200ffd52`: 2 matching lines, one\n  under each heading.) Residual: this is a content predicate, so a third restatement added later\n  is found only by re-running the grep.\n- **Design (v1.8 items).** FR-4: enumerate the closed kind list and give every deny path a member\n  (E6); its claim that `unknown` \"denies `kind=judge-error`, as the Codex gate does for a shell\n  command\" is false at `cff71b49` — the Codex gate's `unknown` shell deny carries no `kind=`\n  token. FR-7: name where the complete author set is kept (E2) and correct \"the GREEN dispatch the\n  orchestrator accepted at 5d\" to 5e (E4).\n- **Design (v1.9, D-15).** FR-1: add `implementation_authors` with FR-1's entry schema to the\n  schema JSON and a `state-schema.md` row. FR-7: retire \"the complete set is kept in the\n  procedure\" and OD-E2 in favour of reading the set from `implementation_authors`; the\n  `--append` mechanism (DO-3) must accept this key with the same guarantees, and the \"When the\n  dispatch list is lost\" rule becomes FR-7's three closed unrecoverable cases.\n- **Design (spec v1.10).** FR-4: record D-16 as a directory check before the target is judged,\n  and add it to §\"Deliberate differences\" as item 6; FR-4 `--self-check`: a verdict `FAIL` exits\n  0 (replacing \"a nonzero exit\"), and the Codex gate's `FAIL shell-policy` is corrected in impl-plan\n  Task 4; FR-2: adopt the halts `step5:implementer_invalid` and `step5:implementer_unresolved`.\n- **Impl-plan.** AC-7.3's surface (FR-7 §\"Attribution\" must have code to drive, or the AC is an\n  OPEN-DECISION), now including the pre-field fixture; AC-1.3's fixtures; AC-4.1's `unknown`\n  fixtures and AC-4.2's per-deny-path kind fixtures.\n- **Plan.** If the plan carries FR-1's field list or AC-7.3's fixtures, it gains\n  `implementation_authors`, AC-1.3 and the pre-field fixture.\n\n## Version History\n- v1.0: Initial spec (2026-10-16) from brainstorm v1.1. Settles 8 open questions (D-1..D-8): run_command takes the Codex command-shape rule, not a stated residual; per-project .agents/hooks.json; root from handler cwd; provisional 1500 s timeout then tracer formula; PATH+status+env availability; feature-only implementer; flag in state; audit legs unchanged. Folds brief fix 3 (#8) WIRE-PIN new-caller RED case as FR-9. OQ-1 (Codex-reviews-Codex flag), OQ-2 (.agents exclude) for the operator.\n- v1.1: Operator decisions 2026-10-16 (2026-10-16). OQ-1 -> D-9: FR-7 flag fires for any reviewer == implementer, Codex reviewing Codex included; warning only. OQ-2 -> D-10: operator narrowed .git/info/exclude /.agents/ to /.agents/skills/ (check-ignore evidence at d65374e9), no git add -f; per-clone residual stated. Open questions section now empty.\n- v1.2: Operator decision 2026-10-16 -> D-11: FR-7 review_independence_unrecorded widened from agy-only to every implementer (codex|agy|grok|claude), matching D-9; warning only. Trigger pinned to a 6a-prime leg that ran (archreview READY_TO_MERGE|WITH_FIXES|NO) with no leg=6a-prime entry; other archreview values already surface. Pre-existing features settled: no grandfather rule, archived features untouched (no batch caller). AC-7.2 added.\n- v1.3: Plan-author findings against v1.2 (2026-10-16). FR-3: a missing state-line field refuses judge-error (tree precedent: missing-field fixture), so the two gate stub-line fixtures are re-shaped with assertions byte-identical — the AC-3.2 exception; test_h_mad_tdd_judge.py ACTIVE_RE and line-quoting mutation rows follow the line. FR-7: writer has no append (--set replaces); add-without-loss is a requirement, mechanism a design obligation; AC-7.1 gains the two-entry fixture. FR-6: rows re-pointed to the shared module, then ALL_CAUGHT; moved names stay bound on the Codex gate module. FR-9/AC-9.1/FR-10: failure-recovery.md red_wrong_reason row; rule surfaces closed with sweep. FR-1/FR-10/AC-1.1: fallback_agent description on schema JSON and state-schema.md, pinned literals named. Owed: second SessionStart surface (Construct-mapping hook-event row). All ACs reformatted to the h_mad_ac_census.py grammar (- **AC-n.m:**).\n- v1.4: Plan audit cycle 1 findings owned by the spec (2026-10-16). F1 -> FR-5 exit-code table: AGY-GATE WIRED/UNWIRED (all seven reasons, no-hooks-file included) exit 0 and the assembler's agy_tdd_hook_unverified verdict HALT exits 0; UNREADABLE and a no-token check exit 2; existing assembler HALTs keep exit 2 (residual); AC-5.2. F2 -> FR-1: .ts is RFC 3339 date-time; neither validator path enforces format today (probed), so refusal on both paths is the requirement and the mechanism a design obligation; rfc3339-validator hazard; residual for started_ts/owner_heartbeat_ts/halt_ts; AC-1.2. F3 -> FR-9 differential corpus over four missing-symbol cases, changed set == {new-caller}; AC-9.2. F4 -> FR-9 incident replay of HemaSuite notebooklm F-3 (T4/T5 WIRE-PIN 1, T6 WIRE-PIN 2) with wire-cut re-run; AC-9.3. F5 -> FR-8 step 2a forced handler timeout with four classified outcomes; step 2 now settles two unknowns; FR-5 re-run trigger includes it; AC-8.2. F6 -> no contradiction; FR-3 now lists the real-line assertions (two in test_h_mad_tdd_gate_fallback_agent.py, four tests in test_h_mad_tdd_judge.py) as placement constraints; AC-3.2 extended.\n- v1.5: Plan audit cycle 2 findings owned by the spec, final corrective revision after the round cap (2026-10-16). B -> FR-10 adds the SKILL.md teammate-audit bullet: audit-leg selection vs resolved authorship (Claude only when resolved=claude), anchor prefix and the four pinned tokens kept. C -> FR-9 surface census gains surface 5, the h_mad_assemble_tdd.py assemble() wiring header (split string literals), and its pin test_h_mad_assemble_tdd.py TestWiringShape.test_the_pin_is_told_what_its_red_must_be; the census method now joins Python adjacent literals via ast and collapses markdown whitespace, measured at 9c3b799d; residual: run-time-built strings. AC-9.1 lists the header. D -> FR-9 replay and AC-9.3 name all three pins with files and commits (531a6296b, 7aa0d9dec, c2d671f6b); T5/T6 replay evaluates the direct-call / two-argument getattr form; the committed scaffolded RED (AssertionError) is recorded as old-verdict valid, correcting the false old-verdict=halt claim. A -> new AC-9.4: the six rule copies bound to one authority, with a per-copy control.\n- v1.6: Advisory delta review of plan v1.3 (608e430), spec-owned parts (2026-10-16). Should-fix 3 -> FR-9: the joined reading (ast str constants) is blind to non-str text in a .py file; axis named with three members (comments, files ast.parse rejects, bytes literals). The sweep is now the union of the line grep and the joined reading, which adds joined .py comment tokens; parse failures are reported as unparsed=N and read by hand; residual: run-time-built strings and a bytes literal split across lines. Measured at 5f72c2f3: union 7 files, line-grep-minus-joined 0, comment hits 0 (incidental), unparsed=0. AC-9.1 re-run is the union and reports unparsed=N. Must-fix 1 (spec side) and Should-fix 4 -> AC-9.4: the carve-out text is the whole three-sentence FR-9 block; each copy is read from its own source location, never from the assembled prompt or a file-level contains-check over a file holding more than one copy; a locator matching zero or several spans fails the test; the control mutates exactly one copy, runs once per copy and once per sentence in at least one copy, with a find unique in its file (mutation harness anchor_status), the .py find written against source bytes including the literal split.\n- v1.7: Design audit cycle 1, spec-owned items (2026-10-16). S1 (codex C1) -> FR-1/AC-1.1/AC-1.2 + D-14: .ts narrowed to seconds 00-59, a leap second (2016-12-31T23:59:60Z) refused on both paths; reason: every .ts comes from Python datetime via the writer, which cannot hold second 60 (orchestrator decision). S2 (codex C6) -> FR-7 Attribution: an entry's implementer is the author of the reviewed code, from its successful implementation dispatch, never implementer_switch.to (historical) or the implementer field; multi-author code is non-independent if the reviewer is any author. S3 -> FR-1/FR-10/AC-1.1: codex_status schema description and state-schema.md row rewritten to FR-2 authorship (no test pins the text; the fallback_agent-after-codex_status order pin stated); FR-8 Version: 1.4.0 at 200ffd52, each reading stamps its version; Owed SessionStart re-check re-stamped 1.4.0 at 200ffd52; FR-6/AC-6.1 shared module = h_mad_target_identity.py (D-12, OQ-A); FR-8/AC-8.1 post-merge stage, COMPLETE only after it (D-13, OQ-B). Open questions: OQ-A/OQ-B settled.\n- v1.8: Design audit cycle 2, final corrective revision after the round cap, not re-audited (2026-10-16). S1/E6 -> FR-4 'Every deny carries a kind': rule over every deny path, closed list owned by the design, spec names only judge-error, tool-not-admitted and the judge's Verdict kind; Codex gate has 6 of 13 kindless _deny call sites at cff71b49. E1 -> FR-4 'The activity reading': three-valued scan (active/unknown/inactive), every step5-gated deny also fires on unknown, class-(b) production .py still read through read_chain under inactive; workspacePaths and Tool classes sentences re-worded. E5 -> FR-4 Target keys: a non-empty-string value counts as no path key. S4 -> FR-4 'Deliberate differences from the Codex gate': design's four adopted plus the kind rule as a fifth. S2/E2/E4 -> FR-7 Attribution sub-bullets (5e GREEN + accepted fixes, never 5d RED; complete author set, never a projection) and new AC-7.3. S5 -> adopted: reviewer none of the authors names the latest accepted dispatch's author. S3 -> leap-second ValueError quote drops the version-dependent tail. AC-4.1/AC-4.2 extended; FR-6 states the unknown-scan shell deny; Owed gains design and impl-plan items.\n- v1.9: Operator decision OD-E2 (a) (2026-10-16) -> D-15: FR-1 gains implementation_authors, an optional array of {module, agent} (agent codex|agy|grok|claude; module non-empty string; no extra keys), appended never altered through FR-7's Record mechanism on accepting a 5e GREEN or fix dispatch, never 5d RED or a halted/timed-out/rejected dispatch; duplicates kept, array order = acceptance order. New AC-1.3 (valid entries accepted, unknown agent and other malformed shapes refused, both validator paths). FR-7 Attribution reads the author set from the field on every leg; unrecoverable cases closed to three (field absent: pre-field features; no entry for the module: a record-step defect; malformed) -> no entry. AC-7.3 multi-author fixture unchanged with its source named; pre-field fixture added. AC-7.2 'only FR-1 field' narrowed to routing field. FR-10 SKILL.md Phase-5 step bullet. Owed: design FR-1/FR-7 (retire procedure-kept set and OD-E2), impl-plan AC-1.3 + pre-field fixture, plan mirror.\n- v1.10: Owed lines from impl-plan v1.1 (Phase 5b), final corrective revision, not re-audited (2026-10-16). P1 -> D-16 (OD-2, operator): a class-(b) target naming an existing directory denies kind=judge-error under active/unknown, allowed under inactive; FR-4 deliberate difference 6, AC-4.1 fixture; residual: trailing-/ path naming no existing directory. P2 -> FR-2 halts named: step5:implementer_invalid (invalid), step5:implementer_unresolved (no-record, unreadable), step5:implementer_switch_unrecorded (record=failed); adopted from impl-plan Task 16; failure-recovery.md rows not added (residual). P3 -> FR-5: the gate's --self-check verdict FAIL prints its token on stdout and exits 0, operational error nonzero; Codex gate FAIL shell-policy corrected in impl-plan Task 4; Codex FAIL parser residual; AC-5.2 gains the self-check fixture and mutant. P4 -> FR-9 Hook line keeps Claude's h-mad-tdd-gate.sh (test_hook_line_names_both_gates). Owed: design FR-4/FR-2 lines.\n\n\nAudit rubric (TWO axes, both mandatory):\n\nAxis A — Generic adversarial:\n- Contradictions inside the doc\n- Gaps (missing error paths, untestable AC, unstated assumptions)\n- Weak claims (load-bearing decisions without justification)\n- Scope creep (work outside the stated feature boundary)\n\nAxis B — Invariant compliance (any breach = must-fix, you cannot downgrade).\nTwo layers, both binding:\n\n### Base invariants (workflow-universal; non-overridable by any project file — but the operator `## Acknowledged-not-fixed` sidecar still applies to base findings):\n\n# H-MAD Base Invariants — Axis B (workflow-universal)\n\n> Shipped with the `/h-mad` skill. The orchestrator inlines this file verbatim into the\n> `INLINE_BASE_INVARIANTS` slot (name written bare — a bracketed mention inside an inlined\n> file survives substitution and reaches the reviewer as an unfilled-looking placeholder)\n> of every plan / design / impl-plan audit, **before** the\n> per-project `<PROJECT_ROOT>/.h-mad/invariants.md` domain layer. These rules apply to every\n> project that uses `/h-mad` and are **non-overridable by any project file**: a project's\n> invariants file may add domain rules but may not downgrade or delete a base rule. The\n> operator `## Acknowledged-not-fixed` sidecar escape hatch still applies to base findings\n> (a base item listed under that section in a sidecar `.audit.v<N+1>.md` is a conscious,\n> audited deferral — it is excluded from the gate count exactly like any other layer's item).\n>\n> Axis A (generic adversarial review) is covered by the audit-prompt template — not repeated here.\n\n## Audit-gate signal discipline\n- Any gate/check whose verdict the orchestrator consumes MUST communicate PASS/FAIL via an\n  explicit **stdout token** and **exit 0** on a normal verdict (PASS or FAIL). Using a non-zero\n  process exit to mean \"FAIL\" is a violation — a non-zero exit registers as a Claude Code tool\n  failure (`PostToolUseFailure`) and leaks into coexisting plugins' error handling. A non-zero\n  exit is permitted ONLY for genuine operational errors (missing/unreadable input).\n\n## Single-source contract\n- A rule applied by more than one surface (e.g. an audit blocking-item count read by both the\n  orchestrator gate step and a precondition checker) MUST have exactly one authoritative\n  implementation that all surfaces call, OR a test asserting byte-equivalence across surfaces.\n  Independent re-implementations that can silently diverge are a violation.\n- A contract stated in a template (e.g. an empty-section sentinel) MUST match what the consuming\n  gate logic treats as that case. Template guidance and gate behavior disagreeing is a violation.\n\n## Standalone / no plugin dependency\n- The skill MUST NOT acquire a **runtime dependency** on any other plugin (OMC, bkit,\n  context-mode, etc.) or external skill (spec-kit, b-mad, pdca). Coexistence accommodations\n  (sharing a state filename, conforming doc structure to an external validator's required\n  sections) are allowed; *requiring another plugin installed at runtime* is a violation.\n\n## No new external dependency\n- Scripts MUST use only Python stdlib plus tooling already depended upon by the skill (the\n  agent dispatch substrate (cmux or orca, via `hmad-dispatch`); `jq` where the existing hook\n  already uses it; `pytest`).\n  Introducing a new third-party package or new CLI is a violation.\n- **Dispatched agent CLIs are not script dependencies.** `codex`, `agy` and `grok` are optional\n  runtime *agents*: reached only through a `hmad-dispatch` verb (`exec`, `send`, …), never imported,\n  required, or invoked by a script or test for its own work — tests reach them through stubs, and an\n  absent agent CLI is a dispatch-time condition the verb reports, never a failure of the skill.\n  The list is closed: adding an agent to it is an operator decision recorded here, not something a\n  document or a dispatch may assume. (`grok` added 2026-09-28, operator decision on\n  `grok-codex-fallback` design audit v2.)\n\n## Portable time bounds\n- A time-bounded command MUST NOT be written as `timeout <s> <cmd>` or `gtimeout <s> <cmd>`.\n  **Neither is a macOS system component**, so the form is unportable in both directions.\n  A stock box ships neither and the call fails at 127; a box carrying `brew install coreutils`\n  runs it fine and the bound silently rests on a CLI h-mad cannot assume is there.\n  **What your box has is not an input to this rule.** h-mad already owns a time-bounder that\n  reachable wherever `hmad-dispatch` is, so the form is forbidden unconditionally — not because\n  of what happens downstream. (Reachable, not universal: where the wrapper itself cannot be\n  called the rule below still applies — halt, do not drop the bound.) Do not reason from a local probe to an exemption. For anything committed\n  or dispatched the form is additionally a new external CLI dependency (§\"No new external\n  dependency\"); a downstream 127 is how that eventually surfaces, never the reason.\n  Do not settle the question by probing: a local `command -v timeout` that succeeds is not\n  licence, it proves only that this box has coreutils, and it means the 127 that would have\n  exposed the improvisation never fires. Use `hmad-dispatch run --timeout <s> -- <cmd...>`,\n  which exits 124 at the deadline.\n- Where a 127 does fire, its aftermath is worse than the 127 itself. Measured: the reflex after `timeout: command not found`\n  is to re-run the same command **unbounded** and call it \"checking directly\". That does not fail\n  at the deadline, it hangs the phase, and in every log h-mad reads a hang and slow work are the\n  same bytes. A plan, a design, or a prompt that leaves an agent no reachable time-bounder is a\n  violation; the correct behaviour when none exists is to **halt**, not to drop the bound.\n\n## Doc-template superset compliance\n- Generated phase documents whose type is validated by an external doc-structure validator\n  (e.g. bkit PDCA: `plan` → `docs/01-plan/...`, `design` → `docs/02-design/...`,\n  `report` → `docs/04-report/...`) MUST be supersets that satisfy that validator's required\n  sections AND retain the existing h-mad sections. Dropping an h-mad section or failing the\n  validator is a violation.\n- The contract binds the **saved document**, not only the template it came from. Where such a\n  validator reclassifies a document by a substring of its *content* — bkit's `isPlanPlus` promotes\n  a plan to a larger required-section list on seeing `Plan-Plus`, `Plan Plus`, `plan-plus`,\n  `Brainstorming-Enhanced`, or `Intent Discovery` anywhere in the file — a generated document\n  carrying that substring is a violation even though the template is clean. Guard it mechanically\n  (`scripts/h_mad_doc_shape_check.py`); the literals are unremarkable prose, so an author cannot\n  be expected to avoid them from memory.\n\n## Operator-override preservation\n- The `## Acknowledged-not-fixed` sidecar override mechanism MUST remain functional for all\n  layers (base + project). Any gate change that causes overridden items to still count as\n  blocking, or that ignores the sidecar, is a violation.\n\n## Backward compatibility\n- A change to the audit gate MUST preserve the PASS verdict for audit docs that passed before\n  the change. Flipping a historically-passing audit to FAIL (absent a real new blocking item)\n  is a violation.\n\n## Marker discipline\n- Orchestrator phase transitions and halts MUST emit `[H-MAD]` log markers so a run is\n  diagnosable from logs alone. Silent state transitions are a violation.\n\n## Mutation verification\n- A step that mutates state (a git operation, a file write, a remote/CLI call) MUST verify the\n  mutation by **re-reading the resulting state**; an **exit code is not evidence that a mutation\n  occurred**. A plan, design, or implementation that treats a zero exit — or an echoed success\n  string such as `Sent N bytes` or `{\"ok\":true}` — as proof of the intended effect is a violation.\n- The failure this blocks is silent and looks exactly like success: two `zsh` no-ops (backtick\n  execution inside `-m`, a leading-dash path) both exited 0 while doing nothing, and a dispatch\n  reported `Sent 7293 bytes` into a dead pane. Where a command reports on its own behaviour, the\n  check must read the *thing it was supposed to change*, from a separate call.\n- **When several mutations target the SAME line, vary one field and keep the anchor shared.** A\n  guard whose failure has separable parts — exit code, stream routing, message content — needs one\n  mutation per part, each replacing the same anchor. That shape is what proves a content assertion\n  load-bearing: the mutants that keep exit and stream and strip only the text are exactly the ones a\n  returncode-only test survives. Two discriminators worth writing into the spec: a\n  first-vs-last-occurrence mutant survives whenever the sought item is last in BOTH regions, so the\n  discriminating fixture must put the decoys between the markers and leave the tail empty; and\n  `survived` has four distinct causes — missing guard, equivalent mutant, weak test, and a mutant\n  that never ran — which the verdict token collapses into one word. Diagnose which before acting.\n\n## Test discrimination\n- A test or guard MUST be **observed failing against the unfixed code** before it is trusted.\n  For a regression test, revert the fix and re-run; for a guard, stub it to its permissive value\n  and re-run the suite. **Zero failures is a finding, not a reassurance** — it means the check is\n  unenforced, not that the code is safe. Keeping a check that has never been seen to fail is a\n  violation.\n- A check that cannot fail is decoration, and it is worse than no check because it reports\n  coverage that does not exist. Two guards shipped green this way and were caught only by\n  stubbing: one passed solely because an unrelated helper stripped the env var its subject read,\n  and a documentation test passed with the documented guidance deleted, because both of its\n  component words already appeared in nearby prose. Neither was visible to review or to a green run.\n- The mutation must itself be verified (§\"Mutation verification\"): a `.replace()` that matches\n  nothing exits 0 and reports the guard as enforced.\n- **A criterion that cannot discriminate in EITHER direction is vacuous, and it survives both a\n  green run and a mutation battery.** The stronger case of the bullet above: not \"this check never\n  fails\" but \"this check returns the same observation whether the behaviour is present or absent\".\n  Measured on AC-2.5b, which spied on `is_cli_available` *returning* `False` — the forced-`True`\n  mutation emits an identical record and returns the same value, so neither the passing run nor the\n  mutated run could tell the two states apart. AC-7.5c was the same shape. **Neither audit leg saw\n  either one**; the revising author found AC-2.5b while fixing an unrelated must. A document audit\n  reads prose and cannot ask \"does this pin discriminate?\", so the question has to be asked where the\n  criterion is written: **name the observation that differs between the implemented and unimplemented\n  states, and if you cannot name one, the criterion is decoration** — delete it or replace it with a\n  positive assertion only the implemented behaviour produces. Spying on a return value that both\n  states produce is the recurring form.\n- **Mutating a path-resolution function can disable the suite's own isolation.** Tests usually\n  isolate by pointing an env override at a temp path, and that override is honoured by a branch —\n  the same kind of branch a mutation deletes. Stubbing the override branch in `_pin_file` redirected\n  every pin write in the suite onto the developer's live session file and replaced two real agent\n  handles, while the run reported 642 passed. Before mutating anything that decides *where* state is\n  written, snapshot the real target and restore it, or run in a sandboxed working directory.\n- **A stub must model the step the real system CONSUMES.** The rules above are about asserting the\n  right thing; this one is about the fixture telling the truth. A stub that replays state the real\n  system consumes once makes a test pass before the fix exists: an orca stub replayed an acked\n  delivery forever, so a sibling-cache test re-matched from the queue, pinned nothing, and passed\n  against unmodified code. Cardinality is part of the model — a fake that writes a path once where\n  production writes it twice let a verb never dispatch with 57 tests green. Before trusting a stub,\n  ask which of its effects are destructive in production and make the stub destroy them too.\n\n## Verifying a review finding\n- **A review finding has three separable parts — facts, concern, prescription — and they fail\n  INDEPENDENTLY.** Before applying a prescription, diff it against the RED tests and the spec's\n  acceptance criteria. A finding that matches the design doc but breaks tests means the DESIGN\n  drifted, not the implementation. Measured: one pass's prescription broke a guard (1 failed, 53\n  passed) while its concern was entirely real, and another review's prescription was backwards.\n- Test a prescription by applying it as a mutation and reverting it. When two passes dissent, ask\n  what the codebase ALREADY does — twice the house pattern beat both readings.\n\n## Guard narrowing\n- When a change **deliberately makes a guard accept something it used to reject**, the relaxation\n  MUST be shown to be *exactly* the intended case: run a corpus of inputs through the old and new\n  logic and diff the verdicts, then account for **every** input whose verdict softened. A green\n  suite is not evidence here — it encodes the cases someone already thought of, which is the wrong\n  population when the question is \"what else did this let through?\". This is the inverse of\n  §\"Mutation verification\": that proves a guard still bites, this proves a loosening did not widen.\n- The relaxation MUST rest on a **guarantee of the thing being parsed**, not on a heuristic that\n  re-implements it. Narrowing a scanner by \"ignoring quoted regions\" means re-deriving the target\n  language's quoting rules in a regex, which is how a bypass gets introduced; narrowing it by a rule\n  the language itself guarantees is safe. Verify that guarantee **against the real interpreter**,\n  not its documentation (§\"Assumption verification\").\n- Both halves were load-bearing when this was written. Narrowing a heredoc scanner so it stopped\n  denying inert prose, a 23-input differential corpus flagged one case as a regression; running the\n  shape through real `bash` showed the body genuinely never expands, so the *test expectation* was\n  wrong and the code was right. Without the corpus that case ships as either an unnoticed hole or a\n  \"fix\" applied to correct behaviour — and nothing else would have distinguished them. The same run\n  confirmed the intended relaxations numbered exactly two, and that the remaining 21 verdicts were\n  untouched.\n- A narrowing is scoped by **which checks consult the reduced input**, not by the reduction itself.\n  Exempting the quoted region from *one* class of check while every other class kept scanning the\n  full input is what kept the change from widening; a blanket exemption would have disabled\n  detections that were still correct for reasons unrelated to the false positive.\n\n## Connection enforcement\n- A task whose deliverable is a **connection** — a call site, an import, a registration, a route, a\n  flag or value propagated across a boundary — MUST ship a test that **fails when the connection\n  alone is removed and the callee is left intact**. A test that exercises the callee directly is not\n  evidence that the callee is reached. Shipping a connection with no such test is a violation.\n- **A whole-module revert cannot establish this**, because it removes both sides: the RED split\n  returns identically for a wired and an unwired build. The same blindness runs through every other\n  layer — a RED phase goes red because the callee is *absent*, an anti-gaming audit finds a\n  callee-scoped unit test perfectly discriminating, and a review of the diff sees a call site that is\n  *present* and therefore reads as correct. **Presence is not enforcement.** Measured across two\n  consecutive wiring tasks: each shipped its single load-bearing decision untested through every\n  audit cycle and through the RED phase, and only a mutation scoped to the connection caught it.\n- Mutate the connection in **both directions**: remove it → the wire test must fail; force it to fire\n  unconditionally → the fall-through/negative test must fail. One direction certifies a connection\n  that exists but is unconditional. Verify the mutation landed before trusting the run\n  (§\"Mutation verification\") — a revert that never happened reports as a pass.\n\n## Incident replay\n- A fix motivated by a specific observed incident MUST be **replayed against the real artifacts\n  already on disk** that motivated it, not only against cases authored alongside the fix.\n  **Synthetic cases alone are a violation** whenever such artifacts exist and are reachable.\n- Cases written next to a fix inherit the author's model of the bug, so they agree with it by\n  construction. A detector validated on 14 handcrafted samples rejected the real historical label\n  it was written to accept; the same replay then measured the true rate (7 of 13), which reclassified\n  the defect from a one-off into the majority case. Replay is how a fix is told from a belief.\n\n## Assumption verification\n- Every **load-bearing assumption** in a plan or design — an API's accepted inputs, a command's\n  output shape, a boundary, a default — MUST be **executed as a throwaway command before it is\n  written into the design**, and the design **cites the observed output**. An assumption asserted\n  without evidence, where evidence was one command away, is a violation.\n- Design review cannot catch a wrong assumption: it reads as reasonable, and the implementation\n  and its tests are then both built from the same wrong model, so they agree with each other and\n  pass. Tracer-bulleting the assumptions of one feature confirmed a `--porcelain` boundary,\n  confirmed a base-ref chain, and found a truncation hole — all before any code existed. Separately,\n  a selector grammar assumed from a wrapper's own code was wrong in a way that had already let a\n  destructive verb run unguarded.\n- The evidence belongs in the document, not only in the author's terminal. A cited output is\n  checkable by a reviewer; \"I verified this\" is not.\n- **Attributing a failure to a cause requires the controlled PAIR, not just the repro.** Run the\n  case with the blamed step and the case without it, and require the observable to differ. A repro\n  that reproduces confirms the symptom, never the cause. Measured four times: pane readiness was\n  blamed for an `injected:false` that a missing `--inject` flag fully explained, and that causality\n  shipped in a doc and a PR body; a title-only reading concluded \"no agents running\" and the\n  operator corrected it; and two carried repros were each falsified by a control that removed the\n  step they blamed. Re-run the MEASUREMENT as well as the claim — a brief's conclusion can be right\n  while its method is wrong.\n\n- **A contract to RECOGNISE something needs a real instance of it, captured from the running\n  system, before any gate scores it clean** — a banner, a log line, a response shape.\n  `pin-agents-tail-banner` passed 2663 tests, 49 mutations and 53 audit cycles while its matcher\n  hit 0 of 5 real agent banners: all 12 corpus positives were written by hand. The fixture corpus must include at\n  least one captured sample with its capture command cited; an idealised fixture is not a sample.\n\n## Behavioural premises carry their command\n- A premise about **behaviour** — \"X currently does Y\", \"this guard fires when Z\", \"the suite\n  collects N\", \"the parser rejects W\" — MUST carry the exact command that produced it, inline and\n  runnable, beside the observed output. An output recorded without its command is not\n  re-derivable, and a premise that cannot be cheaply re-run gets re-READ instead, cycle after\n  cycle, by every reviewer in turn. Measured: one false behavioural premise survived **10** audit\n  cycles that way.\n- Narrower than §\"Assumption verification\" and it fails at a different moment: there the evidence\n  was never gathered; here it was gathered once, written down as a conclusion, and thereby made\n  uncheckable. Both readers are then reasoning about prose.\n- **The command belongs in the document even when the answer is one number**, because the number\n  alone does not say what was counted. Measured: two readers measuring \"the same\" census got **3**\n  and **23**, having run different commands, and neither could tell which was wrong until the\n  command was written down beside it. `git grep -l '```' -- '*.py'` is the census; \"21 files\" is\n  a claim about one.\n- A premise whose command has become expensive to re-run is not exempt. Say so, and say what the\n  cheap proxy is — an unrunnable premise is a premise no later cycle can falsify.\n\n## Counts a dispatch reports\n- **Re-derive every COUNT a dispatched agent reports; never carry it into a document, a commit\n  message or a verdict on the agent's word.** A report that is right about its actions can still be\n  wrong about its numbers, and the two failures look identical from outside — the prose is accurate,\n  the figures are confident, and nothing in the report signals which is which.\n- Measured 2026-08-26: an `exec codex` report stated an anchor sweep as `mutations=238` when the\n  tree held **244**, with every spec byte-identical to HEAD and every other figure in the same\n  report checking out. Nothing had changed; the number was invented. The cheap control existed the\n  whole time — `--check-anchors` prints the count in about a second.\n- This is narrower than §\"Assumption verification\" and it applies at a different moment: there the\n  risk is an assumption written without evidence, here it is evidence that was produced, looks\n  right, and is false. Prefer a count you can re-derive with one command over any count a\n  dispatch hands you, and when a count is load-bearing for a verdict, re-derive it before recording\n  the verdict rather than after.\n\n## Wrapper–runtime reconciliation\n- A wrapper verb over an **external runtime's CLI** MUST be exercised **live against that runtime**\n  — a full create → list → remove cycle, or the verb's own equivalent round trip — before it ships,\n  and its **output-key extraction fixed against the observed envelope**. Shipping a verb whose\n  response shape is known only from stub fixtures is a violation, however green the suite is.\n- Stub fixtures encode the author's model of the envelope, so the wrapper and its tests agree with\n  each other and both are wrong together — the Incident-replay gap, applied to response parsing.\n  Four occurrences: a create-response `.id` read from the envelope (a per-request correlation uuid\n  that always exists, so it yielded a plausible but useless handle) in two different verbs; then\n  probing a live response for a delivery-id field surfaced `run_required` — the whole structured\n  path had been **dead at its first mutation**, and no stub test could see it because the stub had\n  no concept of a Run binding. Later the same day the real ack key proved to be `deliveryId` while\n  the extraction chain led with `delivery_id`, and the only test covering that loop pinned the\n  spelling the runtime never sends.\n- The live probe is what finds these, not the review: each was invisible to design review, to the\n  suite, and to a careful reading of the vendor guide. Where a live run is impossible (destructive\n  verb, no credentials), say so in the doc and name what stayed unverified — an unmeasured shape is\n  an open risk, not a pass.\n\n## Regression provenance\n- When a change makes an existing test fail, the plan or design MUST establish whether that test\n  **asserts current behaviour that the change is fixing** before proposing to edit it. **Changing an\n  existing test to pass is a violation unless** the doc states that the test pinned a defect (or\n  stale contract) as correct, and cites what it asserted.\n- The reflex \"make the failing test pass\" preserves the bug when the test *was* the bug. Three tests\n  in one session asserted the defect as an acceptance criterion — a forwarded selector a live\n  runtime rejects, a create-response handle the pane never has, a cwd-relative path — and each would\n  have survived a naive \"adjust the test\" edit. A red pre-existing test is evidence about the test as\n  often as about the change.\n\n## Both halves of a doc change\n- Removing a documented instruction, flag, or capability MUST be paired with **the executable\n  replacement landed** (or an explicit statement that the capability is intentionally dropped, with\n  the reason). A test asserting only that the old text is *gone* passes for a deletion that silently\n  lost the capability.\n- An unexecutable instruction was deleted in one session; a test checking only its absence would\n  have gone green while the run lost the ability it named. The gate is: assert the new path works in\n  the same change that removes the old one.\n\n## Reimplementation parity\n- Replacing a third-party dependency (validator, parser, formatter) with an in-tree implementation\n  MUST ship a **differential test asserting identical results against the original** across a corpus\n  that covers every construct in use, AND against the **real artifacts on disk**. Shipping the\n  reimplementation with tests that exercise only itself is a violation.\n- A hand-rolled reimplementation is worth exactly what its differential test catches. A bundled\n  JSON-Schema validator agreed with the library on a construct-complete corpus and on the live state\n  records; the traps it had to match (annotation-only `format`, `bool` is not `integer`) were\n  invisible without the library beside it. Parity on a synthetic corpus alone repeats the\n  Incident-replay gap: the corpus shares the author's model of the reimplementation.\n\n---\n\n## How agy uses this file\nagy reads this verbatim as the **base** portion of the Axis B rubric, before the project layer.\nAny finding that violates a base rule is auto-classified `## Must-fix` and cannot be downgraded.\n\n\n### Project invariants (domain rules for this repository):\n\n# H-MAD Project Invariants — Axis B (BrightGold70/skills repo, domain layer)\n\n> Inlined by `/h-mad` as the **project (domain)** portion of Axis B, AFTER the\n> workflow-universal `invariants.base.md`. The base layer already enforces the\n> universal rules (audit-gate signal discipline, single-source contract,\n> no-plugin-dependency, doc-template superset compliance, operator-override\n> preservation, backward-compatibility, marker discipline) — they are NOT repeated\n> here. This file holds only rules specific to this repository (a collection of\n> Claude Code skills: `SKILL.md` + `references/*.md` + `scripts/*.py` + `hooks/*.sh`).\n\n## Skill self-containment\n- A skill MUST remain runnable from a bare clone: no import of another skill's\n  internals, no hardcoded path outside the skill's own directory (except documented\n  host install locations: `~/.claude/...` for Claude Code, `~/.agents/skills/...` for\n  Codex, `~/.gemini/config/skills/...` for the Antigravity CLI (agy), each overridable\n  by its documented environment variable). Cross-skill coupling is a violation.\n  (Operator decision 2026-09-28, multi-host-runtime.)\n\n## Skill manifest integrity\n- Every skill's `SKILL.md` MUST carry valid frontmatter with `name` and `description`.\n  A skill whose entry behavior is changed without updating its `SKILL.md` contract is\n  a violation.\n\n---\n\n## How agy uses this file\nagy reads this as the domain portion of Axis B, after the base layer. Domain-rule\nviolations are `## Must-fix`. If this repo has no domain-specific concern for a given\nfeature, this layer may legitimately add nothing beyond the base rules.\n\n\nAxis C — Spec reconciliation.\n\nThe spec is the source of truth for what this feature must do. A plan or design\nis derived from it and may legitimately argue for a narrower reading — but it\nmay not do so silently, because every downstream phase then measures the\nimplementation against a spec the design already walked away from.\n\nRead the spec above and reconcile it against the target document **by\nidentifier**, not by impression:\n\n**Design audits — every acceptance criterion.** For each `AC-N.M` in the spec,\nclassify the design as exactly one of:\n\n| Classification | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `implemented-as-written` | the design covers the AC in its spec form |\n| `restated` | the design covers it in a different, usually narrower form |\n| `absent` | the design does not address it at all |\n\n**Plan audits — every functional requirement.** Same three classifications at\n`FR-N` granularity. A plan is a strategy document and is not expected to restate\neach AC; it *is* expected to address every FR or explicitly defer it.\n\nReporting rules:\n\n- For every `restated` item, **quote both forms** — the spec's wording and the\n  design's — so the divergence is auditable rather than asserted. Then say which\n  is narrower and in what respect.\n- `restated` and `absent` are **must-fix**. This is not a judgement that the\n  design is wrong: a narrowing may be well-argued and may be the right call. It\n  is a judgement that the divergence must be explicit and must land in the spec\n  before the gate clears, so the operator decides deliberately rather than\n  discovering it at verification.\n- An AC whose narrowing the spec **already reflects** is\n  `implemented-as-written`, not `restated`. That is the loop closing correctly.\n- Do not infer coverage from a section heading or a passing reference in a risk\n  or rationale table. An AC is covered when the document says what will satisfy\n  it.\n\nReport Axis C as a table in your `## Summary`, then raise each `restated` or\n`absent` item as its own `## Must-fix` bullet.\n\nEffort contract (binding on you either way; MEASURED only on some dispatches):\n\n- **Sweep the whole document. Do not stop at the first blocking finding.** Measured\n  over 83 design / 74 plan / 34 impl-plan cycles on one feature: the median pass\n  returned **one** must-fix against a prompt asking for all of them, which turns every\n  defect into its own round trip and is most of why those numbers are that large.\n- **Read the tree before asserting what is in it.** The documents are inlined above,\n  which is exactly why a pass can look thorough having opened nothing. Their claims are\n  *about* the tree — a `path:symbol`, a signature, a test name, a count against its own\n  list — and those are the claims that turn out to be wrong.\n- **When you re-derive a published figure, four things must match or your reading answers a\n  different question. Each has been got wrong here by an auditor, not only by the orchestrator:**\n  - *Commit.* Presence at a sha is not provenance. \"X was introduced in revision N\" needs the\n    needle PRESENT at N's landing commit **and ABSENT at N-1's** — two readings return N and\n    N+1 alike. `git rev-list <base>..<sha> -- <path>` first: if that commit does not touch the\n    file, it is not the figure's provenance.\n  - *Corpus.* **Run the command the document DEFINES, not one that reproduces the document's\n    number.** A head-scoped series verified whole-blob agreed for four shas and then did not;\n    the gating auditor re-ran it, reported it reproduced, and the scope was wrong the whole\n    time. Agreement with a published value is evidence about arithmetic, never about scope.\n  - *Grammar.* `grep -c` answers \"how many lines contain this string\", never \"how many\n    instances of this concept exist\". Print the matching lines and classify them before you\n    publish a count — a Python kwarg and a diagnostic field spell identically. Over markdown,\n    collapse newlines and admit the inline-code delimiters (`` ` ``): a needle that cannot\n    match across a backtick reported 2 sites where there are 4.\n  - *Shell.* Take each control in its own invocation; a `cd` in a chained command is not\n    scoped to that command.\n- **If a document publishes a screen with `# expect 0`, RUN IT** rather than reporting that it\n  is present. Three sheet entries certified \"no scoped census moved\" over a commit at which the\n  document's own trip-wire read 8.\n- **An ABSENCE claim is a figure.** \"No corpus instance …\", \"the corpus has none\", \"nothing in\n  this repository\" — hold each to the same command and sha as a count; both that shipped here\n  were false. A zero bounded only by some *unrelated* constraint is a defect: the document says\n  why it is zero and whether that reason is load-bearing.\n- **A stated PROPERTY of a screen is a claim about code — execute it.** \"No working-file edit can\n  move this\" was false of the shipped line. Do the thing it says cannot move it, and re-run. A\n  control over an ALTERNATION tests the alternation, never its branches: a healthy sibling covers\n  a sick one (one boundary repair was half-applied three rounds running and passed every time).\n- **Your report is held to this too.** Every count carries its UNIT (`grep -n` lines are not\n  occurrences); a population you prescribe is derived once and cited twice; and your PRESCRIPTION\n  is a claim about the tree — one prescribed naming an enclosing function for a line that has\n  none. Check it or mark it unchecked, and give every alternative repair rather than the best-\n  reading one.\n- **A pass at or below two successful tool calls cannot certify a clean.** Writing the\n  report and its marker costs those two by itself, so at that floor nothing was read.\n  When your transcript is captured — an `audit-cycle` dispatch — the combiner enforces\n  this and scores the cycle `UNVERIFIED` rather than clean. When it is not captured,\n  **nothing measures you and the rule binds you anyway**: say what you read. Findings\n  count at any effort; this bounds what a *clean* is worth, never what a finding is.\n- **State your evidence** on its own line in `## Summary`: `Evidence: <N> files opened,\n  <M> greps run.`\n\nOutput framing (mandatory — the orchestrator extracts on these markers):\n\nEmit your report bracketed by these two lines, each alone on its line, with\nnothing before the first or after the second:\n\n    AUDIT-agy-coder-fallback-plan-v1-BEGIN\n    ...your report...\n    AUDIT-agy-coder-fallback-plan-v1-END\n\nYour report is read by scraping this terminal, and the previous cycle's report\nis usually still visible above the prompt. The markers are how the orchestrator\ntells your new report from that old one — omit them and the audit cannot be\nscored. Emit them even if you have nothing to report.\n\nOutput schema (exact, no other top-level sections):\n\n## Summary\n<2-3 sentences>\n\n## Must-fix\n- <issue> — <why it breaks invariant or creates a hard gap>\n  *(empty section: write the single word `None` on its own line — NOT a `- None` bullet; a leading `- ` would be counted as a blocking item)*\n\n## Should-fix\n- <issue> — <why it matters but isn't a hard gate>\n  *(empty section: write the single word `None` on its own line — NOT a `- None` bullet; a leading `- ` would be counted as a blocking item)*\n\n## Nit\n- <style/clarity issue>\n  *(empty section: write the single word `None` on its own line — NOT a `- None` bullet; a leading `- ` would be counted as a blocking item)*\n\nClass rule (Must-fix and Should-fix): **every bullet carries a `class:` continuation line**, `build` or\n`measurement`, under the bullet and never as a `- ` bullet of its own (a leading `- ` is a second\nfinding). The test is one question: **would the code or tests a 5d/5e implementer writes differ if this finding were fixed?** Yes → `class: build` (an interface, a contract, a\npredicate, a fixture, a test's assertion or RED/GREEN count, a false premise about the platform). No →\n`class: measurement` (a published number, stamp, ledger row, sha series, pointer or self-count that is\nstale or mis-derived; the tree is unchanged by fixing it). An untagged bullet is scored as `build`, so\nomitting the line never softens a finding — it only costs the orchestrator the triage. Put the `class:` line directly under its bullet (before or after `quote:`); do not indent a sub-bullet between a finding and its `class:` line, which leaves the finding untagged.\n\n    - <issue> — <why>\n      class: build\n      quote: <path/to/file.md> › `<span copied verbatim from that file>`\n\nQuoting rule (Must-fix and Should-fix): **if a finding asserts what a document says, prove it.**\nAdd an indented `quote:` line under that bullet naming the file and carrying the span copied\nverbatim out of it:\n\n    - <issue> — <why it breaks the invariant>\n      quote: <path/to/file.md> › `<span copied verbatim from that file>`\n\nThe `quote:` line is a continuation of its bullet, never a new `- ` bullet — a leading `- ` would\nbe scored as a second finding. Use one `quote:` line per document the finding names.\n\nThis exists because backticks alone are ambiguous. Reviewers correctly backtick three other kinds\nof span — code they are **proposing** as the fix, test inputs they **constructed**, and commands\nthey **ran** — and those legitimately do not occur in the document. Measured over 37 must-fixes:\n43% of *real* findings carry a backticked span absent from the document, so absence alone proves\nnothing and no automatic check can be built on it. `quote:` is the marker that separates a claim\nabout the document from everything else you are entitled to write in backticks.\n\nA finding that asserts document content with no `quote:` line is not rejected automatically, but it\nis the first thing the orchestrator re-derives from source — and a `quote:` span that does not occur\nin the file it names is a finding withdrawn, not a finding to argue.\n","total_lines":2898}},"locations":[]}
