{"type":"tool_call_update","toolCallId":"call-51c4053e-280a-4ece-8574-c484f99540ad-23","status":"completed","content":[{"type":"content","content":{"type":"text","text":"555→  verification and 6a-prime. Phase 3/4/5b audits review documents, not code, and are not legs.\n  The rule covers every implementer, Codex included: a Codex reviewer on Codex-written code (for\n  example the 5e anti-gaming verifier dispatched to Codex) is flagged exactly as agy on agy is\n  (operator decision D-9). The flag is a warning, never a block.\n- **Attribution: the implementer is the author of the reviewed code.** An entry's `implementer` is\n560→  the agent whose successful implementation dispatch produced the code the leg reviews, as\n  recorded for that dispatch in FR-1's `implementation_authors` (D-15). It is never read from `implementer_switch.to` or from the `implementer` field,\n  which record routing, not authorship. `implementer_switch` is historical: Codex out → agy\n  dispatched → switch stored; Codex back → Codex dispatched with `switch=none`, and the stored\n  `.to` still says `agy`. Attributing from it would mark a Codex reviewer of Codex-written code as\n  independent. When the reviewed code came from more than one agent's dispatch, `independent` is\n  `false` if the reviewer is any of them, and `implementer` names that agent.\n  - **Which dispatches are implementation dispatches (E4, v1.8).** A module's authors are the\n    agents of its accepted 5e GREEN dispatch and of every later fix dispatch for that module the\n    orchestrator accepted. A 5d RED dispatch is never an implementation dispatch (it writes tests,\n570→    not the reviewed code), and a dispatch that halted, timed out or was rejected authored nothing.\n    The 5d/5e assignment is SKILL.md §\"Phase 5 (Implementation) sub-steps\"'s (`5d` RED, `5e` GREEN); if that numbering moves,\n    the rule follows the GREEN and fix dispatches, not the step number.\n  - **The reviewer is none of the authors (S5, v1.8: adopted from design v1.1).** `independent` is\n    `true` and `implementer` names the author of the module's latest accepted implementation\n    dispatch. Reason: the schema holds one agent, the choice changes no `independent` value, and\n    \"latest\" is decidable from `implementation_authors`' array order (D-15).\n  - **The author set is complete, never a projection (E2, v1.8; source fixed by D-15, v1.9).**\n    The orchestrator reads a module's author set from FR-1's `implementation_authors`: the\n    distinct `agent` values of the entries whose `module` equals it, and \"latest accepted\n580→    implementation dispatch\" is that module's last entry in array order. It reads the field on\n    every leg, with or without its dispatch context, so a resumed 6a-prime leg recovers the same\n    set a 5e leg used. It never rebuilds the set from earlier `review_independence` entries: a\n    module written by agy and then fixed by Codex, reviewed at 5e by grok and verified by Codex,\n    stores `implementer=codex` in both 5e entries, and an agy 6a-prime reviewer would then read as"}}],"rawOutput":{"type":"ReadFile","FileContent":{"content":"555→  verification and 6a-prime. Phase 3/4/5b audits review documents, not code, and are not legs.\n  The rule covers every implementer, Codex included: a Codex reviewer on Codex-written code (for\n  example the 5e anti-gaming verifier dispatched to Codex) is flagged exactly as agy on agy is\n  (operator decision D-9). The flag is a warning, never a block.\n- **Attribution: the implementer is the author of the reviewed code.** An entry's `implementer` is\n560→  the agent whose successful implementation dispatch produced the code the leg reviews, as\n  recorded for that dispatch in FR-1's `implementation_authors` (D-15). It is never read from `implementer_switch.to` or from the `implementer` field,\n  which record routing, not authorship. `implementer_switch` is historical: Codex out → agy\n  dispatched → switch stored; Codex back → Codex d